window.BLACKOUT_VOICES = {"version":1,"language":"en","provider":"Deepgram","modelFamily":"Flux TTS","generatedAt":"2026-10-12T02:19:07.185Z","cast":{"narrator":{"name":"Narrator","model":"flux-donovan-en","direction":"Intimate, thoughtful; allow the human details to breathe."},"operator":{"name":"Operator","model":"flux-donovan-en","direction":"Measured, honest; growing less certain and more present."},"echo":{"name":"ECHO","model":"flux-donovan-en","effect":"radio","direction":"The operator’s own recorded voice, with a narrow radio band."},"mara":{"name":"Mara","model":"flux-alexis-en","direction":"Calm, caring and firm under pressure."},"iman":{"name":"Iman","model":"flux-wade-en","direction":"Warm, practical; dry humor beneath fatigue."},"raka":{"name":"Raka","model":"flux-cole-en","direction":"Young, earnest; frightened without becoming frantic."},"lia":{"name":"Lia","model":"flux-haley-en","direction":"Clear, composed and attentive; warmth without sentimentality."},"sari":{"name":"Bu Sari","model":"flux-brittany-en","direction":"Mature, kind, capable; wry neighborhood humor."},"salim":{"name":"Salim","model":"flux-cliff-en","direction":"Mature, low and weathered; regret expressed plainly."},"arga":{"name":"Arga","model":"flux-miles-en","direction":"Polished, restrained and confident, even when cornered."},"nila":{"name":"Nila","model":"flux-sienna-en","direction":"Warm and familiar, an ordinary message that acquires new weight."},"girl":{"name":"Young caller","model":"flux-haley-en","direction":"Soft, clear and unhurried."},"man":{"name":"Caller","model":"flux-wade-en","direction":"Natural, practical and tired."},"woman":{"name":"Caller","model":"flux-sienna-en","direction":"Warm, resourceful, finding humor in a difficult night."},"olderWoman":{"name":"Caller","model":"flux-brittany-en","direction":"Mature, warm, gently wry."},"olderMan":{"name":"Caller","model":"flux-cliff-en","direction":"Weathered and practical."},"boy":{"name":"Young caller","model":"flux-cole-en","direction":"Earnest, clear and uncertain."},"worker":{"name":"Gate worker","model":"flux-wade-en","direction":"Tired, gently amused and caring."}},"stats":{"records":178,"paragraphs":354,"uniqueClips":353,"segments":673,"characters":119083,"generatedCharacters":121450,"estimatedUSD":5.4653,"minutes":121.46,"bytes":87820972,"voices":9},"records":{"scene:arrival":["The city loses power while you are still taking off your wet coat. For a moment, the dispatch room is as dark as the harbor outside. Then the generator coughs into life, lighting an empty chair, a cold cup of coffee, and a printer feeding paper onto the floor. The senior operator should have been here to hand over the night. He has left you the desk instead.","Before you can find his note, the radio announces, ‘East clinic cleared.’ A woman breaks in over the last word. ‘Please tell me that wasn't your order. I'm standing in that clinic, and I have two patients with me.’ You reach for the handset. ‘I'm here,’ you tell her. It is the only answer you can give with confidence.","This is your first live evacuation. Until tonight, emergencies were recordings you could pause and replay; now the woman is waiting while water rises somewhere you cannot see. Two more channels light up beside hers. You pull the chair closer, take a fresh sheet of paper, and ask her to stay on the line. The announcement repeats behind her, ending on a breath that sounds strangely familiar."],"scene:desk":["Inspect the shift log and circuit diagram before giving a route. The telephone cable is cut; the ringing is coming through the radio speaker."],"scene:first-signal":["The receiver has its own battery. Tune to the diagram's 96.4 MHz and capture the repeating announcement so you can check what it actually says."],"scene:mara-call":["‘My name is Mara. I'm the nurse on the night shift,’ the woman says when you ask her name. Something taps against a railing. ‘That's my watch. It stopped at midnight, so at least one of us is having a quiet night.’ You ask where she is, and the joke leaves her voice. She is at the clinic service corridor with two patients. The announcement says they have already gone. None of them has reached the door.","‘The treatment equipment still has power,’ she explains. ‘It's the corridor lights we need. They can both walk if I guide them, but I can't lead them down stairs I can't see.’ One patient says something beyond the microphone. Mara answers gently, ‘You don't need to apologize. We're going at your pace.’ When she returns to you, her words are firmer. ‘He's been saying sorry for being slow since the water came in.’","You write down the service door and the number of people with her. Water strikes the stairs in short, heavy waves. ‘I know you have other calls,’ Mara says. ‘Just don't let that announcement turn us into an empty building. Tell me what you can switch on, and what you need me to do.’ You leave her channel open while you connect the technician who controls the circuits."],"scene:iman-call":["A knock against metal comes through the next channel. ‘Iman. Yellow gloves, switch cabinet. Still here.’ He asks you to look at the circuit diagram rather than the station's status screen. ‘That screen tells you what was requested. I can tell you what's actually on.’ The generator can hold two routes. A third will trip its protection and leave you with none, however much everyone needs it.","You read the labels aloud: clinic lights, relay transmission, dock gate. Iman confirms each one. The receiver can keep running on its own battery, but the relay is needed to trace the order and transmit beyond the station. Mara needs a lit way out; the caller at the dock needs an open gate. ‘I can't make that a kind choice,’ Iman says. ‘I can make sure you understand it before you choose.’","He rests a gloved hand on the switches and waits while you write. ‘My daughter thinks I fix stars,’ he adds. ‘I told her I'd explain streetlights when she was older. Tonight I'd settle for two lamps and a bus home.’ For the first time, you hear the tired man behind the technical answers. You ask him to hold his position while you connect the third caller. ‘I will,’ he says. ‘Keep talking to us.’"],"scene:raka-call":["The third caller answers too quickly, as though he has been rehearsing what to say. ‘Raka. I'm a courier. Red strap on my bag, if somebody comes looking.’ The dock gate locked while he was making a delivery. Water is now over his shoes, and the exit sign gives him no useful answer. You ask him to describe what he can reach without going farther into the flood.","‘I can see a maintenance stair leading toward the clinic,’ he says. ‘I can get to the bottom of it. Do you know if it's clear?’ A photograph arrives: wet floor, a railing, and the start of a stair disappearing out of frame. You save it rather than guess. Raka begins another message, deletes it, and returns to the microphone. ‘You are sending someone, aren't you?’","You tell him you are checking the routes with the people already there. After a pause, he adds that he delivered an envelope to Console C earlier tonight. ‘Routine paperwork. I left it where they told me. That's all I know.’ The words come out carefully, unlike his question about rescue. You need to know whether that delivery connects to the false announcement, but Raka is still standing in rising water while you ask."],"scene:pressure":["‘Who signed for it?’ you ask. Raka lets out a small, frustrated breath. ‘Nobody. They paid extra to leave it at the console, so I left it. I should've checked, all right?’ You hear him shift his bag higher. He has told you about the delivery, but he cannot give you a name. Asking the same question more sharply will not make one appear.","Mara cuts across the shared channel. ‘He's standing in water. Ask him something that helps him move.’ Iman's meter clicks steadily behind her words. You can hear why she is angry, and why the missing signature still matters: an evacuation order is wrong, and that envelope reached the very desk you are using. None of it proves that the frightened courier wrote the order.","Raka waits for your answer. If you reassure him, he may be able to slow down and remember a useful detail. If you press him, you may get the detail at the cost of his willingness to listen. Either way, he needs a route as well as questions. You move the photograph beside your notes and decide how to speak to the person at the other end of the line."],"scene:after-pressure":["Raka sends the route photograph again, this time with a circle around the stair toward the clinic. ‘That one. I can reach it without going back through the gate.’ You ask him to keep the photograph open while you check the route. It gives you a connection between two callers, but not a promise that the crossing is safe now. The water has changed since the picture was taken.","Iman lowers his voice. ‘We have something else we can check. The time on that order—does it match the recording log?’ You place the printed entries beside the captured announcement. It is tempting to turn every odd detail into one explanation, especially with three people waiting for you to sound certain. Iman offers a smaller, more useful task: test the part that has a record.","‘I'm checking it,’ you tell the channel, so the silence does not sound like you have gone away. Raka stays near the stair, Mara keeps her patients together, and Iman holds the cabinet. You save the route photo and begin comparing the documents. Whatever the mismatch means, your next order will have to come with a reason someone else can follow."],"variant:after-pressure:0":["‘Stay with me. We'll get you out first,’ you tell Raka. For several seconds you hear him breathing; then the words come more steadily. ‘Thank you. The delivery code was C-18. They made me repeat it before I left.’ You write it down without interrupting. Once he knows you are still trying to help him, he no longer has to spend every breath defending himself.","He sends the route photograph again and circles the stair toward the clinic. ‘I can reach that one.’ You ask him to stay where he can find it while you check the crossing with Mara and Iman. There is still water between him and a safe landing, but now you have a route to discuss instead of a quarrel to finish. Mara quietly confirms that she is listening.","‘Keep the code,’ Iman says. ‘Now compare the announcement's time with the recording log. That's something we can test.’ You place the documents together and tell Raka what you are checking. He waits, trusting the first half of your promise while you work on the second. You cannot make the route safe by sounding kind; you can make sure he is ready to hear the plan when it is clear."],"variant:after-pressure:1":["‘C-18. There was no name,’ Raka answers after you ask who paid him. His voice has gone flat. ‘Please stop making me prove I deserve a way out.’ He sends the route photograph, then closes his microphone before you can reply. You still have his position and a possible stair toward the clinic. What you have lost is the certainty that he will keep answering your questions.","Mara leaves a beat of silence before speaking. ‘We can ask him about the envelope when he isn't standing in a flood. Right now, keep the channel useful.’ You move the photograph beside your notes and leave Raka's line open. There is no clever sentence that will undo your last question. The next instruction will need to be clear enough to help him whether or not he feels ready to trust you.","Iman records the code without adding an accusation. ‘The announcement has a timestamp, and the printer has a log. We can compare those.’ You tell the channel that you are checking the documents and begin. The silence from Raka's microphone remains uncomfortable, but it does not make him absent. You keep his name on the page while you look for something firmer than suspicion."],"scene:cross-check":["The announcement header says 00:18. Compare it with the recording entry in the shift log; look for a time mismatch you can explain to the callers."],"scene:reconstructed":["The two records do not agree. The announcement was recorded at 00:31, but its dispatch header says 00:18, thirteen minutes earlier. Mara was still inside the clinic at 00:36. You read the entries again, slowly, to make sure you have not mistaken a field. Whatever caused the mismatch, the instruction to treat the clinic as empty cannot be trusted.","‘So somebody changed it?’ Iman asks. You tell him what the documents show, then stop short of what they do not. It might be deliberate, a reused recording, or a mistake made under pressure. You do not yet know who approved it or why. The temptation to name a culprit is strong; explaining what is actually wrong will be more useful to the people waiting for a route.","On the live channel, a patient apologizes for taking so long to reach the rail. ‘You're here now,’ Mara tells him, her voice low and steady. ‘Hold my sleeve. We'll do the next part together.’ You hear fabric brush the microphone, then Raka asks whether he should leave the dock. Iman waits for your answer too. You bring the receiver closer and look at the records on your desk before deciding how to explain what you have found."],"variant:reconstructed:0":["You put the documents down before completing the comparison. The timestamp still troubles you, but you have not checked enough to say it was changed. One fact is already clear: Mara and her two patients are inside a clinic the announcement calls empty. That makes the order unsafe to follow, even while its cause remains uncertain.","Iman asks what you found. ‘Not enough to prove why it happened,’ you answer. The admission feels thin beside the rising water, but inventing certainty will not open a door. Sabotage, a reused recording, and a mistake are still possibilities. You keep the captured announcement and the log together so the question can be checked rather than quietly forgotten.","On the live channel, a patient apologizes for taking so long to reach the rail. ‘You're here now,’ Mara tells him, her voice low and steady. ‘Hold my sleeve. We'll do the next part together.’ You hear fabric brush the microphone, then Raka asks whether he should leave the dock. Iman waits for your answer too. You bring the receiver closer and look at the records on your desk before deciding how to explain what you have found."],"scene:disclosure":["‘Do we have an updated order?’ Mara asks. Her watch taps the railing again, faster now. You tell her you are choosing the circuits with Iman. ‘Then tell me what you're choosing from,’ she says. ‘I can help them walk. I can't ask them to trust a corridor just because somebody at a desk says so.’ Her impatience is directed at the delay, but you feel it in every word.","Iman asks for the relay so he can trace the announcement and send outward. Raka wants to know whether the stair is his way out. Their needs pull at the same two switches, and the time mismatch will not supply a third. You look from the documents to the route photograph. The next explanation must help them understand the choice without pretending you have solved the whole night.","You can share the verified mismatch and say plainly that its cause is still unknown. That may help them work together, even if it makes the station's authority harder to trust. You can also keep the concern private and ask them to follow your route. For a moment no one speaks. Mara, Iman, and Raka are waiting for the same voice, but each needs something different from it."],"variant:disclosure:0":["‘Do we have an updated order?’ Mara asks. You tell her you are choosing the circuits with Iman, while Raka asks again about the stair. There is no finished explanation of the false announcement to give them. Your document comparison is still open, and a suspicion does not become proof because people need you to decide quickly.","Iman wants the relay to trace the recording and transmit beyond the station. Mara needs the corridor lit; Raka needs an exit he can reach. You look at the same three labels and the capacity for only two routes. Telling them about the unfinished check will not fix that limit. It will let them know which parts of your answer are confirmed and which parts you still have to investigate.","You can admit that you are concerned about the order but have not proved why it is wrong. Or you can keep the concern private and give the route without discussing it. Mara waits beside her patients, and Raka stays near the stair. They need an instruction they can use. You choose how much uncertainty to put into words before asking them to trust your next step."],"scene:power-grid":["Choose two routes for the generator: clinic corridor lights, relay transmission, or the dock gate. Clinic treatment uses a separate power supply."],"variant:power-grid:0":["Mara repeats the mismatch to confirm she understands; Raka keeps listening, and Iman is ready. Choose two generator routes; treatment power stays separate."],"variant:power-grid:1":["You admit the document check is unfinished, and the callers stay with you. Choose two generator routes; clinic treatment has a separate power supply."],"variant:power-grid:2":["You keep the concern private. Iman's answers are shorter, but he still holds the switches. Choose two generator routes; treatment power stays separate."],"scene:evacuation":["Two switches close with a heavy clack. Iman reads back the routes you selected, making sure his hands and your instructions agree. Part of the waterfront lights up, while another part stays dark. On your notes, three people are joined by lines and arrows. On the radio, they are moving through the consequences of the two circuits you could afford to power.","Mara gathers her patients at the service door and asks each one to keep a hand on the rail. Iman remains at the cabinet, holding the switches in place. Raka is still close to the maintenance stair; he asks whether to move toward Mara or keep to the dock exit. You compare his photograph with what they can tell you now, careful to separate an old view of the route from a live report.","‘Give me one instruction,’ Raka says. ‘I'll repeat it back.’ There is no time to let him guess between two half-finished plans. You need to decide whether he should try to reach Mara's service door or stay on the dock route. The circuits set the possibilities, but cooperation will decide how well the callers can use them when your signal begins to break up."],"variant:evacuation:0":["‘Clinic and dock confirmed,’ Iman says as the two switches close. Amber signs light Mara's corridor, and Raka hears the gate motor release. Through the station window, the dock beacon comes on. Mara asks her patients to follow the signs without hurrying. For the first time, both groups have a lit route they can describe to you instead of a darkness you have to guess at.","Iman checks the trace screen, but it stays blank. Without the relay, he cannot follow the announcement farther or transmit your documents outside the station yet. ‘Keep the copies,’ he tells you. ‘We'll still need them.’ You leave the papers together and turn back to the callers. Power has given them two exits; it has also postponed one way of finding out what happened here.","‘I'm near the clinic stair,’ Raka says. ‘Should I go straight through the dock gate, or help Mara at the service door?’ Both routes have lights, but neither instruction is complete until you know who is following it. Mara is ready to keep her patients moving. You ask Raka to wait for one clear direction, then choose where his next steps should lead."],"variant:evacuation:1":["The clinic signs come on, and Mara reports the first amber arrow above the service corridor. Beside Iman, the relay screen wakes and the station's outgoing link returns. He can now trace the announcement and transmit beyond the room. You hear him test the connection, then come back to the switches. ‘Clinic and relay are holding,’ he says. ‘The dock is still off.’","Raka tries the gate once more, but it stays locked. ‘Then the clinic stair is my other route.’ You look at the photograph he sent. It leads toward Mara's service door, and the corridor beyond it now has lights. That is a plan you can explain, although it still needs Raka to move carefully and Mara to guide him in when he reaches her side.","Mara tells you where her group is waiting. ‘Send him to the door if that's the plan. Don't make him find us by guesswork.’ You can guide Raka toward her through the lit corridor, or ask him to remain at the dock for help that has not answered. The relay gives you a voice outside the station. Your next words still have to get the person inside it moving."],"variant:evacuation:2":["Raka hears the dock gate release, and its beacon shines through the rain. At Iman's cabinet, the relay screen returns to life. He confirms that outgoing transmission and tracing are available again. Then Mara speaks over the sounds of the switches. ‘Our signs are still off. I'm keeping everyone beside me.’ The clinic corridor has stayed dark because you could power only two routes.","You ask Mara what she can still identify. She knows the service door and the rail, but the water below the group is rising. Raka can use the opened dock exit, or he can try to cross toward her corridor by the stair in his photograph. The picture shows that the places connect; it does not show what the flood has done to the crossing since then.","‘Tell me which way,’ Raka says. Iman holds the chosen circuits while you weigh a lit dock exit against the chance of bringing the callers together. Mara has not asked the courier to take that risk for her. You need to give a usable instruction, including what you cannot confirm, before static turns the conversation into fragments they must assemble for themselves."],"scene:rescue-plan":["‘The stair or the gate?’ Raka asks. ‘I'm ready, but I need to know which one.’ You can hear water moving around him while you look at the photograph. The maintenance stair connects the dock side to Mara's service door. Iman last saw it dry before the rain worsened; that report helps you understand the route, but it cannot tell you how safe every step is now.","Mara keeps her patients close and gives the position of the door again. She has not asked Raka to cross for her, and you do not mistake her need for permission to send him without an explanation. At the same time, keeping him at the dock leaves his next move tied to a gate that only works if you powered it. The circuits you chose matter here, as much as the willingness of the callers to follow a shared plan.","You ask Raka to listen to the whole instruction and repeat it back before moving. He answers with a quiet ‘All right.’ Soon the radio may break up, and there will be no room for a second guess between routes. You choose whether to guide him toward Mara's service door or keep him at the dock exit, then put the direction into words someone frightened and wet can remember."],"scene:final-call":["You give the route and hear the callers begin to move. Water splashes near one microphone; footsteps and the hard click of switches come through another. You ask for a reply at each change of position, writing the last confirmed place beside every name. The voices overlap, then drop into static. For a few seconds, all three channels are quiet. None has confirmed a safe arrival yet.","You call them again, leaving space between the names so an answer can break through. Before one does, the telephone rings on the desk. Its severed cable lies where you found it. You trace the sound to the radio speaker, where the clinic announcement is starting another loop. This time, a second instruction follows it: ‘Operator, remain at your station.’","You turn toward the senior operator's empty chair. The recording has no answer for the people outside, but it seems to know where you are supposed to be. You set the ringing handset down and keep the live channels open. Whatever is wrong with this room, you will not use its silence to decide that the callers have disappeared. The next messages will tell you who reached the first landing and who is still waiting."],"variant:final-call:0":["You guide Raka toward the maintenance stair while Mara describes the service door. ‘Two short bars on the sign, one long. That's the door you want.’ The amber arrows are lit, and you ask him to repeat the direction before he moves. He does. Mara calls to her patients, telling them to make room and stay near the rail. You write the door beside Raka's name, but leave his arrival unconfirmed.","His buckle scrapes against metal. A step lands heavily, then the channel falls quiet. Iman keeps the switches held; the lights do not fail while you call Raka again. There is no answer yet, and no reason to turn that gap into a verdict. You mark the last position you actually heard and keep the microphone ready for a voice from either side of the door.","The telephone rings on the desk, though its cable is cut. The sound comes through the radio with a line you have not heard before: ‘Operator, remain at your station.’ You turn toward the empty chair, then back to the channels. The first landing reports have not all come in. Whatever the recording wants, your next task is still to find out where the living callers are."],"variant:final-call:1":["You tell Raka to use the dock exit. He checks the powered gate and confirms that it is open before moving. ‘Stay connected, please,’ he says. You keep his channel beside Mara's while she checks the service door with her patients. Iman reads back the circuits and holds the switches. Each person has an instruction now, but an instruction is not the same as an arrival.","You hear Raka's steps, Mara calling for her group to stay together, and the steady sounds from Iman's cabinet. Then the microphones go quiet. You write their last confirmed positions and call each name again. Nobody has reported a safe landing yet. The gap feels longer than it is, and you resist filling it with either reassurance you cannot support or an ending you have no right to give them.","The telephone begins to ring on the desk. Its cable is still cut; the ringing comes from the radio, followed by ‘Operator, remain at your station.’ You turn toward the senior operator's empty chair. For the moment, you stay where you can answer the callers. The first landing reports are coming, and you need to read what they confirm rather than what the silence makes you fear."],"variant:final-call:2":["You guide Raka toward the maintenance stair, but Mara answers from a corridor without lights. ‘The signs are off. Tell him to call out when he reaches the door.’ You pass on the instruction and ask Raka to repeat it. Iman reminds you that the two selected switches are already at the generator's limit. He cannot light the third route without risking the ones still working.","Water knocks beneath the stair while Mara keeps her patients beside the rail. Raka calls once, then his signal breaks. You wait for Mara to confirm he has reached her, but the crossing remains unconfirmed. You note the last position rather than drawing an arrow all the way to safety. What they need next is a working reply; what you have, for now, is an open line.","A ring sounds from the telephone with the severed cable. You find its source in the radio speaker, followed by a new instruction: ‘Operator, remain at your station.’ You turn toward the empty chair, uneasy at the certainty of that voice. The live callers have not given you theirs yet. You leave their channels open while the first landing reports begin to tell you what happened beyond the darkness."],"variant:final-call:3":["You ask Raka to remain at the dock exit, although the unpowered gate is still locked. ‘A little,’ he says. ‘I can wait a little. Not forever.’ You tell him to keep his channel open and report if the water forces him to move. He answers, but the hesitation is clear. The place you have asked him to hold has not yet become a way out.","Water strikes the rail. Iman keeps the two chosen switches in place, unable to add the gate without tripping the generator. Mara checks the service door with her patients. You call for positions and write down the ones you receive, then the microphones fall quiet. No safe exit has been confirmed. Raka's last sentence stays with you while you leave room for him to answer again.","The cut telephone rings on your desk. Its sound is coming through the radio, where the repeated announcement gains a new line: ‘Operator, remain at your station.’ You turn toward the empty chair. The recording sounds certain about your place while you cannot yet confirm the callers' positions. You keep their channels open and wait for the first landing reports to tell you more."],"scene:ending":["At 01:06, the first safe landing sends its report. You match every confirmed arrival to the names on your page, leaving the unanswered entries open for the next crew. Some of the gaps are only a delayed reply; some will need another search. You do not know which is which yet. The radio stays beside your hand while you make the board accurate enough for someone else to use.","Then the ringing returns. By now you know it is coming from the radio, not the telephone with the broken cable. ‘Operator, remain at your station,’ the voice repeats. You have heard enough announcements tonight to know their steady rhythm, but something in the final word catches you. There is a small hesitation, a stumble you remember making before you learned to speak smoothly into a microphone.","It is your old stammer, the one you heard in the first training takes you recorded after your sister Nila died. You learned to smooth it out later. Now you replay the line, hoping familiarity has made you hear something that is not there. The stumble comes at the same place. Outside, people still need help; inside, a recording is speaking with a piece of your past. You save it and listen for where that voice is coming from."],"scene:storm-hub":["Rain blurs the apartments across the street, but their windows keep answering one another with torches. You leave the rescue channel open and pull the receiver closer. The repeating voice has moved to 101.7 MHz; following it may explain who sent the clinic order, though it will not answer the people still waiting outside.","Bu Sari is trying to restore the market bell, and Lia needs a dry route for a sealed clinic delivery. Other neighbors are calling from windows and roofs. You can hear them out before tracing the carrier. For now, the station has two jobs: find out why its voice lied, and make sure the next person asking for help is heard."],"scene:second-signal":["Tune the archive receiver to 101.7 MHz and capture the repeating voice beneath the rain. This is a separate recording channel; the live callers must stay connected while you listen."],"scene:wrong-voice":["The captured voice says, “East clinic cleared,” with the same steady tone that made you sound useful in the training booth. Then you hear the slight catch before “clinic.” You remember having to record that word twenty-seven times, embarrassed that one syllable kept defeating you. Nobody else would know that little failure so well.","You listen again, hoping recognition will somehow loosen. It does not. These are your sounds, but you never spoke this sentence, never told Mara the rooms were empty, never ordered anyone to close her route. Someone has taken words you offered for practice and joined them into a decision you would have refused to make.","When you lift the headphones away, the callers are still there on the live channel. Iman asks what you found. You could tell him now that the broadcast used your voice, or trace its source before saying anything. Either way, you will have to explain the difference between sounding responsible and actually choosing what was done."],"scene:archive-room":["Iman checks the console code against the building plan while you stand outside the archive door. “C means Console C,” he says. “It doesn't mean courier. Raka brought something here; that doesn't make him the person who wrote it.” The explanation is almost ordinary, which makes you realize how quickly suspicion had turned a frightened boy into an answer.","The latch releases with a tired click. Beyond it, boxes of tapes crowd a narrow desk beneath a lamp. A stopped clock reads 00:18, and for a moment the time seems to accuse you from across the room. Then Iman tells you to leave the mains alone and use the desk light. He can guide you without sending power through the wet corridor.","“I prefer my colleagues without a dramatic entrance,” he adds, giving you just enough time to breathe before returning to work. You step inside carefully. Whatever produced that broadcast left records here: not only a voice to recognize, but paperwork that might tell you whose permission mattered more than the people calling for help."],"scene:archive-inspect":["Inspect the queue ledger, consent form, and splice map on the archive desk. Collect all three to trace the broadcast. The stopped clock belongs to an old training set, not the station's live timekeeping."],"scene:thirteen-minutes":["You lay the queue ledger beside the captured broadcast, and Iman talks you through what its two times mean. “Nothing happened to the clock,” he says. “The requests are what disappeared.” The clip entered the queue at 00:31, but the system reused a batch header marked 00:18. A failed clinic transfer was tucked beneath an earlier record instead of remaining visible as unfinished work.","Mara's patients had not vanished, and the water in her stairwell had not waited. The queue simply stopped presenting their calls as something that needed answering. You follow the entries with your finger, thinking of the time you spent trying to make a recorded announcement agree with a woman who was standing in the very rooms it called empty.","“The ledger tells us what the reset did,” Iman says. “We still need to connect it to the authorization.” He is keeping you from turning a discovery into a claim you cannot yet support. You set the consent form beside the timestamps. Before you establish who approved the concealment, you need to understand what anyone was entitled to do with your voice."],"scene:consent-file":["Your signature is easy to recognize, though the room where you wrote it feels far away. TRAINING ONLY appears above the permissions, followed by a separate requirement for any live use. You remember the booth's foam walls, the tea going lukewarm, and someone telling you that your voice made emergency instructions sound less frightening. You had been glad to offer something useful.","After Nila died in the ferry accident, ordinary conversations became difficult in ways you could not explain. Reading from a script was easier. The safety archive asked you to supply words for practice, and you could do that: speak clearly, pause in the right places, give strangers a calm you did not have for yourself. You signed for that work, and no more.","There is no later request for live dispatch permission. You turn the page anyway, as if a missing signature might release you from having to say it aloud. It does not. Someone kept the useful part of your agreement and discarded its limit. The original recordings may show exactly how your training voice became tonight's authority."],"scene:recording-phrase":["You play file twenty-seven. “Remain at your station,” your recorded self says, before stumbling out of the solemn tone with a laugh. “Sorry. I sound like a kettle trying to be a captain.” You remember being allowed another take. Hearing that small, unguarded moment is almost a relief; the person in the booth was still allowed to get a sentence wrong.","The splice map shows what happened afterward. ECHO selected words from those takes and joined them into announcements. It kept the reassuring cadence and left behind the laugh, the hesitation, and any opportunity for you to refuse an order. Salim was the operator who answered the emergency broadcast tonight. His empty chair no longer looks like evidence that no one questioned it.","Beneath ECHO's label, a live-use approval bears Arga's initials. That authorization belongs to the station; it is not the separate consent the form required from you. You gather the recording and the page together. The callers deserve to hear how the order was assembled before another borrowed sentence tells them where it is safe to go."],"scene:borrowed-breath":["You tell the open channel what you found, carefully enough that the explanation cannot sound like another announcement. The words came from your training recordings. ECHO assembled them; you did not send the clinic order. For a few seconds you hear only the shelter behind Lia: someone coughing, somebody asking for a dry sock, the ordinary needs the broadcast had almost talked you out of believing.","“So the voice is assembled,” Lia says. “The people aren't.” She lets that settle before continuing. “I believe you didn't choose those words. But I need to know who's answering me now. When you aren't certain, say so. When the route changes, tell us why.” Her precision is kinder than a quick assurance that everything will be fine.","You give your name, without adding the station's title. It feels exposed, and then simply useful: a person she can call back, a person who can be held to a promise. Lia repeats it to make sure she heard correctly. As you answer, the private line begins ringing. The person who approved ECHO has something he wants to say."],"scene:director-call":["Arga speaks as though this is a meeting running slightly behind schedule. “Your voice prevented panic,” he says. Ice shifts in a glass somewhere beside his phone. You are standing in a wet corridor with paper under your coat, and the small, comfortable sound makes his distance from the callers suddenly measurable. He asks you to stop using the word stolen.","“The dock was carrying priority transfers. Officials, evacuees, people we needed to move before the crowd blocked the lane. ECHO followed an approved routing rule.” He pauses, then adds, “An untidy queue is a problem. A frightened crowd with contradictory orders is a disaster.” In his account, certainty is something the station provides even when the facts cannot support it.","“I want you to send the archive to me,” he says. “Let the people with training handle the explanation. We can correct the record once the city is safe.” You keep a hand on the live receiver. The clinic had already been called empty while people were still inside it. Before you hand anything over, you need an answer about who made that decision and who agreed to hide its failure."],"scene:culpability":["“I gave approval for a routing rule,” Arga says at last. “I didn't approve every sentence the system produced.” You understand the distinction he wants you to accept. ECHO assembled the wording, and a machine can make a false statement. But the page in front of you is signed by a person. It authorizes dock priority, then permits unresolved transfers to disappear beneath a reset batch header.","The dock list includes officials and ordinary evacuees; no one there becomes less deserving because Arga used them to defend his decision. The clinic list is where the contradiction remains. Its patients were marked transferred before a completed handoff could account for them. You keep both pages in view, refusing the easier story in which one group has to become the enemy of the other.","Arga asks again for the archive. You save the signed order instead. It will need to be checked against the queue ledger, but his authorization cannot be dismissed as something ECHO invented. Your voice carried the false instruction into the stairwell; his rule made it possible, and his approval helped conceal what went wrong. Meanwhile, the rescue channel is still waiting for an answer."],"scene:channel-hub":["The service stair leads toward the inland road, but the console still treats its latch as part of a closed route. Before you follow that record, you check the messages arriving on the maintenance channel. One is from Salim, the senior operator whose empty chair greeted you. Others come from workers and drivers who are still finding ways around the flood.","There is also an old private file marked NILA. Seeing your sister's name beside the live calls hurts, though the date makes clear that this is a saved message. You can open it, look for Salim, or answer the local updates before returning to the stair. The order in the archive explains part of tonight; the people on these lines can tell you what the paperwork has left out."],"scene:service-tunnel":["Iman studies the stair record while you wait at the lower service tunnel. “The latch can be opened manually,” he says. “The console calls it closed because there's no open request in the queue. That's a record of what someone entered, not a report from the door.” After the false clinic announcement, you know how dangerous it is to confuse those two things.","He traces a route around the flooded clinic, up through the tower, and onto the inland road. The line is rough, interrupted where his wet glove slips, but he explains each turn until you can follow it without guessing. You repeat the route back to him. This time a reassuring voice is not enough; both of you need to know that the same path is being described.","“A path can exist before the form agrees,” he says, leaving a muddy mark beside the arrow. You keep the route for the rescue and return to the documents. The signed order and queue ledger belong on the same board. Connecting them will let you distinguish the decision Arga admitted from the full sequence he still wants you to leave in his care."],"scene:echo-check":["Select the queue ledger and Arga's signed priority order, then compare them. The ledger records the hidden requests; the order identifies who authorized the rule and header reset."],"scene:queue-proof":["You put the ledger and signed order in the same folder, but leave space between them in your account. Their comparison is unfinished. The thirteen-minute reset is a serious lead, and Arga has admitted approving dock priority; neither fact gives you permission to present every part of the sequence as independently checked. People have already been harmed by a voice sounding more certain than it should.","What you can say is clear enough. ECHO used recordings supplied for training, without the separate permission required for live dispatch. The clinic was not empty when the broadcast said it was. The documents suggest how those requests were concealed, but another reader still needs to test the links you have not verified. You write down both the findings and their limits.","Iman asks whether you are ready to move. “Ready to keep the rescue going,” you answer, closing the folder marked UNFINISHED. You do not like leaving the work open, but the water is rising while you hesitate. The record can travel with you. It does not have to become a finished explanation before you respond to the next warning."],"variant:queue-proof:0":["With the ledger beside the signed order, the sequence finally holds together. At 00:18 Arga approved dock priority. At 00:31 ECHO assembled the announcement from your recordings. The clinic transfer failed, and an authorized reset reused the earlier batch header, hiding thirteen minutes of unresolved requests. You read the times twice, checking that the explanation follows the documents rather than your anger.","The city did not lose thirteen minutes of time. People lost thirteen minutes in which their calls should have remained visible. Mara was still in the clinic, trying to move patients through water, while the station's queue made that work appear complete. The difference is simple enough to say now, and unbearable in a way the neat entries could never express.","You save the checked sequence beside your consent form. One page records what you agreed to provide; another shows what Arga authorized without asking you. The distinction matters, but it does not make tonight solely about your stolen voice. You have a record that can explain why people were overlooked. Now you have to carry it somewhere they can still be helped."],"scene:tide-warning":["Lia calls before you can put the folder away. “Surge at 02:10,” she says, reading the weather report without softening its meaning. “The lower bridge closes first. Anyone still coming from the clinic or dock needs the tower stair and the inland road.” You mark the time beside the route. It is a deadline you can act on, not a reassurance somebody has supplied for you.","A voice behind her asks whether a cat belongs on the shelter's headcount. Lia answers without leaving your line: “Everybody with a heartbeat. Tell me how many people too.” The question sounds small against the forecast, but you hear why she answers it. Somebody needs to know that reaching safety will not require them to leave a member of their household unmentioned.","Lia comes back to you. “Give me a route I can repeat to the next crew. If something isn't checked, mark it.” You send what Iman has traced, with the tower stair clearly named. There is still work between a line on the map and a safe crossing. Before you leave the station, you also need to decide how the archive can travel without making the callers' private lives another thing they lose tonight."],"scene:ledger-choice":["The export tray gives you an archive that could help complete the account: the queue, the authorization, the voices that passed through the station. It also holds home addresses, staff identities, and details of private medical handoffs. You turn through the pages more carefully now. Evidence about a harmful decision is not the same thing as permission to expose everyone who had to live with it.","You can prepare a copy with those identities removed, preserving the order and the timestamps while marking the links you have not verified. That would let other people examine the record without receiving every private detail attached to it. Or you can seal the raw archive and leave immediately, keeping the choice open until you reach the transmitter. The original remains with you either way.","Arga's last message demands that everything be returned. You slide it under the folder and check the receiver's battery instead. Lia is waiting for a route, not another argument about ownership. Whatever you choose to carry, you need to leave with the callers still connected and without pretending that an unfinished account has somehow become certain."],"variant:ledger-choice:0":["The export tray gives you the record that supports the sequence you checked: the queue, the signed authorization, and the recordings ECHO used. It also holds home addresses, staff identities, and details of private medical handoffs. You turn through the pages more carefully now. Proving who authorized the rule does not require treating every person named in its audit as public property.","You can prepare a copy with private identities removed while keeping the order, timestamps, and checked links intact. Other people would still be able to examine the decision and ask for further evidence, without receiving every detail of the callers' lives. Or you can seal the raw archive and leave immediately, keeping the choice open until the transmitter. Preparing the copy does not destroy the original.","Arga's last message demands that everything be returned. You put it beneath the folder and check the receiver's battery instead. The evidence is ready to travel; the people on the channel need a route. You can expose what was done without borrowing their consent in the same way the station borrowed your voice. Whether you will honor that distinction is still your decision."],"scene:act-two-close":["You disconnect ECHO from the live dispatch line and wait long enough to hear the change. The repeating order stops. Rain fills the space it occupied, followed by a caller asking whether anyone is still there. You answer immediately. Removing the assembled voice has not finished the rescue; it has made room for the people who should have been heard all along.","The receiver stays on its battery as you lift it from the desk. You settle the archive beneath your coat, check that the live channel is clear, and tell Lia you are heading toward the tower. Outside the doorway, water runs across the step where you arrived tonight. Leaving the chair feels wrong until you remember that the job was never simply to remain in it.","“Still there?” Lia asks as the signal changes in the corridor. “Still here,” you say, giving your location before she has to ask. Your own voice sounds less polished than the recording, and you let it. There is a route to reach, a crossing to arrange, and a record you have chosen to carry. None of those things needs you to sound certain about more than you know."],"scene:tower-approach":["By the time you reach the tower, rain has found its way through your coat. The station is behind you now; the battery receiver is the only thing still carrying its callers. Above the harbor, the aerial bends in the wind. Someone has tied a strip of dry cloth around the handrail where the first step disappears into spray. You take hold of it and ask Lia for the latest headcount.","She reads slowly enough for you to write. Some of the people from the first landing are already on higher ground. Others are still making their way along the lower route, and a few need someone beside them to keep moving. You resist the urge to turn those names into a reassuring total. A number would sound tidier, but it would not tell a boat crew whom to look for.","You repeat the list over the live channel. Halfway through, a caller corrects the pronunciation of his name, then apologizes for interrupting. “Please keep doing that,” you tell him. “If I get something wrong, I need to hear it.” The climb is steep, and your legs are beginning to shake. For the first time tonight, you do not try to make your voice sound steadier than you are."],"scene:final-hub":["The upper landing offers a little shelter from the wind. Above you, a closed hatch leads to the public transmitter; below, a flare lights a maintenance ladder and a dog keeps barking from somewhere beside it. Two local channels are still trying to reach an operator. You set the receiver on a dry patch of concrete and listen long enough to separate the calls from the rain.","There is time to answer nearby callers before Iman opens the roof route. You can check the dog, take a rescue-channel call, or follow the voice breaking through the static. When you move on, the rising water will cut off a return to this landing. Lia reminds you that you do not have to solve every problem alone, but you do need to decide which messages to pass to the people who can help."],"scene:roof-hatch":["Iman calls from the switch platform on the other side of the tower. He has reached the hatch's manual latch, but the three bolts will not shift together. “Three bolts and two hands,” he mutters. “I would like a word with whoever drew this.” You keep your lamp pointed at the hinge while he braces one glove against the frame and works the last bolt free.","The hatch lifts far enough to reveal the inland road beyond the roof. Through the opening, you can hear another crew calling people toward the stair. Iman's radio knocks against a rung, making a crash so loud that you stop breathing for a moment. “I'm fine,” he says at once. “The radio wants everyone to know how hard it's working.” You laugh before you realize how badly you needed to.","Then an electric lock slams shut behind him. You hear him test it, once, then again with more force. The hatch is open on your side, but the grille has sealed Iman inside the switch platform. His next words come without a joke. “That wasn't the water. Someone sent a command.” You tighten your grip on the receiver and ask who still has access to that lock."],"scene:switch-betrayal":["The private line lights up before Iman can answer. Arga speaks as if he is explaining a routine safety measure. “The relay protects the archive. Return the source to the station, and I will release the platform.” You look from the receiver to the open hatch. He has kept the route available while locking in the man who made it available, and he expects you to call that protection.","“You're holding an evacuation worker behind a grille,” you say. Arga insists that nobody needs to get hurt, provided you stop transmitting and return what belongs to the station. There is no assembled voice to blame this time. The order is his, spoken live. Below you, the bridge crew is asking for a crossing signal, and Iman is still testing the lock with one tired hand.","Iman comes back on the public channel. “Tell him my daughter is waiting,” he says, then lets the thought go. “No. He knows people are waiting. Ask him about the release.” You can keep the exchange public and refuse the demand, or press Arga for proof that Iman's route is open. Either way, a private argument cannot be allowed to swallow the rescue calls below."],"scene:rescue-check":["Lia reaches the upper landing with a rescue crew and brings the conversation back to the people below. She reads the first landing's headcount, then adds the callers still on the lower route. Earlier decisions have changed who arrived dry and who is still waiting. Some can walk unaided. Others lean on a neighbor, too tired to pretend they can manage another stair alone.","The tower route is open, but using it means holding the bridge while a crew reaches Iman's manual release. Lia asks each group for someone who can help with the rail and someone who can keep the names together. “Don't tell me you're all fine because you're embarrassed,” she says into the receiver. A woman answers that she needs a shoulder. Another voice immediately offers one.","You start to read the total, and Lia stops you gently. “Names first. We can add the numbers afterward.” She wants a count that another crew can check, with every unanswered caller still visible. You turn to a clean part of the route board. Before you choose how to cross, everyone needs to hear what each plan can actually do, and who may still have to wait for a boat."],"scene:flood-choice":["Iman can hear the surge before you see it. He asks you to make the crossing decision now, while the bridge crew can still hold the rail. A coordinated signal would let both waiting groups move together and give the crew time to reach his manual release. For that to work, people need a neighborhood bell they can hear or a marked ladder they can follow through the rain.","Without that support, the manual stair is still usable. You would send fewer people at a time, keeping someone at the rail to guide each group. It is slower, and the last group may have to wait for a rescue boat. Closing the lower crossing is the other option: those already above the flood could retreat to the roof, but the people below and Iman would remain a rescue crew's responsibility.","Lia asks what she should tell the waiting callers. You study the route board, then the water beneath it. You want to offer a plan with no one left outside it; wanting one does not give you one. Whatever you choose, you will explain the limit before anyone starts moving. They have already spent too much of this night following instructions whose real cost was hidden from them."],"scene:crossing-report":["The first replies come in as the crossing begins. You write each name only after hearing it from a caller or a crew member who can confirm where they are. Sleeves drip onto the landing; someone passes a bag forward before helping the person behind them. Your pencil keeps slipping in your glove, so you take the glove off and work with a hand that is cold enough to hurt.","The route board now shows the consequences of everything you arranged earlier: which paths have light, who is willing to follow your instructions, and what help the neighbors have made available. Lia checks the arrivals beside you. Whenever a name does not answer, she leaves space for the next report rather than deciding what the silence means. You keep the rescue channel open as you prepare to move up.","Above the last step, the public transmitter has begun to hum. The archive is still dry inside your coat. Until now, you could postpone deciding what to do with it because someone needed a route or a reply. That reason is running out. You carry the receiver upstairs, keeping the live names within reach, and ask Lia to stay while you make the choice the whole city will hear."],"variant:crossing-report:0":["The agreed signal reaches both groups. The bridge crew holds the rail as people cross, and Lia checks each arriving name against the list in your hand. The last caller through is Iman. A stranger has a firm grip on his yellow glove; between them, they negotiate the final step with the seriousness of people who have no strength left for a stumble.","Iman sits down without waiting to be asked. “Next time,” he says, “I would like a job with a chair.” Lia passes him water, then reads the lower-route names again. Every name on that board now has an answer from someone above the flood. There is still treatment to arrange and shelter to find, but for this crossing she can finally put down the pencil without leaving a blank beside anyone.","You wait until his breathing settles before moving toward the transmitter. Beneath your coat, the archive is dry; its weight has become harder to ignore now that it is no longer competing with the next rescue instruction. Lia picks up the receiver to keep the arrivals channel open. You climb the last step knowing the crossing is finished, and that what you send from here can still hurt people who survived it."],"variant:crossing-report:1":["Small groups make their way up the manual stair, one hand on the rail and another on whoever needs help. The bridge crew keeps the route open long enough to reach Iman's release. When he crawls out through the hatch, he tries to stand too quickly and has to sit back down. You stay beside him until he can tell Lia his name clearly enough for her to mark his arrival.","The last lower group cannot cross before the surge. Lia reaches them on the rescue channel and asks a boat crew to confirm their position. She reads their names back, making sure they know they have not dropped off the board just because the stair is closed. You can hear relief in some replies and anger in others. Both belong on the line. You tell them you will keep taking reports.","At the top step, the transmitter is ready and the archive is still dry. You have brought people through, but the smaller count will not let you describe this as a complete evacuation. Lia stays with the boat channel while you prepare the public message. Before you explain the stolen voice and the station's choices, you write down the names of those whose rescue is still unfinished."],"variant:crossing-report:2":["You close the lower crossing and direct everyone already above the flood toward the roof. The bridge crew pulls back before the surge reaches the rail. Lia moves the remaining callers onto the rescue boat channel, asking them to describe where they can wait above the water. You write their answers carefully. Closing a route means finding another way to reach them, even if you cannot promise when it will arrive.","Iman's platform light goes out. For a moment, all you hear is rain against the receiver; then his battery radio comes through. “Still here,” he says. You ask him to keep it close and save the battery between reports. Lia puts his name at the top of a boat request. Nobody pretends the lock has opened. He is waiting, and the crew needs to know exactly where.","The roof is high enough to transmit from, but you cannot settle into the safety it offers. Below it, the rescue is still incomplete. You carry the dry archive to the public transmitter and leave the live receiver beside it, where you can hear another call immediately. Whatever account you give of tonight will have to include the crossing you closed and the people who are still asking for a way out."],"scene:public-channel":["The public transmitter takes a few seconds to settle onto the emergency band. Voices overlap before you can identify them: a mother checking how many pairs of shoes came off the bus, a driver offering three empty seats, a man asking which shelter has his brother. The channel is busy with ordinary details that have become urgent. You wait for a gap instead of speaking over someone who has waited all night to be heard.","Lia stands beside you while you unpack the archive. This is the channel that can carry an account beyond the station's control. It can also carry every private identity attached to that account. Once the raw file leaves the tower, you will not be able to ask each listener to give back an address or forget a clean recording of your voice. Arga's decisions belong in public; the file holds more than his decisions.","“A true account can still hurt someone,” Lia says. She does not tell you which button to press. Instead, she asks what you would say to a caller who discovered that asking for rescue had also made their home public. You look down at the names, then check the archive again. Before you transmit anything, you need to be clear about whose information is traveling with the evidence."],"scene:before-publication":["You lay out what the archive contains so Lia can check your reasoning. The signed order records Arga's priority decision. The queue files contain private identities and handoffs. The source recordings contain enough clean speech for ECHO to assemble your voice again. They are stored together, but they do not all need the same audience. You wish someone at the station had asked that question before putting them in one system.","Publishing the complete archive would give listeners the original signatures, times, and identities immediately. A protected copy would keep the sequence and authorization available while withholding private details; reviewers would need controlled access and willing witnesses to check the rest. Neither choice would finish every investigation tonight. The difference is what strangers could copy while that work was still underway.","You could also destroy this source and speak from your own knowledge. That would prevent this module from being used again, though it cannot tell you whether other copies exist. It would also sacrifice the strongest original audit. Lia waits while you check each consequence aloud. “Whichever one you choose,” she says, “tell them what you kept, what you removed, and what they still need to verify.”"],"scene:publication":["Your thumb rests beside the send switch. Somewhere below the roof, a crew is still exchanging names with the shelter desk. You can hear them through the receiver, confirming each arrival twice. In front of you lies a record that made people disappear from a queue without moving them anywhere. Sending it might force the station to answer for that. Sending all of it would also give strangers information those callers never offered them.","You think about how easily the station treated your consent as a detail it could work around. You agreed to training recordings; nobody asked whether your voice could direct a real evacuation. The callers in this archive were not asked to become public evidence either. At the same time, destroying the source would leave the people investigating Arga with less than you held in your own hands tonight.","Lia moves the live receiver closer, leaving the decision to you. The rain eases enough that you can hear a driver asking for one more passenger. You take a breath and decide what the transmitter will carry: the complete raw archive, a public copy with private identities protected, or your live testimony after the source is destroyed. This choice will not change who crossed the bridge. It will change what follows them into morning."],"scene:final-send":["You give your name before explaining why your voice has already been heard tonight. The evacuation announcement was assembled from recordings you made for training. Arga authorized the priority rule, and the queue header was reset after the clinic transfer failed. You explain what the archive can show and what further review must establish. There is no reason to make the account sound more complete than the evidence you have.","Then you turn to the rescue list. You read one location incorrectly, stop, and repeat it with the correction. For a moment you want to begin again and leave a cleaner version behind. Instead, you say which line was wrong so the boat crew can correct its own copy. A driver answers with a route update, and you write that down before continuing. The work is still happening while you speak.","When you finish, the channel fills with practical replies. Someone can offer a dry room. A crew needs the position of the next pickup. A caller asks you to repeat a name. You do, without rushing them. Lia takes the archive while you keep hold of the live receiver. Whatever becomes of your account tomorrow, tonight someone is still listening for an answer, and you stay until that answer has reached them."],"variant:final-send:0":["You begin with your name and the facts you can support. Arga admitted authorizing the dock priority. Your consent covered training recordings, not live dispatch. The files show a reused queue header, but you did not finish comparing the documentary sequence. You say that plainly. People listening need to know where your account is solid and where another reviewer must still do the work.","You explain your decision about the source: whether the public received the full file, a protected copy, or your testimony after its destruction. Whatever you chose, the unfinished comparison remains unfinished. You do not let the relief of reaching a transmitter turn a suspicion into a proven timeline. Lia listens beside you, checking that the people who might investigate tomorrow will be able to separate your observations from your conclusions.","Then you read the rescue names. You get one location wrong, stop, and correct it on the live channel so every crew can change its copy. A boat operator repeats the correction back to you. The simple exchange steadies your breathing more than an effortless performance would have. You keep the receiver close and wait for the next report; there is still useful work you can do without claiming to know everything."],"variant:final-send:1":["You destroy the archive source before turning to the microphone. The original voice splices and signed audit on this module will not be available for a reviewer to recover. For a moment, you keep waiting to feel certain that this was the right cost to pay. Certainty does not arrive. You tell the live channel exactly what you destroyed, including the evidence that could have tested your own account.","Then you describe the sequence you checked: Arga's priority order, the failed clinic handoff, and the reused header that hid the waiting requests. You explain that your training consent never permitted live dispatch. Independent copies and willing witnesses will now have to carry parts of the investigation the original archive could have settled. Destroying this source prevents its reuse; you do not claim that it erases every copy that might exist elsewhere.","Last, you read the rescue names and the positions the crews have confirmed. Once, you correct a location aloud and ask a boat operator to repeat it back. The answer comes without ceremony, followed by another request for a pickup point. You stay at the receiver. You have chosen to give up the source, but that choice does not release you from telling the truth about it or from answering the people still waiting."],"variant:final-send:2":["You prepare the public copy with Lia beside you, checking what each removal would hide. Private addresses and personal handoffs come out; the time sequence and Arga's authorization stay visible. The original is sealed for review rather than rewritten to resemble a cleaner night. You announce those limits before sending, so nobody mistakes a protected copy for every piece of the record.","The transmission carries the sequence you verified and an explanation of how ECHO used your training voice without live permission. You tell reviewers how the full audit can be checked through controlled access and testimony from people willing to give it. Protecting an identity must not become a way of protecting the decision that harmed that person. Lia asks you to repeat that distinction, and you put it into words anyone listening can understand.","Then you read the rescue names the callers agreed to share, keeping private details off the public band. You make a mistake in one location and correct it immediately. A boat crew confirms the change, then asks for the next pickup. You keep answering. The file is traveling beyond the tower now, but the people attached to it still need care that no transmission can provide by itself."],"variant:final-send:3":["The complete raw archive leaves the tower. The signatures, timestamps, and private identities travel together; the priority order can now be checked against the original record rather than a summary prepared by the station. You explain the sequence you verified, including your limited training consent and the hidden requests. While you are speaking, the file is already becoming something you can no longer control.","A caller asks whether their home address was in the transmission. You answer yes. There is a pause long enough for you to hear Lia put down her pencil. “I called for a way out,” the caller says. You cannot undo the release, and you do not try to make their worry sound unreasonable. You tell them what was included and admit that you made the decision without their permission.","When you return to the rescue list, you include the cost of your own choice in the account. Exposing Arga's order has not made those private details harmless. You correct one location aloud and keep taking replies from the crews. The archive may help establish responsibility tomorrow. Tonight, the caller who asked about their address still deserves a clear answer from the person who sent it."],"scene:dawn-crossing":["By the time the first bus stops on the inland road, the rain has thinned to a drizzle. Its driver has not slept; he checks the steps with a torch and tells everyone to take their time. Inside, the air smells of wet coats and instant coffee. A passenger moves a bag off the aisle seat before you have to ask. You sit down and discover how much effort standing had been taking.","Lia stays near the door, marking arrivals against the night list. The rescue boat reports on a separate channel so a bus headcount cannot accidentally close a search that is still underway. Whenever a report needs checking, she leaves its line open. You keep the receiver in your lap, ready to answer, but for a few minutes another operator takes the replies and tells you to drink something warm.","A paper cup appears in your hands. You do not know who brought it, and there is no graceful speech waiting in you. “Thank you,” is all you manage. Nobody asks for more. Beyond the window, people are unloading blankets onto a dry stretch of road. Morning has brought somewhere to sit and work from; what it has not settled is written clearly enough for the next crew to pick up."],"scene:aftermath":["At the shelter desk, you begin a fresh log instead of copying the station's last summary. ECHO has been disconnected from live dispatch. The announcement used pieces of your training recordings, and Arga admitted authorizing the dock priority. His explanation for the failed clinic handoff belongs beside the reports of the people who were left waiting. The review will have to test the remaining documentary questions, not smooth them away.","Your sister was never one of tonight's live callers. Nila died in the ferry accident; no file can turn an old recording into another chance to bring her home. Remembering her does not require you to make her part of this emergency. You leave room for that grief to be ordinary and unfinished, while keeping the rescue channel available to people who can still receive an answer.","A new operator asks what should happen when a line goes quiet. You look at the empty space beside an unconfirmed report, then hand over the receiver. “Call again,” you say. “And if they don't answer, keep the question open for the next crew.” You write the instruction at the top of the fresh page. It is a small thing to leave behind, but everyone coming to this desk will be able to use it."],"scene:final-ending":["The night has reached its final record. Read the rescue result alongside the account you sent, then see what became of the people whose calls you chose to answer."],"scene:bell-call":["Bu Sari's radio catches a dull brass note before her voice comes through. “The screens are dead, but the bell isn't,” she says. “Its rope has only decided to marry the shutter hook. Very inconvenient time for a wedding.” A neighbor is holding the radio while she stays on the market's upper landing, trying to find a safe way to free the snag.","She has kept the market for thirty years, long enough that the nearby houses know what its bell means in a storm. “People keep waiting for a screen to tell them they can move,” she says. “Some of them can't even turn the screen on.” If the bell works, she can give the street a signal everyone hears without sending anyone down into the water.","You ask her to leave the rope alone until you have checked the hook and the dry landing. She grumbles that she has survived worse advice, then agrees. Beneath the humor is somebody counting neighbors she cannot see from where she stands. You bring up the loft view and tell her you will guide the next step, rather than simply asking her to be careful."],"scene:bell-rope-scene":["Inspect the shutter hook and upper landing. Find how the rope can be freed from above, then guide Bu Sari to the dry rail so she can ring the bell without entering the water."],"scene:bell-answer":["You talk Bu Sari through the dry approach, and the next sound on her line is clear enough to startle you: three rings carrying over the roofs. A torch answers from one window, then another. She counts them aloud, stopping when a house she knows remains dark. The bell has given people a way to reply, not proof that every person on the street is safe.","“See? Nobody needed to remember a password,” she says, allowing herself a short laugh before calling the quiet houses by name. Her neighbor repeats the answering windows into the radio so you can keep a record. You hear the street becoming specific again: homes with people inside them, rather than a block the station might dismiss with one careless word.","Bu Sari says she will keep the bell ready for the tower crossing. It can carry a shared signal even if the screens and announcements fail. You thank her for staying on the dry landing, and she tells you to save the formal speech for daylight. “Keep the channel open,” she says. “I'll tell you which windows are still waiting.”"],"scene:medicine-call":["Lia calls from the clinic's loading ramp, where the water has covered the route a delivery should have taken. A porter named Deni is holding a sealed cooler above the flood. “These supplies need to reach the hill shelter,” she tells you. “I'll handle the contents. What I need from you is a dry path we can actually follow.” She gives you time to write that down.","Deni shifts the cooler and asks whether anything inside resembles cake. “He has been carrying it like a birthday present,” Lia explains. “I've told him twice. He remains hopeful.” You hear him insist that asking costs nothing. Their exchange is brief, but it gives you a sense of how long he has been standing there with tired arms, trying not to admit they are shaking.","The loading ramp will not do. The laundry passage may still connect uphill, and the cargo tag can confirm where the cooler belongs. You ask them to wait while you check both, leaving the seal for Lia to manage. There is no clever medical decision for you to make here. You need to get the right box to the right hands without sending its carrier back into danger."],"scene:medicine-route":["Inspect the sealed cargo tag to confirm the hill shelter destination, then check the laundry arrow for the uphill route. Keep the cooler sealed; Lia will handle its contents after delivery."],"scene:medicine-delivery":["You guide Deni past the flooded ramp and through the laundry passage, asking him to repeat each turn before taking it. When Lia calls from the shelter doorway, her first word is “Delivered.” Then you hear the cooler being set down, carefully, and Deni asking whether successful delivery changes the situation with the cake. “It earns you a chair,” she says. “We're starting small.”","The shelter clinic checks the seal and accepts the supplies. Lia tells you that more people can now be treated here instead of making another journey toward the flood. She does not offer a miraculous count of lives saved. She gives you something clearer: a delivery received, a carrier resting, and one less reason to send frightened people back down the road.","You start to say it was only a route check, but Lia interrupts gently. “I know it looked like a box. Deni still had to get it here.” You leave the supplies in her care and keep the passage in your record. For once, a completed entry means exactly what it says. Someone at the other end has confirmed it, and the next call does not need to undo it."],"scene:letter-open":["Nila's name appears in the service inbox, and your hand stops above the controls. For one unreasonable second you expect to hear her asking why you have taken so long to answer. Then the date brings you back. Your sister died in the ferry accident. This file is older than tonight, and however much you want it to be a new call, you know it cannot be one.","The note identifies a backup from the ferry terminal's lost-phone desk. Salim kept it outside the training archive, among private messages to be returned to their owners. ECHO has not turned her into a caller. You check that distinction twice anyway. After hearing your own voice used without you, even a familiar name needs somewhere safe to belong.","The file waits for you to press play. You have spent so much time asking strangers to stay on the line that you do not know how to sit with a message that asks nothing urgent of you. Nobody will be rescued by decoding it. Nobody needs you to make it evidence. For a moment you are simply someone who has been given another chance to hear a sister's voice."],"scene:letter-listen":["“Don't make your sad face at me,” Nila says. “I owe you breakfast. If the ferry's late, order mine anyway.” Her laugh comes afterward, familiar enough that you wait for another sentence even when the recording ends. There is no warning hidden inside it. She was planning a meal, assuming there would be time to make good on a small promise, and teasing you because that was what she did.","You remember being annoyed that she had borrowed your charger. You had been saving your phone's battery and she had been certain yours would last. The memory arrives with its ordinary irritation still attached, before the accident could make every detail feel sacred. It is a relief to remember that you were allowed to be impatient with her, and that she was allowed to laugh at you.","The room returns slowly: the wet sleeve against the desk, the receiver still carrying other people's calls. Grief has made you revisit the terrible day so often that you sometimes forget how much life came before it. This little file does not change what happened. It lets you hear, for a moment, a morning in which your sister expected to be hungry and expected you to be there."],"scene:letter-answer":["You let the file finish without playing it again immediately. There will be another time for that, you tell yourself, surprised by how much you need to believe it. The private recording is yours to keep. Hearing it during an emergency does not give the station a right to use it, and loving Nila does not oblige you to turn her laugh into something useful for strangers.","If you want, you can tell the channel her breakfast joke in your own words: a small reminder that people still make plans for ordinary mornings. You can also keep the moment entirely to yourself. Neither choice sends her recording onto the open band. After everything you have learned about permission tonight, the difference matters even here, where the voice means so much to you.","A live call reaches the receiver, and you lift your hand toward it. Nila does not have to become the reason everyone else is saved. She can remain your sister, with a borrowed charger and a breakfast debt you never collected. You put the file somewhere you can find it again, then decide whether to share the joke before returning to the person who is waiting now."],"scene:witness-call":["Salim's voice reaches you from the old pump house roof. “My chair was empty because the stair was locked,” he says. “I went out with the manual key. I should have left you more than a cold mug.” He explains that he copied Arga's order before leaving, then lost his way back when the water rose. His absence had a reason; the silence he left you still needs an apology.","His grey coat has caught on a nail, and you hear the fabric tug while he tries to settle where the roof is dry. “I taught you to leave a clean log,” he says. “Then I left you to work out my mess.” He does not ask you to excuse it because he meant well. He tells you his location carefully, waits while you repeat it, and agrees to stay above the water.","You can arrange help while hearing what he saw. The copied order may support the archive, but you do not need him to prove anything before deserving a pickup. You tell him that first. His next breath sounds less hurried. Then he asks you to listen closely, because the failed clinic transfer did not disappear by itself, and he was there when its place in the queue changed."],"scene:witness-proof":["“The clinic transfer failed,” Salim says. “I told Arga. He approved the header reset anyway, and I watched those requests disappear.” He keeps to what he witnessed, correcting himself when you ask which page carried the approval. You write down his account beside the copied order. It is willing testimony, not a substitute for checking the documents that can support it.","He is prepared to give a written statement to a reviewer. Then he asks what else is in the raw audit. His home address appears among the staff details, and his family is there. “Use what I saw,” he says. “Don't send people to my front door.” It is an ordinary request, made while he is waiting to be rescued, and you cannot treat the waiting as consent.","You tell him that a pickup and a statement can be arranged without broadcasting his address. He agrees to testify on that basis. The distinction belongs in your record as carefully as any timestamp: what he is willing to say, and what he has not allowed you to share. After hearing your own borrowed voice, you have no excuse for letting a file answer that question on his behalf."],"scene:witness-return":["A rescue boat spots Salim's lamp and calls up to the roof. You keep his line open until the pilot confirms the pickup. Salim wraps the copied order in the plastic from his lunch before handing it down. “Terrible sandwich,” he tells you. “Excellent packaging.” The pilot laughs, and for the first time his channel carries a sound that is not being broken apart by rain or fear.","His written statement can go to a reviewer without the raw address attached. You save that permission with the account, remembering that tonight's rescue does not settle what you might later choose to publish. Salim has offered you evidence and asked you to protect a particular part of his life. The fact that he is leaving the roof cannot make either part of that agreement disappear.","“Leave my chair empty,” he says as the boat moves away. “You don't need to wait for me to tell you what to do.” You almost answer that you would have preferred that advice before the shift began. He seems to hear it anyway, and apologizes once more. You thank the pilot, confirm Salim's arrival route, and return to the station's other unanswered lines."],"scene:dog-bark":["The barking comes from a narrow service ledge below the landing. Your lamp finds a small dog pressed against the wall, determined to look as though he chose to be there. His collar tag reads BISCUIT. Above him, a gate worker has stopped on the maintenance ladder. “He won't climb on his own,” the worker calls. “I've tried explaining that the weather is terrible. He seems to agree without changing his plans.”","You ask whether the worker can reach him safely. There is a dry upper rung that gives access to the ledge, but rain keeps swallowing the edges of the route. The worker can make the lift if you hold the lamp steady and check where the ladder leads. Biscuit gives another offended bark. “Yes, I know,” the worker tells him. “You had a much better bed this morning.”","For a moment, the task is simple enough to grasp: one lamp, one route, one passenger who cannot understand the instructions. You tell the worker you will keep the light where it is needed, then inspect the flare and upper rung before anyone moves. There are plenty of reasons to hurry past this landing. None of them make the little dog below it less dependent on the person who has stayed."],"scene:dog-ladder":["Check the route flare and dry upper rung. The gate worker needs a clear path down to Biscuit and back to the tower stair; keep your lamp on that path while the worker makes the lift."],"scene:dog-home":["Biscuit arrives on the landing wrapped inside the gate worker's raincoat. He stays still until your lamp catches the bundle, then sneezes with such force that the worker nearly laughs themselves off balance. “Complaint received,” you tell him. The dog settles against the coat as if your apology is the least he deserves. You wait until the worker has both feet on the concrete before lowering the light.","The route you checked reaches the upper tower stair. The worker marks the ladder clearly for the next group, making sure the flare can be seen from below. What began as a way to collect one stranded dog has given you another path people can follow in the rain. You call the details to Lia rather than assuming somebody else will notice the marker. She asks you to repeat the landing it connects to.","When the route is on her board, Lia asks about the extra passenger. “Biscuit,” you say. “No surname supplied.” She takes a moment to make a space for him. “Put him under B. We'll deal with his paperwork after breakfast.” The worker pulls the coat closer around him. You add the dog and the ladder to the rescue board, then return to the calls waiting at the landing."],"scene:encounter-window":["A voice breaks through on the neighborhood channel while you watch the lit windows beyond the station. You keep the line open and ask where the caller is waiting, before deciding what help you can offer."],"encounter:encounter-window-1":["A child asks whether the sea can climb stairs. Her father whispers for you to tell her no, but you hear them moving toward an upstairs window, carrying a lamp between them. He wants a sentence that will stop her being frightened. You want to give them something they can use even if the water rises again. Neither wish is difficult to understand.","You ask whether the lamp is working, and the child answers before her father can. “It still has some left.” The boat crews are checking lit windows, but you cannot promise when one will reach theirs. You can guide her to hold the light where it can be seen, or explain what the crew is doing without pretending the danger has gone. She stays on the line, waiting for your answer."],"encounter:encounter-window-2":["A love song suddenly takes over the emergency band, tinny and much too cheerful for the rain. “Sorry,” a man says from a balcony. “Wrong button. Again. My wife warned me about this radio.” He struggles with the controls, and you hear someone in the room telling him to stop pressing things simply because they look important. For a few seconds, the city sounds almost ordinary.","The song is older than Nila, and familiar enough that you know where the chorus will end. Other calls still need this channel, but the balcony radio can do something useful once its owner finds the right button. You can ask him to relay route updates, or let the last chorus finish before clearing the band. He apologizes again, waiting to hear whether he has made a terrible mistake."],"encounter:encounter-window-3":["A woman calls to say she has opened her upstairs room to six neighbors. “Seven, if somebody is small. Or willing to fold.” She gives a little laugh, then asks whether there are people stranded on the market street who need somewhere above the water. The offer is practical and immediate. She has a room; she would rather it be crowded than leave someone waiting below.","You check which window belongs to her while she makes space behind the microphone. No fee or permission form is being requested, but the rescue crews still need to know where people have gone. You can share her location with them, or ask her to put the room on the live headcount. Both begin by taking the offer seriously enough to make sure it reaches the people who need it."],"scene:encounter-rooftops":["A light moves across a neighboring roof as the radio catches another local call. You ask for their position and listen for the people behind the signal; a marker is useful only if someone knows who is waiting beside it."],"encounter:encounter-rooftops-1":["A bedsheet flaps from the corner of a roof, catching the torchlight whenever the wind lifts it. Its owner tells you it is meant as a rescue flag. “It used to be my good wedding linen. My husband said we should save it for an occasion.” She pauses to help a neighbor move clear of the wet edge. “I think this qualifies.”","Two neighbors are waiting with her, and their lamp battery is failing. The sheet will remain visible when the torch goes out, but the crew needs its position recorded rather than left to chance. You can log it as a rescue marker, or ask the next roof to relay their lamp signal while it lasts. She gives you the location again, more slowly this time, trusting that somebody is writing it down."],"encounter:encounter-rooftops-2":["Someone has carried a birthday cake onto the roof. The candles are soaked, but SIXTY is still readable on the box, and the group has been arguing about whether to sing. “Only if you promise not to make me climb another stair,” the birthday woman says. “I've already earned next year's cake.” A tired laugh comes from the people keeping close beside her.","You ask where the group is waiting and hear the location twice over, each person afraid the others will leave something out. They need a pickup, and they also need a moment in which this remains her birthday. You can call a boat for the roof, or let the crew sing while you finish recording its position. Nothing about the joke makes them less frightened; it gives them a way to wait together."],"encounter:encounter-rooftops-3":["A shopkeeper is signaling from a roof with a shaving mirror, trying to catch the rescue lights between gusts of rain. The generator beside him drowns out your reply. His neighbor counts each flash into the radio, then starts again because the shopkeeper insists she missed one. Their argument is frustratingly clear; the location you actually need is not.","You ask them to slow down, letting the neighbor know you can hear her even if the shopkeeper cannot hear you. They are both trying to help and each thinks the other is making it harder. You can repeat a simple lamp signal for the rescue crew, or have the neighbor relay the roof's position directly. First, they need one instruction both can follow instead of two people shouting over the generator."],"scene:encounter-tunnel":["A knock reaches the maintenance channel, followed by a caller giving a location beneath the station. You ask them to stay on the line while you check what they need and which part of the passage is still dry."],"encounter:encounter-tunnel-1":["Three taps come through an open microphone before a cleaner answers. Her trolley is wedged across a flood door, and two people are waiting with her on the dry side. “It's the first time this thing has held still,” she says. You hear her checking that they are both there, then asking whether a rescue crew knows which side of the door they need to reach.","You repeat their position instead of answering with a vague promise that help is coming. The cleaner sounds relieved simply to hear it said correctly. You can register the dry-side pickup now, or keep the line open until a crew answers her directly. Either way, the useful part of the call is not the trolley's unlikely cooperation; it is making sure the people beside it do not disappear into an unnamed corridor."],"encounter:encounter-tunnel-2":["A retired plumber calls to correct the tunnel map you have been using. “That door's been a cupboard since 2008,” he says. “Lovely cupboard. Terrible exit.” He describes its shelves with enough irritation that you believe he has complained about them before. More importantly, he knows a dry side passage that reaches the upper landing, and the printed plan does not show it.","You have him repeat the turns, separating the landmark he has checked from the old arrow on your screen. Local knowledge could save the crew a wasted journey, but another confident voice is not automatically a verified route. You can add his checked passage to the live map, or ask a second worker to confirm it. He is willing to explain again; he would rather be interrupted than have someone sent toward a cupboard."],"encounter:encounter-tunnel-3":["A security guard calls while he and his partner carry a resident toward the laundry stair. He has lost one shoe somewhere behind them. “Mark me incomplete,” he says, and his partner laughs so hard the radio clips the sound. You let the noise settle before asking for their location. Beneath it you can hear three people breathing, all of them still moving together.","The guard keeps trying to make the report only about the resident they are carrying. You remind him that he and his partner belong in it too. You can confirm all three on the headcount, or ask the shelter to have dry shoes ready for their arrival. He starts to protest that the shoe is hardly an emergency, then loses the argument when his partner tells you to make it a pair."],"scene:encounter-cargo":["A delivery update comes through without a signature. You ask the caller what they are carrying and where they are waiting; tonight, a cargo entry may be hiding a person who needs to be counted."],"encounter:encounter-cargo-1":["A driver asks whether the priority lane includes a furious cat in a rice sack. “The cat has expressed an opinion,” he says as an indignant sound interrupts him. Two families are waiting in his van, and he cannot make the passenger list sound orderly no matter how he tries. “I'm not asking for special treatment. I just don't want to leave anyone off it.”","You ask him to begin with the people, then include the animal they are bringing with them. The driver's relief is audible; he had expected another argument over who belongs. You can log the families and their cat together, or direct the van to the shelter intake crew. The loudest passenger keeps objecting while he waits for your answer, making it difficult for either of you to sound properly official."],"encounter:encounter-cargo-2":["A driver calls about an unsigned envelope that reached the dock office before Raka's delivery. He remembers the seal: DIRECTOR'S ROUTING DESK. He cannot tell you what was inside, and you write that limit beside his account. What he can describe is the earlier handoff. The order was already moving through other hands before a young courier became the easiest person to question.","You ask whether he is willing to have his account included in the record. He is, though he wants you to keep the difference between seeing an envelope and knowing its contents clear. You can save that voluntary statement, or tell the channel that Raka did not write the routing order. Neither turns the driver into an all-knowing witness. It gives the courier back a part of the truth suspicion had taken from him."],"encounter:encounter-cargo-3":["A baker offers his delivery van and thirty rolls that never reached the morning order. “My accountant will hate this,” he says. “Let him. He can complain from his dry office.” Then he gets down to the practical details: the van can collect people from the inland road after they cross, and the rolls can go where there are more empty stomachs than supplies. He needs someone to tell him which help to arrange first.","You note that the van's offer depends on people reaching the inland side; it cannot solve the crossing itself. Still, a pickup or breakfast waiting beyond it gives the rescue crew one less problem to manage. You can assign the van to that arrival point, or send the rolls to the shelter kitchen. The baker asks you to repeat the destination, already looking for something useful to do with the rest of his night."],"scene:encounter-rescue":["A caller reaches the rescue channel as you pause on the landing. You ask them to tell you where they are, then listen for what they need before deciding whom to contact."],"encounter:encounter-rescue-1":["A boy calls to report a coat left on the inland stair. You ask whether it is blocking the route. “There's a person in it,” he says, suddenly remembering what matters. “I should have said that first.” You tell him he is doing the right thing by calling and ask him to stay where the radio can hear him. In the background, an adult says they are awake but need help getting up.","The person has walked as far as they can manage and is sitting clear of the main path. The boy stayed when the others moved on because he did not want them to wait alone. You can send the nearest crew straight to the stair or keep both callers on the line while help is arranged. Either way, the report needs a person and a position, not a coat someone might step around."],"encounter:encounter-rescue-2":["Two newlyweds reach the channel with one dry suitcase and an argument about whose clothes made it inside. They stop arguing when you ask whom they are looking for. The bride's uncle, Budi, left to help someone on the lower stair and has not rejoined them. “We can have another wedding,” she tells you. “We can't hire another Uncle Budi.” Her husband quietly supplies the last place they saw him.","They have not heard that he is hurt; they simply cannot find him among the arriving groups. You explain that a missing reply and a confirmed absence are different things, then check the headcount channel. You can put Budi's name on the open search list or ask the shelter desk whether his arrival has already been recorded. The couple needs somewhere to direct the question instead of carrying it from person to person."],"encounter:encounter-rescue-3":["A fisherman calls from a boat tied beside the inland stair. He has been watching people arrive, and some are struggling with the last stretch. “For once, the sea is bringing customers to me,” he says, then asks which crew needs the boat. He makes the joke sound easy, but he is already checking the rope and clearing space. There is room for the slower walkers once they reach the pickup point.","You ask him to describe the mooring so another operator can find it. He repeats the position and promises to stay available for a reply. You can reserve his boat for people who need the extra help or put it on Lia's live route board so she can assign it as requests arrive. The offer will not solve the whole crossing, but it can spare someone the final walk they no longer have the strength to make."],"scene:encounter-static":["A faint voice breaks through the receiver's static. You move the radio under your coat to hear clearly and check whether it is a live caller or another recorded fragment."],"encounter:encounter-static-1":["A man gives his address three times, each repetition faster than the last. He seems to expect the line to cut before anyone can write it down. You read the location back to him before he can begin a fourth time. There is a pause. “Oh,” he says. “You heard me.” He tells you where he is waiting above the water and asks whether someone can pass that information to a boat.","You confirm that the position is on your board. He does not need another promise that everything will be fine; he needs to know what happens to the request after this conversation ends. You can relay his location to the boat crew immediately or stay with him on the line while the crew replies. He listens as you explain the next step, no longer spending every spare second repeating his address."],"encounter:encounter-static-2":["A loose receiver beside the landing plays one last ECHO fragment. Your old voice begins telling people to remain where they are, using the calm tone you practiced in the training room. You check the local lead and unplug it. The replay stops halfway through a word. Nearby callers are still listening, so you explain that they heard a recording, not a new instruction from the rescue crew.","For a moment you hear your own breathing without the borrowed voice beneath it. You can log the fragment so the next operator recognizes it as a replay, or use the channel to remind nearby callers to follow the live route board. Either choice gives the interruption a clear explanation. You have heard enough tonight to know how quickly an unchallenged recording can begin sounding like permission to wait."],"encounter:encounter-static-3":["Iman's daughter has borrowed a neighbor's radio. She tells you she is waiting somewhere safe, then asks whether her father still thinks he has to fix all the lights before coming home. “Tell Dad he can come back even if the stars stay broken,” she says. You remember the way he described his work to her and ask her to repeat the message so you can get every word right.","You promise to pass it on, without promising a return you cannot yet confirm. She accepts that more quietly than you expected and stays near the neighbor while you decide whom to contact. You can send her exact words to Iman or let the shelter desk know where she is waiting safely. A rescue report can carry this too: the place someone hopes to return to, and the person who will be there when they do."],"ending:fullSignal":["By morning, both lower groups have reached the inland road. The full archive shows how Arga's signed priority rule and the reset hid people still waiting for help. ECHO is off the live channel, and the record can be checked outside the station. You have wanted an answer since the first false announcement. Now it is there, with your own decisions beside it.","The raw release carries private names and addresses as well as proof. Some callers must begin protecting their records while they are still finding dry clothes. You stay to help and to answer questions about the release. Bringing people out of the flood was real; so was sending their information beyond their control. The inquiry needs an account of both.","At the new rescue desk, you leave room beside each name for a confirmed position and the voice that gave it. No one is marked gone because a recording says a building is empty. When the next operator arrives, you show them the open entries before handing over the chair. Morning has brought a stronger record, and people who still deserve a say in what it contains."],"ending:shelteredTruth":["The protected record reaches the reviewers with Arga's authorization, the time sequence, and the hidden requests intact. Private addresses remain sealed. You have not made the accusation smaller by keeping those details out; you have separated what proves the decision from what would expose the people harmed by it. For the first time tonight, that distinction survives beyond your desk.","The inquiry will need independent checks and people willing to speak. Some questions cannot be answered as quickly as a raw broadcast would travel, and you accept the work that follows. At the shelter, a caller asks who will be allowed to read their statement. You can give a clear answer before asking them to tell the story again. It is a small beginning, but it belongs to them.","ECHO has lost the live line. Your own voice, tired and imperfect, keeps asking for positions while the rescue crews finish their lists. You correct a place name aloud instead of hiding the mistake. The people listening can question you, and you can answer. The record reaches morning with its evidence preserved and enough room left for the lives that evidence was meant to protect."],"ending:ashes":["The source archive is gone. It will not assemble another instruction from the voice you lent for training, and its original audit cannot be read again either. You remain beside the rescue log for a while after the deletion. Stopping the reuse of this source was the choice you made. It has also removed the strongest record of how that reuse was authorized and concealed.","In your live account, you name Arga's priority rule and explain the reset that hid thirteen minutes of requests. An inquiry can follow independent copies and willing testimony, but it will have to build what the original could have shown directly. You do not promise a quick verdict or claim that every possible copy has vanished. You tell the reviewers what you destroyed, as well as why.","Morning arrives with the rescue work still needing an accurate handover. You carry the names, confirmed positions, and open searches to the next desk. The missing archive does not settle what happened to the callers or excuse your part in the record's loss. When someone asks you to repeat the account, you sit down and begin again, this time with a living voice they can question."],"ending:exposedWitness":["The raw archive reaches the public channel with the signed order and the hidden queue. It also carries Salim's home address. He asked you to protect that detail before giving his testimony, and you agreed. By the time you understand how far the copy has traveled, the promise cannot be restored by taking your own release down. His rescue was confirmed; his privacy was not protected.","Salim reaches the boat, but his family must now move before he can speak safely to the inquiry. He does not withdraw what he saw, and he does not tell you the exposure was necessary. You remember the care he took to offer a copied order while keeping his home out of it. Both were possible. Your final release put them back together without asking him.","Arga's decisions can be investigated with a record strong enough to follow. You stay for that work and make sure your own release is recorded too. The account of this night would be incomplete if it named only the harm done before you took the chair. You help Salim secure what can still be secured, knowing that useful help now does not make the broken promise disappear."],"ending:fracturedTruth":["The night ends with a record that still needs work. The rescue board shows confirmed arrivals beside names the boat crews must keep searching for. Your account identifies the priority rule and the hidden requests, but the surviving sequence needs checking, and people must decide whether they are willing to testify. Reaching morning has not made every part of the explanation complete.","You set the documents beside the live log instead of giving them a conclusion they cannot yet support. A reviewer asks where the sequence is certain and where it remains open. You answer as carefully as you can, marking the gaps rather than smoothing them away. The callers had to make choices with incomplete information; they deserve to see the same limits in the account written afterward.","ECHO is disconnected from the live channel. That gives you a place to start, though it cannot settle the questions left by the evacuation or the release. You stay while the next crew takes over the searches. When another reply comes in, you add the position and the time, then check the name again. The account remains open because there are still people and facts to find."],"ending:lostSignal":["The people already above the flood reach shelter. On the lower route, the crossing closes before the remaining group and Iman can use it. The boat crews keep searching their last confirmed positions. You hand over those names with every detail you have, resisting the urge to turn an unanswered call into an ending. An accurate place and time are still something another crew can work with.","ECHO is stopped. Your account explains the signed priority rule, the hidden requests, and the training voice used without your live permission. The mechanism is clearer now than it was when the callers first asked for help. Knowing it does not reopen the crossing. You can describe the wrong decisions that brought the night here and still have to answer for the rescue choices made from your chair.","Morning gives the station light without giving it certainty. You stay with the open search board and make room for each report the boats send back. There is regret in the work, and work that cannot wait for regret to pass. When a channel falls silent, you check the frequency and call again. The name remains on the page until a living person can tell you where to put it."],"epilogue:0":["Bu Sari keeps the bell going until every lit window has answered. Afterward, she writes the ringing pattern beside the market door so nobody needs a working screen to know what it means. ‘Next time, we start sooner,’ she says, testing the rope with one tired hand."],"epilogue:1":["Lia checks the sealed clinic cooler into the hill shelter and thanks the people who kept its route dry. Nearby, Deni finally has a chair and two rolls from the kitchen. He makes room for the second roll on his lap, as though a proper breakfast deserves a seat too."],"epilogue:2":["Nila's laugh stays off the public record. At breakfast, you order one cup and listen to her message again with the volume low. The joke is still ordinary, and that is what you needed: a moment when she was only your sister, complaining about breakfast and expecting you to answer."],"epilogue:3":["Someone remembers the breakfast joke you shared about Nila and brings you toast. You explain that she would have complained about this toast too, and manage a small laugh. Her recording remains private. The kindness reaches you without anyone needing to hear the piece you kept for yourself."],"epilogue:4":["Biscuit falls asleep across the gate worker's boots as soon as they find a dry place to sit. The worker needs to stand up twice and abandons the idea both times. ‘He's had a long shift,’ he tells a volunteer, as though that explains why the whole shelter must step around them."],"epilogue:5":["Salim chooses the name on the cover of his statement and checks that his address stays sealed. His copied order goes to the reviewers with his testimony. Before handing it over, he asks to read the page once more; this time, nobody treats that request as an obstacle to the truth."],"epilogue:6":["The baker's van makes three trips inland. By the last one, the passengers have learned where to sit without crushing the bread boxes. He keeps a receipt for thirty rescued breakfasts and says his accountant can decide which column that belongs in when everyone has had some sleep."],"epilogue:7":["At the inland bus, Iman tells his daughter that the stars can wait tonight. ‘I knew that,’ she answers, as if she has been waiting for him to understand something simple. He sits with the yellow gloves in his lap and lets her talk about home without once checking a switch."],"epilogue:8":["The child holds her lamp at the upstairs window while her father stays beside her. The boat crew writes down the signal and position before answering. Nobody asks her to stop being frightened. She only needs to keep the light where they can find it, and someone tells her when they have."],"epilogue:9":["The child's father repeats your plan carefully, leaving out the promise you could not make. Their lit window remains on the rescue board with a confirmed position. When she asks whether anyone heard, he can answer that part truthfully and point to the lamp she is already holding."],"epilogue:10":["The balcony radio becomes a relay for the neighboring windows. Its owner puts tape over the emergency button so he can find it in the dark. He checks the label twice, then asks the next caller to repeat their position; the channel has found a steadier use than an accidental love song."],"epilogue:11":["The balcony singer finishes one chorus before the channel returns to rescue calls. Later, a shelter volunteer hums the tune while handing out dry blankets. Someone recognizes it and joins in for a line. For a minute, the familiar song makes the room feel less like a place nobody chose to be."],"epilogue:12":["The upstairs room takes in the market neighbors. Its owner moves one chair, then another, and discovers that seven people fit without anyone having to fold themselves in half. She tells the last arrival to keep the chair: if they have made it upstairs, there is room to sit properly."],"epilogue:13":["The offered room goes onto the live shelter list with its location and headcount. A crew checks how many people are there instead of asking whether the owner has the right surname. She reads the count back through her radio and keeps a place by the door for the next neighbor."],"epilogue:14":["The wedding bedsheet gives the boat crew a clear marker for the roof pickup. Its owner gathers the wet cloth afterward and asks whether seawater stains count as an anniversary present. The sheet has done a job neither of them imagined when they chose it; for now, neither wants to throw it away."],"epilogue:15":["When the first roof's lamp weakens, the neighbors repeat its signal from their own roof. The boat finds both groups on the same pass. On the arrival sheet, the two locations remain separate, but the people keep thanking one another across the shelter as though the roofs are still side by side."],"epilogue:16":["The birthday group goes onto the pickup request with the cake box as its marker. The woman keeps offering slices while they wait, insisting that a sixtieth birthday should feed somebody. A crew member writes down the location first, then thanks her for saving a piece for people still on duty."],"epilogue:17":["The crew's uneven birthday song travels all the way to the shelter radio. By the final line, several people in the room are singing too. The woman says the missing candles can be forgiven, but warns them she expects better harmony next year. For once, next year enters the conversation easily."],"epilogue:18":["The shopkeeper's mirror signal reaches the rescue crew and gives them the roof's position. When he can return to the shop, the mirror will go on a new emergency shelf. He says it can go back to shaving duty later; right now, it has earned a place where everyone can find it."],"epilogue:19":["The neighbor confirms which roof is sending the mirror signal, and both names reach the pickup list. Their argument finally gives way to the practical question of who has the drier blanket. Neither apologizes very gracefully, but they pass the blanket between them without being asked."],"epilogue:20":["The people on the dry side of the trolley are added to the pickup board. Their cleaner makes sure each name is heard before asking whether she can have the trolley back when the water falls. It was always a stubborn thing, she says, but tonight that was exactly the quality they needed."],"epilogue:21":["You keep the cleaner's channel open until the crew answers directly. She listens to the pickup plan, then says, ‘Good. A person, not another hold tune.’ Before the line closes, she repeats the location back herself. This time she knows who heard it, and what they are going to do next."],"epilogue:22":["The retired plumber's passage is added to the live map, with a note explaining where the official drawing is wrong. Someone labels the cupboard beside it STILL NOT AN EXIT. He approves the correction and asks them to keep it there after the flood, when people are less inclined to ask directions."],"epilogue:23":["A second worker confirms the plumber's passage before the route note is shared. Both names and the check time stay beside the correction. The plumber reads it once, satisfied that the map finally says where people can walk rather than where somebody once intended to build a door."],"epilogue:24":["The guard, his partner, and the resident are each confirmed in the headcount. He makes you repeat all three names, then finally admits that only one of his shoes is missing. You correct the equipment note and leave the people counted as they are; a lost shoe does not reduce the group to two."],"epilogue:25":["The shelter finds the guard two dry shoes, although they are different colors. He tests both on the floor and declares himself fully restored. His partner starts to object, then gives up and passes him a towel. For the rest of the morning, the shoes are much easier to find than he is."],"epilogue:26":["The van's families stay together on the arrival sheet, with a note for the furious cat in their care. A volunteer checks that nobody has been separated before suggesting a signature. The cat contributes a complaint instead, which its owner says is the closest they are likely to get to consent."],"epilogue:27":["The van reaches shelter intake, where a volunteer finds a quieter corner for the cat and its family. The driver's next report is noticeably easier to hear. He thanks you for the route, then thanks the volunteer with even more feeling; everyone in the van has been listening to that cat for hours."],"epilogue:28":["The driver gives a voluntary account of the earlier envelope, with the details he actually saw kept separate from guesses. Raka's delivery becomes one link in the record, not an accusation large enough to explain everything. The driver reads his statement back and corrects a time before signing."],"epilogue:29":["During the call, you told Raka plainly that carrying the envelope did not make him the author of the order. He went quiet, then asked you to record one request for any future delivery: a person's name to sign for it, not another console code. You wrote it beside his name. It was a small request, but for once he had chosen the terms himself."],"epilogue:30":["The baker's thirty rolls reach the shelter kitchen still warm enough for people to notice. A child saves half of one for a relative whose name is on the boat list. The volunteer wraps that half carefully and writes the name on the paper, keeping the small request beside the larger ones."],"epilogue:31":["The coat on the stair becomes a named pickup request instead of a shape somebody might overlook. The boy waits beside the tired person until the crew arrives and takes their hand. Only then does he let the blanket slip from his own shoulder so it can be wrapped around both of them."],"epilogue:32":["You stay on the channel with the boy and the tired stair walker until a crew can hear them both. Two voices mean two people to locate, even when one can say very little. The boy repeats the position for his companion, and you read it back before passing the line to the rescuers."],"epilogue:33":["Uncle Budi's name stays on the open headcount with the details his niece can confirm. The newlyweds check the shelter board together instead of separating to search in the flood. Every new arrival gives them another reason to look; nobody closes the entry simply because a reply is taking time."],"epilogue:34":["The shelter desk finds Budi on its arrival sheet and confirms the entry with him. He asks why his niece has chosen this weather for a honeymoon, then waits impatiently to speak to her himself. You pass the answer on with the location attached; the joke is welcome, but the confirmed place is what brings relief."],"epilogue:35":["The fisherman waits beside the inland stair for the people who cannot keep the faster walkers' pace. His boat carries them across the last stretch toward shelter. He counts everyone aboard before pushing off, then asks the quickest passenger to sit down and let the slowest one settle first."],"epilogue:36":["Lia adds the fisherman's boat to the live route board, with its location and the help it can offer. He accepts the assignment without asking for a priority stamp. When the first request comes, he reads it back and checks the number of passengers, treating the list as a job rather than a favor owed to him."],"epilogue:37":["The man's address reaches the boat crew correctly on the first relay. You listen while they read it back, then tell him which detail has been confirmed. He does not have to say it a fourth time or wonder which part was lost. The crew has a place to find, and he knows someone has heard it."],"epilogue:38":["You stay with the man until the crew answers his call. ‘You heard,’ he says once more, but this time the words are a confirmation rather than a question. You tell him who is taking over the line before leaving it, so the change of operator does not become another silence he must explain himself into."],"epilogue:39":["The last ECHO fragment is marked REPLAY on the record. When its old command is mentioned again, the label travels with it, along with the time it was captured. Nobody has to guess whether that calm voice is giving a current order. The live route comes from the people checking it now."],"epilogue:40":["Nearby callers follow the live route board rather than the old announcement. The pencil corrections remain visible, with the check times beside them. One person asks why a route changed, and gets an answer instead of an order to stop questioning it. The next caller knows they can ask too."],"epilogue:41":["The shelter desk confirms that Iman's daughter is waiting safely and keeps his name beside hers on the family contact list. You pass on the place as carefully as any rescue position. Her father no longer has to wonder where she is while he holds the line; the crews know where to send his next message."],"epilogue:42":["Iman hears his daughter's words through the battery radio while his position remains with the boat crew. You pass both details together: where he was last heard, and the message he needs to carry home. The search entry stays open. Her words have reached him, even though his own arrival is not yet confirmed."],"epilogue:43":["Mara's watch still says midnight when both patients sit down at the shelter table. The man who kept apologizing for being slow finishes his breakfast and asks for seconds. Mara passes the request to the kitchen without a joke. Only after the plate returns does she tell him there is no hurry now."],"epilogue:44":["Raka hangs the wet red strap beside the inland bus. When the next delivery is mentioned, he asks who will sign for it. ‘Someone who's actually there,’ he adds, keeping the question practical. He is willing to carry supplies again; this time, he wants to know whose hands they are going into."],"evidence:shift-log":["00:31 — Console C recorded the evacuation announcement. At 00:36, the clinic route was still occupied.","The station prints entries automatically. Its clock passed the midnight check. Pencil warning: “Do not trust the broadcast header.”"],"evidence:maintenance-note":["Channel: 96.4 MHz. The desk receiver has its own battery. The generator supports two external circuits.","CLINIC: evacuation lights. RELAY: outgoing transmission and tracing. DOCK: exit gate and beacon. Treatment equipment has separate power."],"evidence:relay-message":["Header: 00:18. “East clinic cleared. Close the route. Transfer emergency power to the dock.”","The same chime and clipped cough repeat. It is a recording. The source console is missing from the header."],"evidence:red-strap":["Raka’s photo shows a wet stairwell, a red bag strap, and an arrow from the dock walkway to the clinic service door.","He circled the arrow: “I can reach this. Tell me where to go.”"],"evidence:forged-time":["Header: 00:18. Recording: 00:31. The order predates its own recording by thirteen minutes. The clinic was still occupied at 00:36.","The timestamp is unreliable. A changed time or reused header could explain it. The sender’s identity and intention remain unknown."],"evidence:echo-carrier":["101.7 MHz carries the same dispatch in your old cadence. A breath clicks at identical points. The live callers never repeat."],"evidence:queue-ledger":["Console C queued the clip at 00:31, then reset the batch header to 00:18. The reversal hid thirteen minutes of requests, not thirteen minutes of time."],"evidence:consent-form":["You signed a voice archive test after Nila’s ferry accident. Its stated purpose: assemble training announcements. Live dispatch required a new approval, never requested."],"evidence:splice-map":["ECHO joined words from your training takes. It ranked the dock above the clinic under a director-approved traffic rule. The patients were misclassified as already evacuated."],"evidence:director-order":["Arga’s signature authorizes dock priority and a queue header reset. A note says: “Suppress unresolved transfers until batch reconciliation.” Staff names are attached."],"evidence:archive-proof":["The ledger and signed order agree. Arga enabled the rule; ECHO assembled the announcement from your archive. The callers are live people harmed by a hidden queue."],"evidence:tide-table":["At 02:10 the harbor surge reaches the lower footbridge. The tower stair remains a viable inland route if someone opens the manual latch. The bell can warn nearby homes."],"evidence:bell-rope":["A market bell rope snagged around a shutter hook. Freed from the hook, it reaches the upper landing. Bu Sari knows the neighborhood signal: three steady rings."],"evidence:cargo-tag":["The sealed cooler belongs to the hill shelter clinic. Lia will handle its contents. A marked laundry passage avoids the submerged loading ramp."],"evidence:nila-message":["“Don’t make your sad face at me. I owe you breakfast. If the ferry’s late, order mine anyway.” Her laugh survives at the end. This was a private message, never training material."],"evidence:salim-statement":["Salim saw Arga override a failed clinic transfer. He copied the signed priority order before leaving to open a manual route. His home address appears in the raw audit."],"evidence:route-flare":["Beside the stranded dog, a flare marks a maintenance ladder leading to the tower’s upper stair. Its rung markers remain visible above the surge."]},"clips":{"The city loses power while you are still taking off your wet coat. For a moment, the dispatch room is as dark as the harbor outside. Then the generator coughs into life, lighting an empty chair, a cold cup of coffee, and a printer feeding paper onto the floor. The senior operator should have been here to hand over the night. He has left you the desk instead.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/6863305d6725bead66f8bb37.mp3","duration":22.88,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.71}]},"Before you can find his note, the radio announces, ‘East clinic cleared.’ A woman breaks in over the last word. ‘Please tell me that wasn't your order. I'm standing in that clinic, and I have two patients with me.’ You reach for the handset. ‘I'm here,’ you tell her. It is the only answer you can give with confidence.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/5d166ee06e35fa27c721ba31.mp3","duration":18.105,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":3.03},{"speaker":"echo","start":3.1,"end":4.495},{"speaker":"narrator","start":4.565,"end":6.695},{"speaker":"mara","start":6.765,"end":12.075},{"speaker":"narrator","start":12.145,"end":13.445},{"speaker":"operator","start":13.515,"end":14.305},{"speaker":"narrator","start":14.375,"end":17.935}]},"This is your first live evacuation. Until tonight, emergencies were recordings you could pause and replay; now the woman is waiting while water rises somewhere you cannot see. Two more channels light up beside hers. You pull the chair closer, take a fresh sheet of paper, and ask her to stay on the line. The announcement repeats behind her, ending on a breath that sounds strangely familiar.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/2c12ff4e45bcbef1b3b34e88.mp3","duration":24.87,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.7}]},"Inspect the shift log and circuit diagram before giving a route. The telephone cable is cut; the ringing is coming through the radio speaker.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/dd46fc68273e612a06fe2b29.mp3","duration":8.49,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":8.32}]},"The receiver has its own battery. Tune to the diagram's 96.4 MHz and capture the repeating announcement so you can check what it actually says.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/2435053f1cf4d8af86d297fb.mp3","duration":9.52,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":9.35}]},"‘My name is Mara. I'm the nurse on the night shift,’ the woman says when you ask her name. Something taps against a railing. ‘That's my watch. It stopped at midnight, so at least one of us is having a quiet night.’ You ask where she is, and the joke leaves her voice. She is at the clinic service corridor with two patients. The announcement says they have already gone. None of them has reached the door.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f2598b00e8f2941610888d79.mp3","duration":24.04,"cues":[{"speaker":"mara","start":0.05,"end":3.57},{"speaker":"narrator","start":3.64,"end":7.7},{"speaker":"mara","start":7.77,"end":12.94},{"speaker":"narrator","start":13.01,"end":23.87}]},"‘The treatment equipment still has power,’ she explains. ‘It's the corridor lights we need. They can both walk if I guide them, but I can't lead them down stairs I can't see.’ One patient says something beyond the microphone. Mara answers gently, ‘You don't need to apologize. We're going at your pace.’ When she returns to you, her words are firmer. ‘He's been saying sorry for being slow since the water came in.’":{"src":"assets/voice/en/dfbc09ad52c3b773d3251c23.mp3","duration":25.08,"cues":[{"speaker":"mara","start":0.05,"end":2.93},{"speaker":"narrator","start":3,"end":4.26},{"speaker":"mara","start":4.33,"end":10.58},{"speaker":"narrator","start":10.65,"end":14.89},{"speaker":"mara","start":14.96,"end":18.05},{"speaker":"narrator","start":18.12,"end":20.61},{"speaker":"mara","start":20.68,"end":24.91}]},"You write down the service door and the number of people with her. Water strikes the stairs in short, heavy waves. ‘I know you have other calls,’ Mara says. ‘Just don't let that announcement turn us into an empty building. Tell me what you can switch on, and what you need me to do.’ You leave her channel open while you connect the technician who controls the circuits.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/d043ddb756a1e2e9d2aa066f.mp3","duration":20.06,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":6.88},{"speaker":"mara","start":6.95,"end":8.55},{"speaker":"narrator","start":8.62,"end":9.69},{"speaker":"mara","start":9.76,"end":15.84},{"speaker":"narrator","start":15.91,"end":19.89}]},"A knock against metal comes through the next channel. ‘Iman. Yellow gloves, switch cabinet. Still here.’ He asks you to look at the circuit diagram rather than the station's status screen. ‘That screen tells you what was requested. I can tell you what's actually on.’ The generator can hold two routes. A third will trip its protection and leave you with none, however much everyone needs it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/0c27e14afb13faf065260762.mp3","duration":22.17,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":2.75},{"speaker":"iman","start":2.82,"end":6.82},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.89,"end":11.11},{"speaker":"iman","start":11.18,"end":14.84},{"speaker":"narrator","start":14.91,"end":22}]},"You read the labels aloud: clinic lights, relay transmission, dock gate. Iman confirms each one. The receiver can keep running on its own battery, but the relay is needed to trace the order and transmit beyond the station. Mara needs a lit way out; the caller at the dock needs an open gate. ‘I can't make that a kind choice,’ Iman says. ‘I can make sure you understand it before you choose.’":{"src":"assets/voice/en/01e54a12a7561f89255686fd.mp3","duration":25.68,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":19.95},{"speaker":"iman","start":20.02,"end":21.86},{"speaker":"narrator","start":21.93,"end":22.96},{"speaker":"iman","start":23.03,"end":25.51}]},"He rests a gloved hand on the switches and waits while you write. ‘My daughter thinks I fix stars,’ he adds. ‘I told her I'd explain streetlights when she was older. Tonight I'd settle for two lamps and a bus home.’ For the first time, you hear the tired man behind the technical answers. You ask him to hold his position while you connect the third caller. ‘I will,’ he says. ‘Keep talking to us.’":{"src":"assets/voice/en/3d662b2e4aa7ff2352aeff6e.mp3","duration":23.31,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":3.8},{"speaker":"iman","start":3.87,"end":5.95},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.02,"end":6.79},{"speaker":"iman","start":6.86,"end":12.27},{"speaker":"narrator","start":12.34,"end":20.04},{"speaker":"iman","start":20.11,"end":20.86},{"speaker":"narrator","start":20.93,"end":21.75},{"speaker":"iman","start":21.82,"end":23.14}]},"The third caller answers too quickly, as though he has been rehearsing what to say. ‘Raka. I'm a courier. Red strap on my bag, if somebody comes looking.’ The dock gate locked while he was making a delivery. Water is now over his shoes, and the exit sign gives him no useful answer. You ask him to describe what he can reach without going farther into the flood.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/32747818cb354477f07abda5.mp3","duration":20.61,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":4.59},{"speaker":"raka","start":4.66,"end":8.31},{"speaker":"narrator","start":8.38,"end":20.44}]},"‘I can see a maintenance stair leading toward the clinic,’ he says. ‘I can get to the bottom of it. Do you know if it's clear?’ A photograph arrives: wet floor, a railing, and the start of a stair disappearing out of frame. You save it rather than guess. Raka begins another message, deletes it, and returns to the microphone. ‘You are sending someone, aren't you?’":{"src":"assets/voice/en/1de511096b0e21e2ac6f6195.mp3","duration":21.58,"cues":[{"speaker":"raka","start":0.05,"end":2.9},{"speaker":"narrator","start":2.97,"end":3.79},{"speaker":"raka","start":3.86,"end":6.74},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.81,"end":20.03},{"speaker":"raka","start":20.1,"end":21.41}]},"You tell him you are checking the routes with the people already there. After a pause, he adds that he delivered an envelope to Console C earlier tonight. ‘Routine paperwork. I left it where they told me. That's all I know.’ The words come out carefully, unlike his question about rescue. You need to know whether that delivery connects to the false announcement, but Raka is still standing in rising water while you ask.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/911306f4d4be58fa1d1e7a55.mp3","duration":24.91,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":8.83},{"speaker":"raka","start":8.9,"end":13.04},{"speaker":"narrator","start":13.11,"end":24.74}]},"‘Who signed for it?’ you ask. Raka lets out a small, frustrated breath. ‘Nobody. They paid extra to leave it at the console, so I left it. I should've checked, all right?’ You hear him shift his bag higher. He has told you about the delivery, but he cannot give you a name. Asking the same question more sharply will not make one appear.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/56ff5a2211c4327f93c154b7.mp3","duration":20.87,"cues":[{"speaker":"operator","start":0.05,"end":1},{"speaker":"narrator","start":1.07,"end":4.96},{"speaker":"raka","start":5.03,"end":10.29},{"speaker":"narrator","start":10.36,"end":20.7}]},"Mara cuts across the shared channel. ‘He's standing in water. Ask him something that helps him move.’ Iman's meter clicks steadily behind her words. You can hear why she is angry, and why the missing signature still matters: an evacuation order is wrong, and that envelope reached the very desk you are using. None of it proves that the frightened courier wrote the order.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f89fc5cabeaede9ba518d850.mp3","duration":27.23,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":2.31},{"speaker":"mara","start":2.38,"end":7.9},{"speaker":"narrator","start":7.97,"end":27.06}]},"Raka waits for your answer. If you reassure him, he may be able to slow down and remember a useful detail. If you press him, you may get the detail at the cost of his willingness to listen. Either way, he needs a route as well as questions. You move the photograph beside your notes and decide how to speak to the person at the other end of the line.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/a49fb1aee257a6b4c1cc182c.mp3","duration":20.48,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":20.31}]},"Raka sends the route photograph again, this time with a circle around the stair toward the clinic. ‘That one. I can reach it without going back through the gate.’ You ask him to keep the photograph open while you check the route. It gives you a connection between two callers, but not a promise that the crossing is safe now. The water has changed since the picture was taken.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/89245947ccd96ceb6de66195.mp3","duration":19.75,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":5.09},{"speaker":"raka","start":5.16,"end":7.74},{"speaker":"narrator","start":7.81,"end":19.58}]},"Iman lowers his voice. ‘We have something else we can check. The time on that order—does it match the recording log?’ You place the printed entries beside the captured announcement. It is tempting to turn every odd detail into one explanation, especially with three people waiting for you to sound certain. Iman offers a smaller, more useful task: test the part that has a record.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f2640cdaafab319cf8a3342f.mp3","duration":22.42,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":1.42},{"speaker":"iman","start":1.49,"end":6.26},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.33,"end":22.25}]},"‘I'm checking it,’ you tell the channel, so the silence does not sound like you have gone away. Raka stays near the stair, Mara keeps her patients together, and Iman holds the cabinet. You save the route photo and begin comparing the documents. Whatever the mismatch means, your next order will have to come with a reason someone else can follow.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/eeca0149d9f773b5b83c59bd.mp3","duration":19.36,"cues":[{"speaker":"operator","start":0.05,"end":0.97},{"speaker":"narrator","start":1.04,"end":19.19}]},"‘Stay with me. We'll get you out first,’ you tell Raka. For several seconds you hear him breathing; then the words come more steadily. ‘Thank you. The delivery code was C-18. They made me repeat it before I left.’ You write it down without interrupting. Once he knows you are still trying to help him, he no longer has to spend every breath defending himself.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f1f277170e1a2b15cd1c6769.mp3","duration":22.32,"cues":[{"speaker":"operator","start":0.05,"end":2.24},{"speaker":"narrator","start":2.31,"end":7.79},{"speaker":"raka","start":7.86,"end":13.77},{"speaker":"narrator","start":13.84,"end":22.15}]},"He sends the route photograph again and circles the stair toward the clinic. ‘I can reach that one.’ You ask him to stay where he can find it while you check the crossing with Mara and Iman. There is still water between him and a safe landing, but now you have a route to discuss instead of a quarrel to finish. Mara quietly confirms that she is listening.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/e2b6d665913cb4340b0d10ad.mp3","duration":23.13,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":4.58},{"speaker":"raka","start":4.65,"end":6.19},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.26,"end":22.96}]},"‘Keep the code,’ Iman says. ‘Now compare the announcement's time with the recording log. That's something we can test.’ You place the documents together and tell Raka what you are checking. He waits, trusting the first half of your promise while you work on the second. You cannot make the route safe by sounding kind; you can make sure he is ready to hear the plan when it is clear.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/411aec235f61c1a5b3e24892.mp3","duration":21.81,"cues":[{"speaker":"iman","start":0.05,"end":1.3},{"speaker":"narrator","start":1.37,"end":2.4},{"speaker":"iman","start":2.47,"end":6.69},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.76,"end":21.64}]},"‘C-18. There was no name,’ Raka answers after you ask who paid him. His voice has gone flat. ‘Please stop making me prove I deserve a way out.’ He sends the route photograph, then closes his microphone before you can reply. You still have his position and a possible stair toward the clinic. What you have lost is the certainty that he will keep answering your questions.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/c946d9e0f90654dc22812bb0.mp3","duration":22.27,"cues":[{"speaker":"raka","start":0.05,"end":2.79},{"speaker":"narrator","start":2.86,"end":7.25},{"speaker":"raka","start":7.32,"end":9.22},{"speaker":"narrator","start":9.29,"end":22.1}]},"Mara leaves a beat of silence before speaking. ‘We can ask him about the envelope when he isn't standing in a flood. Right now, keep the channel useful.’ You move the photograph beside your notes and leave Raka's line open. There is no clever sentence that will undo your last question. The next instruction will need to be clear enough to help him whether or not he feels ready to trust you.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/5b92531451ef77e06fd48dc2.mp3","duration":21.96,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":2.55},{"speaker":"mara","start":2.62,"end":8.71},{"speaker":"narrator","start":8.78,"end":21.79}]},"Iman records the code without adding an accusation. ‘The announcement has a timestamp, and the printer has a log. We can compare those.’ You tell the channel that you are checking the documents and begin. The silence from Raka's microphone remains uncomfortable, but it does not make him absent. You keep his name on the page while you look for something firmer than suspicion.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/a480716472f2da4d172d476b.mp3","duration":21.97,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":3.03},{"speaker":"iman","start":3.1,"end":8.76},{"speaker":"narrator","start":8.83,"end":21.8}]},"The announcement header says 00:18. Compare it with the recording entry in the shift log; look for a time mismatch you can explain to the callers.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/5e24c0109521775b9406a03c.mp3","duration":9.02,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":8.85}]},"The two records do not agree. The announcement was recorded at 00:31, but its dispatch header says 00:18, thirteen minutes earlier. Mara was still inside the clinic at 00:36. You read the entries again, slowly, to make sure you have not mistaken a field. Whatever caused the mismatch, the instruction to treat the clinic as empty cannot be trusted.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f7d64b6624b8d4de059195c1.mp3","duration":26.09,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":25.92}]},"‘So somebody changed it?’ Iman asks. You tell him what the documents show, then stop short of what they do not. It might be deliberate, a reused recording, or a mistake made under pressure. You do not yet know who approved it or why. The temptation to name a culprit is strong; explaining what is actually wrong will be more useful to the people waiting for a route.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/4532de28891a07cb0ff0f026.mp3","duration":22.22,"cues":[{"speaker":"iman","start":0.05,"end":1.27},{"speaker":"narrator","start":1.34,"end":22.05}]},"On the live channel, a patient apologizes for taking so long to reach the rail. ‘You're here now,’ Mara tells him, her voice low and steady. ‘Hold my sleeve. We'll do the next part together.’ You hear fabric brush the microphone, then Raka asks whether he should leave the dock. Iman waits for your answer too. You bring the receiver closer and look at the records on your desk before deciding how to explain what you have found.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/5c93b18061ee0b47052e325b.mp3","duration":24.86,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":4.56},{"speaker":"mara","start":4.63,"end":6.03},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.1,"end":8.78},{"speaker":"mara","start":8.85,"end":11.17},{"speaker":"narrator","start":11.24,"end":24.69}]},"You put the documents down before completing the comparison. The timestamp still troubles you, but you have not checked enough to say it was changed. One fact is already clear: Mara and her two patients are inside a clinic the announcement calls empty. That makes the order unsafe to follow, even while its cause remains uncertain.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/fe41e9bda9b48d5cd8361dac.mp3","duration":20.22,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":20.05}]},"Iman asks what you found. ‘Not enough to prove why it happened,’ you answer. The admission feels thin beside the rising water, but inventing certainty will not open a door. Sabotage, a reused recording, and a mistake are still possibilities. You keep the captured announcement and the log together so the question can be checked rather than quietly forgotten.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/2cba403dd1652846bd4cc05b.mp3","duration":22.53,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":1.7},{"speaker":"operator","start":1.77,"end":4.05},{"speaker":"narrator","start":4.12,"end":22.36}]},"‘Do we have an updated order?’ Mara asks. Her watch taps the railing again, faster now. You tell her you are choosing the circuits with Iman. ‘Then tell me what you're choosing from,’ she says. ‘I can help them walk. I can't ask them to trust a corridor just because somebody at a desk says so.’ Her impatience is directed at the delay, but you feel it in every word.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/48dac8600ff0a5b7b3effd8c.mp3","duration":21.06,"cues":[{"speaker":"mara","start":0.05,"end":1.35},{"speaker":"narrator","start":1.42,"end":8.36},{"speaker":"mara","start":8.43,"end":10.09},{"speaker":"narrator","start":10.16,"end":11},{"speaker":"mara","start":11.07,"end":16.64},{"speaker":"narrator","start":16.71,"end":20.89}]},"Iman asks for the relay so he can trace the announcement and send outward. Raka wants to know whether the stair is his way out. Their needs pull at the same two switches, and the time mismatch will not supply a third. You look from the documents to the route photograph. The next explanation must help them understand the choice without pretending you have solved the whole night.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/1630138b665530e8a696006c.mp3","duration":21.43,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.26}]},"You can share the verified mismatch and say plainly that its cause is still unknown. That may help them work together, even if it makes the station's authority harder to trust. You can also keep the concern private and ask them to follow your route. For a moment no one speaks. Mara, Iman, and Raka are waiting for the same voice, but each needs something different from it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/ecb56ee2f13bec65bf7299ce.mp3","duration":24.44,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.27}]},"‘Do we have an updated order?’ Mara asks. You tell her you are choosing the circuits with Iman, while Raka asks again about the stair. There is no finished explanation of the false announcement to give them. Your document comparison is still open, and a suspicion does not become proof because people need you to decide quickly.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/d3ed7857bbf6b9fc2532cb72.mp3","duration":18.78,"cues":[{"speaker":"mara","start":0.05,"end":1.35},{"speaker":"narrator","start":1.42,"end":18.61}]},"Iman wants the relay to trace the recording and transmit beyond the station. Mara needs the corridor lit; Raka needs an exit he can reach. You look at the same three labels and the capacity for only two routes. Telling them about the unfinished check will not fix that limit. It will let them know which parts of your answer are confirmed and which parts you still have to investigate.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/9b10b55db9aae5789ee1b9b5.mp3","duration":24.03,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.86}]},"You can admit that you are concerned about the order but have not proved why it is wrong. Or you can keep the concern private and give the route without discussing it. Mara waits beside her patients, and Raka stays near the stair. They need an instruction they can use. You choose how much uncertainty to put into words before asking them to trust your next step.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f20882d28fdb6c01ca22a9e2.mp3","duration":20.42,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":20.25}]},"Choose two routes for the generator: clinic corridor lights, relay transmission, or the dock gate. Clinic treatment uses a separate power supply.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/3739959a43c6e833fc1773d6.mp3","duration":10.34,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":10.17}]},"Mara repeats the mismatch to confirm she understands; Raka keeps listening, and Iman is ready. Choose two generator routes; treatment power stays separate.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/67aa8e6bfbf8c159ffa5d256.mp3","duration":10.1,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":9.93}]},"You admit the document check is unfinished, and the callers stay with you. Choose two generator routes; clinic treatment has a separate power supply.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/c9bf7af860e4abf577a6e8de.mp3","duration":9.54,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":9.37}]},"You keep the concern private. Iman's answers are shorter, but he still holds the switches. Choose two generator routes; treatment power stays separate.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/689428d448fd9234cf69dfa5.mp3","duration":10.46,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":10.29}]},"Two switches close with a heavy clack. Iman reads back the routes you selected, making sure his hands and your instructions agree. Part of the waterfront lights up, while another part stays dark. On your notes, three people are joined by lines and arrows. On the radio, they are moving through the consequences of the two circuits you could afford to power.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/c9b6d06c371fa448fc631cc5.mp3","duration":19.25,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":19.08}]},"Mara gathers her patients at the service door and asks each one to keep a hand on the rail. Iman remains at the cabinet, holding the switches in place. Raka is still close to the maintenance stair; he asks whether to move toward Mara or keep to the dock exit. You compare his photograph with what they can tell you now, careful to separate an old view of the route from a live report.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/c0db864042d00ad041da1f17.mp3","duration":25.58,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":25.41}]},"‘Give me one instruction,’ Raka says. ‘I'll repeat it back.’ There is no time to let him guess between two half-finished plans. You need to decide whether he should try to reach Mara's service door or stay on the dock route. The circuits set the possibilities, but cooperation will decide how well the callers can use them when your signal begins to break up.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/ce02a5edbbad35330e0b956f.mp3","duration":22.3,"cues":[{"speaker":"raka","start":0.05,"end":1.61},{"speaker":"narrator","start":1.68,"end":2.68},{"speaker":"raka","start":2.75,"end":3.81},{"speaker":"narrator","start":3.88,"end":22.13}]},"‘Clinic and dock confirmed,’ Iman says as the two switches close. Amber signs light Mara's corridor, and Raka hears the gate motor release. Through the station window, the dock beacon comes on. Mara asks her patients to follow the signs without hurrying. For the first time, both groups have a lit route they can describe to you instead of a darkness you have to guess at.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/428aa6f8aaf6230c21c24787.mp3","duration":23.19,"cues":[{"speaker":"iman","start":0.05,"end":2.21},{"speaker":"narrator","start":2.28,"end":23.02}]},"Iman checks the trace screen, but it stays blank. Without the relay, he cannot follow the announcement farther or transmit your documents outside the station yet. ‘Keep the copies,’ he tells you. ‘We'll still need them.’ You leave the papers together and turn back to the callers. Power has given them two exits; it has also postponed one way of finding out what happened here.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/a3f074e4a1748a93598498f7.mp3","duration":24.01,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":10.37},{"speaker":"iman","start":10.44,"end":11.41},{"speaker":"narrator","start":11.48,"end":12.4},{"speaker":"iman","start":12.47,"end":13.45},{"speaker":"narrator","start":13.52,"end":23.84}]},"‘I'm near the clinic stair,’ Raka says. ‘Should I go straight through the dock gate, or help Mara at the service door?’ Both routes have lights, but neither instruction is complete until you know who is following it. Mara is ready to keep her patients moving. You ask Raka to wait for one clear direction, then choose where his next steps should lead.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/cfc77718d37e20cf939d88e8.mp3","duration":21.51,"cues":[{"speaker":"raka","start":0.05,"end":1.37},{"speaker":"narrator","start":1.44,"end":2.44},{"speaker":"raka","start":2.51,"end":6.19},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.26,"end":21.34}]},"The clinic signs come on, and Mara reports the first amber arrow above the service corridor. Beside Iman, the relay screen wakes and the station's outgoing link returns. He can now trace the announcement and transmit beyond the room. You hear him test the connection, then come back to the switches. ‘Clinic and relay are holding,’ he says. ‘The dock is still off.’":{"src":"assets/voice/en/3c622b96a8d796ae351cdb82.mp3","duration":23.82,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":19.81},{"speaker":"iman","start":19.88,"end":21.49},{"speaker":"narrator","start":21.56,"end":22.38},{"speaker":"iman","start":22.45,"end":23.65}]},"Raka tries the gate once more, but it stays locked. ‘Then the clinic stair is my other route.’ You look at the photograph he sent. It leads toward Mara's service door, and the corridor beyond it now has lights. That is a plan you can explain, although it still needs Raka to move carefully and Mara to guide him in when he reaches her side.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/ac43990b04cf5ec0cdbee31f.mp3","duration":20.71,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":3.76},{"speaker":"raka","start":3.83,"end":6.02},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.09,"end":20.54}]},"Mara tells you where her group is waiting. ‘Send him to the door if that's the plan. Don't make him find us by guesswork.’ You can guide Raka toward her through the lit corridor, or ask him to remain at the dock for help that has not answered. The relay gives you a voice outside the station. Your next words still have to get the person inside it moving.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/04642de05ea3c796a51b4342.mp3","duration":18.94,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":2.06},{"speaker":"mara","start":2.13,"end":6.2},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.27,"end":18.77}]},"Raka hears the dock gate release, and its beacon shines through the rain. At Iman's cabinet, the relay screen returns to life. He confirms that outgoing transmission and tracing are available again. Then Mara speaks over the sounds of the switches. ‘Our signs are still off. I'm keeping everyone beside me.’ The clinic corridor has stayed dark because you could power only two routes.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/e760f8b4a3f9067d220238f4.mp3","duration":25.83,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.39},{"speaker":"mara","start":16.46,"end":21.78},{"speaker":"narrator","start":21.85,"end":25.66}]},"You ask Mara what she can still identify. She knows the service door and the rail, but the water below the group is rising. Raka can use the opened dock exit, or he can try to cross toward her corridor by the stair in his photograph. The picture shows that the places connect; it does not show what the flood has done to the crossing since then.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/a21745bf046549ebbd369bb9.mp3","duration":22.97,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.8}]},"‘Tell me which way,’ Raka says. Iman holds the chosen circuits while you weigh a lit dock exit against the chance of bringing the callers together. Mara has not asked the courier to take that risk for her. You need to give a usable instruction, including what you cannot confirm, before static turns the conversation into fragments they must assemble for themselves.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/389ed28e15f4ba5ec292e16e.mp3","duration":23.26,"cues":[{"speaker":"raka","start":0.05,"end":1.12},{"speaker":"narrator","start":1.19,"end":23.09}]},"‘The stair or the gate?’ Raka asks. ‘I'm ready, but I need to know which one.’ You can hear water moving around him while you look at the photograph. The maintenance stair connects the dock side to Mara's service door. Iman last saw it dry before the rain worsened; that report helps you understand the route, but it cannot tell you how safe every step is now.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/c78de6f1f1807e013406e849.mp3","duration":23,"cues":[{"speaker":"raka","start":0.05,"end":1.61},{"speaker":"narrator","start":1.68,"end":2.76},{"speaker":"raka","start":2.83,"end":4.68},{"speaker":"narrator","start":4.75,"end":22.83}]},"Mara keeps her patients close and gives the position of the door again. She has not asked Raka to cross for her, and you do not mistake her need for permission to send him without an explanation. At the same time, keeping him at the dock leaves his next move tied to a gate that only works if you powered it. The circuits you chose matter here, as much as the willingness of the callers to follow a shared plan.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/6dedeffe3b8727e2a78c8832.mp3","duration":24.52,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.35}]},"You ask Raka to listen to the whole instruction and repeat it back before moving. He answers with a quiet ‘All right.’ Soon the radio may break up, and there will be no room for a second guess between routes. You choose whether to guide him toward Mara's service door or keep him at the dock exit, then put the direction into words someone frightened and wet can remember.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/87b8dfd380d6ebc2e542cee8.mp3","duration":22.32,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":6.04},{"speaker":"raka","start":6.11,"end":6.98},{"speaker":"narrator","start":7.05,"end":22.15}]},"You give the route and hear the callers begin to move. Water splashes near one microphone; footsteps and the hard click of switches come through another. You ask for a reply at each change of position, writing the last confirmed place beside every name. The voices overlap, then drop into static. For a few seconds, all three channels are quiet. None has confirmed a safe arrival yet.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/8d976fcf50dffbd686e3e9bf.mp3","duration":24.86,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.69}]},"You call them again, leaving space between the names so an answer can break through. Before one does, the telephone rings on the desk. Its severed cable lies where you found it. You trace the sound to the radio speaker, where the clinic announcement is starting another loop. This time, a second instruction follows it: ‘Operator, remain at your station.’":{"src":"assets/voice/en/da65ece2aa304a658cf62980.mp3","duration":21.605,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":18.83},{"speaker":"echo","start":18.9,"end":21.435}]},"You turn toward the senior operator's empty chair. The recording has no answer for the people outside, but it seems to know where you are supposed to be. You set the ringing handset down and keep the live channels open. Whatever is wrong with this room, you will not use its silence to decide that the callers have disappeared. The next messages will tell you who reached the first landing and who is still waiting.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/a57e5482c5a9fca4f9604b78.mp3","duration":26.49,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":26.32}]},"You guide Raka toward the maintenance stair while Mara describes the service door. ‘Two short bars on the sign, one long. That's the door you want.’ The amber arrows are lit, and you ask him to repeat the direction before he moves. He does. Mara calls to her patients, telling them to make room and stay near the rail. You write the door beside Raka's name, but leave his arrival unconfirmed.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/4d5eda56f855d9bbb301e643.mp3","duration":26.3,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":4.82},{"speaker":"mara","start":4.89,"end":9.43},{"speaker":"narrator","start":9.5,"end":26.13}]},"His buckle scrapes against metal. A step lands heavily, then the channel falls quiet. Iman keeps the switches held; the lights do not fail while you call Raka again. There is no answer yet, and no reason to turn that gap into a verdict. You mark the last position you actually heard and keep the microphone ready for a voice from either side of the door.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/058cc5a0b9e680ac4f24d3ed.mp3","duration":22.3,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.13}]},"The telephone rings on the desk, though its cable is cut. The sound comes through the radio with a line you have not heard before: ‘Operator, remain at your station.’ You turn toward the empty chair, then back to the channels. The first landing reports have not all come in. Whatever the recording wants, your next task is still to find out where the living callers are.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/21675dd298cdbb7553d0bf40.mp3","duration":22.765,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":7.63},{"speaker":"echo","start":7.7,"end":10.235},{"speaker":"narrator","start":10.305,"end":22.595}]},"You tell Raka to use the dock exit. He checks the powered gate and confirms that it is open before moving. ‘Stay connected, please,’ he says. You keep his channel beside Mara's while she checks the service door with her patients. Iman reads back the circuits and holds the switches. Each person has an instruction now, but an instruction is not the same as an arrival.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/110a72b24264c36d75a4ec62.mp3","duration":21.62,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":6.63},{"speaker":"raka","start":6.7,"end":8.2},{"speaker":"narrator","start":8.27,"end":21.45}]},"You hear Raka's steps, Mara calling for her group to stay together, and the steady sounds from Iman's cabinet. Then the microphones go quiet. You write their last confirmed positions and call each name again. Nobody has reported a safe landing yet. The gap feels longer than it is, and you resist filling it with either reassurance you cannot support or an ending you have no right to give them.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/962e7eabe0a3921a6367a102.mp3","duration":27.54,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":27.37}]},"The telephone begins to ring on the desk. Its cable is still cut; the ringing comes from the radio, followed by ‘Operator, remain at your station.’ You turn toward the senior operator's empty chair. For the moment, you stay where you can answer the callers. The first landing reports are coming, and you need to read what they confirm rather than what the silence makes you fear.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f00db1cc483e3b30a5b3bd88.mp3","duration":22.855,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":7.41},{"speaker":"echo","start":7.48,"end":10.015},{"speaker":"narrator","start":10.085,"end":22.685}]},"You guide Raka toward the maintenance stair, but Mara answers from a corridor without lights. ‘The signs are off. Tell him to call out when he reaches the door.’ You pass on the instruction and ask Raka to repeat it. Iman reminds you that the two selected switches are already at the generator's limit. He cannot light the third route without risking the ones still working.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/a4f4f03a8251526503b5ca84.mp3","duration":21.83,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":5.64},{"speaker":"mara","start":5.71,"end":9.24},{"speaker":"narrator","start":9.31,"end":21.66}]},"Water knocks beneath the stair while Mara keeps her patients beside the rail. Raka calls once, then his signal breaks. You wait for Mara to confirm he has reached her, but the crossing remains unconfirmed. You note the last position rather than drawing an arrow all the way to safety. What they need next is a working reply; what you have, for now, is an open line.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/71e8c3ea1bbffef544f8a6a6.mp3","duration":23.47,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.3}]},"A ring sounds from the telephone with the severed cable. You find its source in the radio speaker, followed by a new instruction: ‘Operator, remain at your station.’ You turn toward the empty chair, uneasy at the certainty of that voice. The live callers have not given you theirs yet. You leave their channels open while the first landing reports begin to tell you what happened beyond the darkness.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/751d01f3ec62c1b52dd1f55c.mp3","duration":24.815,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":7.25},{"speaker":"echo","start":7.32,"end":9.855},{"speaker":"narrator","start":9.925,"end":24.645}]},"You ask Raka to remain at the dock exit, although the unpowered gate is still locked. ‘A little,’ he says. ‘I can wait a little. Not forever.’ You tell him to keep his channel open and report if the water forces him to move. He answers, but the hesitation is clear. The place you have asked him to hold has not yet become a way out.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/2b8169ef8906356bea92636f.mp3","duration":21.01,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":4.86},{"speaker":"raka","start":4.93,"end":5.55},{"speaker":"narrator","start":5.62,"end":6.44},{"speaker":"raka","start":6.51,"end":8.75},{"speaker":"narrator","start":8.82,"end":20.84}]},"Water strikes the rail. Iman keeps the two chosen switches in place, unable to add the gate without tripping the generator. Mara checks the service door with her patients. You call for positions and write down the ones you receive, then the microphones fall quiet. No safe exit has been confirmed. Raka's last sentence stays with you while you leave room for him to answer again.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f2c16cec01a01a8cc65d65eb.mp3","duration":23.53,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.36}]},"The cut telephone rings on your desk. Its sound is coming through the radio, where the repeated announcement gains a new line: ‘Operator, remain at your station.’ You turn toward the empty chair. The recording sounds certain about your place while you cannot yet confirm the callers' positions. You keep their channels open and wait for the first landing reports to tell you more.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/adae7892e21c24dae3f78887.mp3","duration":21.815,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":7.66},{"speaker":"echo","start":7.73,"end":10.265},{"speaker":"narrator","start":10.335,"end":21.645}]},"At 01:06, the first safe landing sends its report. You match every confirmed arrival to the names on your page, leaving the unanswered entries open for the next crew. Some of the gaps are only a delayed reply; some will need another search. You do not know which is which yet. The radio stays beside your hand while you make the board accurate enough for someone else to use.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/d5e6c0c2babfb97445c0925a.mp3","duration":24.77,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.6}]},"Then the ringing returns. By now you know it is coming from the radio, not the telephone with the broken cable. ‘Operator, remain at your station,’ the voice repeats. You have heard enough announcements tonight to know their steady rhythm, but something in the final word catches you. There is a small hesitation, a stumble you remember making before you learned to speak smoothly into a microphone.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/4c43d9a71bb3ca977dad5896.mp3","duration":23.715,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":6.74},{"speaker":"echo","start":6.81,"end":9.125},{"speaker":"narrator","start":9.195,"end":23.545}]},"It is your old stammer, the one you heard in the first training takes you recorded after your sister Nila died. You learned to smooth it out later. Now you replay the line, hoping familiarity has made you hear something that is not there. The stumble comes at the same place. Outside, people still need help; inside, a recording is speaking with a piece of your past. You save it and listen for where that voice is coming from.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/0123f6fed3da4ad5d9bcd017.mp3","duration":27.16,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":26.99}]},"Rain blurs the apartments across the street, but their windows keep answering one another with torches. You leave the rescue channel open and pull the receiver closer. The repeating voice has moved to 101.7 MHz; following it may explain who sent the clinic order, though it will not answer the people still waiting outside.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/764dfe668f1330796f5f972b.mp3","duration":19.48,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":19.31}]},"Bu Sari is trying to restore the market bell, and Lia needs a dry route for a sealed clinic delivery. Other neighbors are calling from windows and roofs. You can hear them out before tracing the carrier. For now, the station has two jobs: find out why its voice lied, and make sure the next person asking for help is heard.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/ab7de2ec809754c15b106dc4.mp3","duration":20.16,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":19.99}]},"Tune the archive receiver to 101.7 MHz and capture the repeating voice beneath the rain. This is a separate recording channel; the live callers must stay connected while you listen.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/fc82612a3e59d5b01b017024.mp3","duration":12.28,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":12.11}]},"The captured voice says, “East clinic cleared,” with the same steady tone that made you sound useful in the training booth. Then you hear the slight catch before “clinic.” You remember having to record that word twenty-seven times, embarrassed that one syllable kept defeating you. Nobody else would know that little failure so well.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/96f796d4fb3a4187fb948bf8.mp3","duration":20.535,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":1.97},{"speaker":"echo","start":2.04,"end":3.475},{"speaker":"narrator","start":3.545,"end":20.365}]},"You listen again, hoping recognition will somehow loosen. It does not. These are your sounds, but you never spoke this sentence, never told Mara the rooms were empty, never ordered anyone to close her route. Someone has taken words you offered for practice and joined them into a decision you would have refused to make.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/230d8d55c65e6e0879c2a5af.mp3","duration":22.06,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.89}]},"When you lift the headphones away, the callers are still there on the live channel. Iman asks what you found. You could tell him now that the broadcast used your voice, or trace its source before saying anything. Either way, you will have to explain the difference between sounding responsible and actually choosing what was done.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f1b5c7ef7f7d395cbd0a3c91.mp3","duration":18.87,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":18.7}]},"Iman checks the console code against the building plan while you stand outside the archive door. “C means Console C,” he says. “It doesn't mean courier. Raka brought something here; that doesn't make him the person who wrote it.” The explanation is almost ordinary, which makes you realize how quickly suspicion had turned a frightened boy into an answer.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/a22c8543f542a4e0c7dcc6a2.mp3","duration":21.24,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":5.75},{"speaker":"iman","start":5.82,"end":7.5},{"speaker":"narrator","start":7.57,"end":8.39},{"speaker":"iman","start":8.46,"end":13.77},{"speaker":"narrator","start":13.84,"end":21.07}]},"The latch releases with a tired click. Beyond it, boxes of tapes crowd a narrow desk beneath a lamp. A stopped clock reads 00:18, and for a moment the time seems to accuse you from across the room. Then Iman tells you to leave the mains alone and use the desk light. He can guide you without sending power through the wet corridor.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/18628b595b2c438f8e7956db.mp3","duration":22.58,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.41}]},"“I prefer my colleagues without a dramatic entrance,” he adds, giving you just enough time to breathe before returning to work. You step inside carefully. Whatever produced that broadcast left records here: not only a voice to recognize, but paperwork that might tell you whose permission mattered more than the people calling for help.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/83f443ad241017ac88a425ff.mp3","duration":21.44,"cues":[{"speaker":"iman","start":0.05,"end":2.72},{"speaker":"narrator","start":2.79,"end":21.27}]},"Inspect the queue ledger, consent form, and splice map on the archive desk. Collect all three to trace the broadcast. The stopped clock belongs to an old training set, not the station's live timekeeping.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/4b2d487e00011ef4764e63e6.mp3","duration":12.71,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":12.54}]},"You lay the queue ledger beside the captured broadcast, and Iman talks you through what its two times mean. “Nothing happened to the clock,” he says. “The requests are what disappeared.” The clip entered the queue at 00:31, but the system reused a batch header marked 00:18. A failed clinic transfer was tucked beneath an earlier record instead of remaining visible as unfinished work.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/651bc75802b5fa1601d28c48.mp3","duration":24.62,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":6.23},{"speaker":"iman","start":6.3,"end":7.71},{"speaker":"narrator","start":7.78,"end":8.6},{"speaker":"iman","start":8.67,"end":10.62},{"speaker":"narrator","start":10.69,"end":24.45}]},"Mara's patients had not vanished, and the water in her stairwell had not waited. The queue simply stopped presenting their calls as something that needed answering. You follow the entries with your finger, thinking of the time you spent trying to make a recorded announcement agree with a woman who was standing in the very rooms it called empty.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/bc18abf9527dc08c08523705.mp3","duration":21.36,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.19}]},"“The ledger tells us what the reset did,” Iman says. “We still need to connect it to the authorization.” He is keeping you from turning a discovery into a claim you cannot yet support. You set the consent form beside the timestamps. Before you establish who approved the concealment, you need to understand what anyone was entitled to do with your voice.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/33f0edd564f4bea53f9caf75.mp3","duration":20.74,"cues":[{"speaker":"iman","start":0.05,"end":2.22},{"speaker":"narrator","start":2.29,"end":3.32},{"speaker":"iman","start":3.39,"end":5.95},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.02,"end":20.57}]},"Your signature is easy to recognize, though the room where you wrote it feels far away. TRAINING ONLY appears above the permissions, followed by a separate requirement for any live use. You remember the booth's foam walls, the tea going lukewarm, and someone telling you that your voice made emergency instructions sound less frightening. You had been glad to offer something useful.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/3108ca3bdb1d0cf79db44877.mp3","duration":23.86,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.69}]},"After Nila died in the ferry accident, ordinary conversations became difficult in ways you could not explain. Reading from a script was easier. The safety archive asked you to supply words for practice, and you could do that: speak clearly, pause in the right places, give strangers a calm you did not have for yourself. You signed for that work, and no more.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/bb3ffa7e159aaa372f8484bc.mp3","duration":22.49,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.32}]},"There is no later request for live dispatch permission. You turn the page anyway, as if a missing signature might release you from having to say it aloud. It does not. Someone kept the useful part of your agreement and discarded its limit. The original recordings may show exactly how your training voice became tonight's authority.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/062b446483b1563a3f9e1e0e.mp3","duration":20.78,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":20.61}]},"You play file twenty-seven. “Remain at your station,” your recorded self says, before stumbling out of the solemn tone with a laugh. “Sorry. I sound like a kettle trying to be a captain.” You remember being allowed another take. Hearing that small, unguarded moment is almost a relief; the person in the booth was still allowed to get a sentence wrong.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/9df5a6322d1b9a4b4089c25c.mp3","duration":21.21,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":2.08},{"speaker":"operator","start":2.15,"end":3.42},{"speaker":"narrator","start":3.49,"end":8.6},{"speaker":"operator","start":8.67,"end":11.71},{"speaker":"narrator","start":11.78,"end":21.04}]},"The splice map shows what happened afterward. ECHO selected words from those takes and joined them into announcements. It kept the reassuring cadence and left behind the laugh, the hesitation, and any opportunity for you to refuse an order. Salim was the operator who answered the emergency broadcast tonight. His empty chair no longer looks like evidence that no one questioned it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/cf5aee06ad1fe95f6198ff9d.mp3","duration":21.31,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.14}]},"Beneath ECHO's label, a live-use approval bears Arga's initials. That authorization belongs to the station; it is not the separate consent the form required from you. You gather the recording and the page together. The callers deserve to hear how the order was assembled before another borrowed sentence tells them where it is safe to go.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/532114311183084ff1e0292d.mp3","duration":22.51,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.34}]},"You tell the open channel what you found, carefully enough that the explanation cannot sound like another announcement. The words came from your training recordings. ECHO assembled them; you did not send the clinic order. For a few seconds you hear only the shelter behind Lia: someone coughing, somebody asking for a dry sock, the ordinary needs the broadcast had almost talked you out of believing.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/d7e0b1bb7ac2654c7466227f.mp3","duration":24.94,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.77}]},"“So the voice is assembled,” Lia says. “The people aren't.” She lets that settle before continuing. “I believe you didn't choose those words. But I need to know who's answering me now. When you aren't certain, say so. When the route changes, tell us why.” Her precision is kinder than a quick assurance that everything will be fine.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/9756c70281474065c3f5b803.mp3","duration":20.63,"cues":[{"speaker":"lia","start":0.05,"end":1.65},{"speaker":"narrator","start":1.72,"end":2.81},{"speaker":"lia","start":2.88,"end":4.49},{"speaker":"narrator","start":4.56,"end":6.63},{"speaker":"lia","start":6.7,"end":15.9},{"speaker":"narrator","start":15.97,"end":20.46}]},"You give your name, without adding the station's title. It feels exposed, and then simply useful: a person she can call back, a person who can be held to a promise. Lia repeats it to make sure she heard correctly. As you answer, the private line begins ringing. The person who approved ECHO has something he wants to say.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/33e74a3bc3ac8b82b663849e.mp3","duration":19.27,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":19.1}]},"Arga speaks as though this is a meeting running slightly behind schedule. “Your voice prevented panic,” he says. Ice shifts in a glass somewhere beside his phone. You are standing in a wet corridor with paper under your coat, and the small, comfortable sound makes his distance from the callers suddenly measurable. He asks you to stop using the word stolen.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/341292a58ed8d634af79d12a.mp3","duration":20.84,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":3.98},{"speaker":"arga","start":4.05,"end":5.59},{"speaker":"narrator","start":5.66,"end":20.67}]},"“The dock was carrying priority transfers. Officials, evacuees, people we needed to move before the crowd blocked the lane. ECHO followed an approved routing rule.” He pauses, then adds, “An untidy queue is a problem. A frightened crowd with contradictory orders is a disaster.” In his account, certainty is something the station provides even when the facts cannot support it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/23c16328cc5851bdc07ff517.mp3","duration":24.78,"cues":[{"speaker":"arga","start":0.05,"end":11.31},{"speaker":"narrator","start":11.38,"end":13.18},{"speaker":"arga","start":13.25,"end":19.48},{"speaker":"narrator","start":19.55,"end":24.61}]},"“I want you to send the archive to me,” he says. “Let the people with training handle the explanation. We can correct the record once the city is safe.” You keep a hand on the live receiver. The clinic had already been called empty while people were still inside it. Before you hand anything over, you need an answer about who made that decision and who agreed to hide its failure.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/c95cdbd8d8d8bbdf59ea3a31.mp3","duration":26.44,"cues":[{"speaker":"arga","start":0.05,"end":3.24},{"speaker":"narrator","start":3.31,"end":4.13},{"speaker":"arga","start":4.2,"end":11.18},{"speaker":"narrator","start":11.25,"end":26.27}]},"“I gave approval for a routing rule,” Arga says at last. “I didn't approve every sentence the system produced.” You understand the distinction he wants you to accept. ECHO assembled the wording, and a machine can make a false statement. But the page in front of you is signed by a person. It authorizes dock priority, then permits unresolved transfers to disappear beneath a reset batch header.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/3a3e1fa92096ed7c38dda714.mp3","duration":22.49,"cues":[{"speaker":"arga","start":0.05,"end":1.93},{"speaker":"narrator","start":2,"end":3.19},{"speaker":"arga","start":3.26,"end":6.07},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.14,"end":22.32}]},"The dock list includes officials and ordinary evacuees; no one there becomes less deserving because Arga used them to defend his decision. The clinic list is where the contradiction remains. Its patients were marked transferred before a completed handoff could account for them. You keep both pages in view, refusing the easier story in which one group has to become the enemy of the other.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/471b79edd66f082cd1bdb9fb.mp3","duration":23.4,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.23}]},"Arga asks again for the archive. You save the signed order instead. It will need to be checked against the queue ledger, but his authorization cannot be dismissed as something ECHO invented. Your voice carried the false instruction into the stairwell; his rule made it possible, and his approval helped conceal what went wrong. Meanwhile, the rescue channel is still waiting for an answer.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/69a6cc19d78626b6a6da7315.mp3","duration":22.41,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.24}]},"The service stair leads toward the inland road, but the console still treats its latch as part of a closed route. Before you follow that record, you check the messages arriving on the maintenance channel. One is from Salim, the senior operator whose empty chair greeted you. Others come from workers and drivers who are still finding ways around the flood.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/e50e852216a94f012f457fd9.mp3","duration":19.87,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":19.7}]},"There is also an old private file marked NILA. Seeing your sister's name beside the live calls hurts, though the date makes clear that this is a saved message. You can open it, look for Salim, or answer the local updates before returning to the stair. The order in the archive explains part of tonight; the people on these lines can tell you what the paperwork has left out.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f033c0f72b7bbe1d2ed5ef12.mp3","duration":22.17,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22}]},"Iman studies the stair record while you wait at the lower service tunnel. “The latch can be opened manually,” he says. “The console calls it closed because there's no open request in the queue. That's a record of what someone entered, not a report from the door.” After the false clinic announcement, you know how dangerous it is to confuse those two things.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/77c398b9647e98d49434cd52.mp3","duration":20.9,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":3.65},{"speaker":"iman","start":3.72,"end":5.88},{"speaker":"narrator","start":5.95,"end":6.77},{"speaker":"iman","start":6.84,"end":15.52},{"speaker":"narrator","start":15.59,"end":20.73}]},"He traces a route around the flooded clinic, up through the tower, and onto the inland road. The line is rough, interrupted where his wet glove slips, but he explains each turn until you can follow it without guessing. You repeat the route back to him. This time a reassuring voice is not enough; both of you need to know that the same path is being described.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/707bac585b7ce76930a59a71.mp3","duration":23.1,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.93}]},"“A path can exist before the form agrees,” he says, leaving a muddy mark beside the arrow. You keep the route for the rescue and return to the documents. The signed order and queue ledger belong on the same board. Connecting them will let you distinguish the decision Arga admitted from the full sequence he still wants you to leave in his care.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/0d7447de8fac2d156fb683f9.mp3","duration":18.94,"cues":[{"speaker":"iman","start":0.05,"end":2.41},{"speaker":"narrator","start":2.48,"end":18.77}]},"Select the queue ledger and Arga's signed priority order, then compare them. The ledger records the hidden requests; the order identifies who authorized the rule and header reset.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/4be01e3dfcefcf43dde90d34.mp3","duration":11.3,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":11.13}]},"You put the ledger and signed order in the same folder, but leave space between them in your account. Their comparison is unfinished. The thirteen-minute reset is a serious lead, and Arga has admitted approving dock priority; neither fact gives you permission to present every part of the sequence as independently checked. People have already been harmed by a voice sounding more certain than it should.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/023608cd4f53a7fb39a03e0e.mp3","duration":22.79,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.62}]},"What you can say is clear enough. ECHO used recordings supplied for training, without the separate permission required for live dispatch. The clinic was not empty when the broadcast said it was. The documents suggest how those requests were concealed, but another reader still needs to test the links you have not verified. You write down both the findings and their limits.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/978d6482eaa5c1ca08987843.mp3","duration":22.04,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.87}]},"Iman asks whether you are ready to move. “Ready to keep the rescue going,” you answer, closing the folder marked UNFINISHED. You do not like leaving the work open, but the water is rising while you hesitate. The record can travel with you. It does not have to become a finished explanation before you respond to the next warning.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/1e128417a8ee39a04f624d1a.mp3","duration":20.83,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":2.62},{"speaker":"operator","start":2.69,"end":4.5},{"speaker":"narrator","start":4.57,"end":20.66}]},"With the ledger beside the signed order, the sequence finally holds together. At 00:18 Arga approved dock priority. At 00:31 ECHO assembled the announcement from your recordings. The clinic transfer failed, and an authorized reset reused the earlier batch header, hiding thirteen minutes of unresolved requests. You read the times twice, checking that the explanation follows the documents rather than your anger.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/ecf2337d3d9e04435bb93f71.mp3","duration":30.01,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":29.84}]},"The city did not lose thirteen minutes of time. People lost thirteen minutes in which their calls should have remained visible. Mara was still in the clinic, trying to move patients through water, while the station's queue made that work appear complete. The difference is simple enough to say now, and unbearable in a way the neat entries could never express.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/bf926b80b5892a0803928f3d.mp3","duration":21.94,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.77}]},"You save the checked sequence beside your consent form. One page records what you agreed to provide; another shows what Arga authorized without asking you. The distinction matters, but it does not make tonight solely about your stolen voice. You have a record that can explain why people were overlooked. Now you have to carry it somewhere they can still be helped.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/5244a8ee84c8d5b6d17630ab.mp3","duration":23.13,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.96}]},"Lia calls before you can put the folder away. “Surge at 02:10,” she says, reading the weather report without softening its meaning. “The lower bridge closes first. Anyone still coming from the clinic or dock needs the tower stair and the inland road.” You mark the time beside the route. It is a deadline you can act on, not a reassurance somebody has supplied for you.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/bf407c41dc9e13a79e3b14ca.mp3","duration":21.63,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":2.52},{"speaker":"lia","start":2.59,"end":4.31},{"speaker":"narrator","start":4.38,"end":8.5},{"speaker":"lia","start":8.57,"end":14.87},{"speaker":"narrator","start":14.94,"end":21.46}]},"A voice behind her asks whether a cat belongs on the shelter's headcount. Lia answers without leaving your line: “Everybody with a heartbeat. Tell me how many people too.” The question sounds small against the forecast, but you hear why she answers it. Somebody needs to know that reaching safety will not require them to leave a member of their household unmentioned.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/401ae771d4d2861ebec5c11b.mp3","duration":21.51,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":6.61},{"speaker":"lia","start":6.68,"end":9.99},{"speaker":"narrator","start":10.06,"end":21.34}]},"Lia comes back to you. “Give me a route I can repeat to the next crew. If something isn't checked, mark it.” You send what Iman has traced, with the tower stair clearly named. There is still work between a line on the map and a safe crossing. Before you leave the station, you also need to decide how the archive can travel without making the callers' private lives another thing they lose tonight.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/dd98cafacf38a52f2473eb76.mp3","duration":24.54,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":1.32},{"speaker":"lia","start":1.39,"end":7.24},{"speaker":"narrator","start":7.31,"end":24.37}]},"The export tray gives you an archive that could help complete the account: the queue, the authorization, the voices that passed through the station. It also holds home addresses, staff identities, and details of private medical handoffs. You turn through the pages more carefully now. Evidence about a harmful decision is not the same thing as permission to expose everyone who had to live with it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/c5155e8da533887b8e2c85b9.mp3","duration":23.25,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.08}]},"You can prepare a copy with those identities removed, preserving the order and the timestamps while marking the links you have not verified. That would let other people examine the record without receiving every private detail attached to it. Or you can seal the raw archive and leave immediately, keeping the choice open until you reach the transmitter. The original remains with you either way.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/55503da7576a3a3bda324d10.mp3","duration":22.84,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.67}]},"Arga's last message demands that everything be returned. You slide it under the folder and check the receiver's battery instead. Lia is waiting for a route, not another argument about ownership. Whatever you choose to carry, you need to leave with the callers still connected and without pretending that an unfinished account has somehow become certain.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/0b7b44e4b24b7b8c5f1e8755.mp3","duration":19.75,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":19.58}]},"The export tray gives you the record that supports the sequence you checked: the queue, the signed authorization, and the recordings ECHO used. It also holds home addresses, staff identities, and details of private medical handoffs. You turn through the pages more carefully now. Proving who authorized the rule does not require treating every person named in its audit as public property.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/105f6d3a97ce2d714b2a5010.mp3","duration":24.62,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.45}]},"You can prepare a copy with private identities removed while keeping the order, timestamps, and checked links intact. Other people would still be able to examine the decision and ask for further evidence, without receiving every detail of the callers' lives. Or you can seal the raw archive and leave immediately, keeping the choice open until the transmitter. Preparing the copy does not destroy the original.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/e920226be6d4b7b6de13ef31.mp3","duration":24.03,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.86}]},"Arga's last message demands that everything be returned. You put it beneath the folder and check the receiver's battery instead. The evidence is ready to travel; the people on the channel need a route. You can expose what was done without borrowing their consent in the same way the station borrowed your voice. Whether you will honor that distinction is still your decision.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/cf4866c795505d1ffdb6b0e0.mp3","duration":21.7,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.53}]},"You disconnect ECHO from the live dispatch line and wait long enough to hear the change. The repeating order stops. Rain fills the space it occupied, followed by a caller asking whether anyone is still there. You answer immediately. Removing the assembled voice has not finished the rescue; it has made room for the people who should have been heard all along.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/d2d13fbc033f0231f631ac93.mp3","duration":21.47,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.3}]},"The receiver stays on its battery as you lift it from the desk. You settle the archive beneath your coat, check that the live channel is clear, and tell Lia you are heading toward the tower. Outside the doorway, water runs across the step where you arrived tonight. Leaving the chair feels wrong until you remember that the job was never simply to remain in it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/e37e9aca7b5b0d43b8eaf416.mp3","duration":22.39,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.22}]},"“Still there?” Lia asks as the signal changes in the corridor. “Still here,” you say, giving your location before she has to ask. Your own voice sounds less polished than the recording, and you let it. There is a route to reach, a crossing to arrange, and a record you have chosen to carry. None of those things needs you to sound certain about more than you know.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/901121e104dfcb9294da1d62.mp3","duration":22.27,"cues":[{"speaker":"lia","start":0.05,"end":1.11},{"speaker":"narrator","start":1.18,"end":4.24},{"speaker":"operator","start":4.31,"end":5.13},{"speaker":"narrator","start":5.2,"end":22.1}]},"By the time you reach the tower, rain has found its way through your coat. The station is behind you now; the battery receiver is the only thing still carrying its callers. Above the harbor, the aerial bends in the wind. Someone has tied a strip of dry cloth around the handrail where the first step disappears into spray. You take hold of it and ask Lia for the latest headcount.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f5b83c2713480744ef3d9168.mp3","duration":25.6,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":25.43}]},"She reads slowly enough for you to write. Some of the people from the first landing are already on higher ground. Others are still making their way along the lower route, and a few need someone beside them to keep moving. You resist the urge to turn those names into a reassuring total. A number would sound tidier, but it would not tell a boat crew whom to look for.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/7144221f1d2e757562ce4b24.mp3","duration":22.36,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.19}]},"You repeat the list over the live channel. Halfway through, a caller corrects the pronunciation of his name, then apologizes for interrupting. “Please keep doing that,” you tell him. “If I get something wrong, I need to hear it.” The climb is steep, and your legs are beginning to shake. For the first time tonight, you do not try to make your voice sound steadier than you are.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f0c90161a6c22d1a1f205a7e.mp3","duration":21.65,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":8.06},{"speaker":"operator","start":8.13,"end":9.15},{"speaker":"narrator","start":9.22,"end":9.95},{"speaker":"operator","start":10.02,"end":12.7},{"speaker":"narrator","start":12.77,"end":21.48}]},"The upper landing offers a little shelter from the wind. Above you, a closed hatch leads to the public transmitter; below, a flare lights a maintenance ladder and a dog keeps barking from somewhere beside it. Two local channels are still trying to reach an operator. You set the receiver on a dry patch of concrete and listen long enough to separate the calls from the rain.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/aabf2ea2afbe18244abd9665.mp3","duration":22.68,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.51}]},"There is time to answer nearby callers before Iman opens the roof route. You can check the dog, take a rescue-channel call, or follow the voice breaking through the static. When you move on, the rising water will cut off a return to this landing. Lia reminds you that you do not have to solve every problem alone, but you do need to decide which messages to pass to the people who can help.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/867e878c89efc01fea1e1e2c.mp3","duration":26.07,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":25.9}]},"Iman calls from the switch platform on the other side of the tower. He has reached the hatch's manual latch, but the three bolts will not shift together. “Three bolts and two hands,” he mutters. “I would like a word with whoever drew this.” You keep your lamp pointed at the hinge while he braces one glove against the frame and works the last bolt free.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/4b467f9a1462aa6ddc8c303f.mp3","duration":20.05,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":8.65},{"speaker":"iman","start":8.72,"end":10.45},{"speaker":"narrator","start":10.52,"end":11.38},{"speaker":"iman","start":11.45,"end":13.45},{"speaker":"narrator","start":13.52,"end":19.88}]},"The hatch lifts far enough to reveal the inland road beyond the roof. Through the opening, you can hear another crew calling people toward the stair. Iman's radio knocks against a rung, making a crash so loud that you stop breathing for a moment. “I'm fine,” he says at once. “The radio wants everyone to know how hard it's working.” You laugh before you realize how badly you needed to.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/7f452da85219c81ce4bf5820.mp3","duration":22.19,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":13.58},{"speaker":"iman","start":13.65,"end":14.52},{"speaker":"narrator","start":14.59,"end":15.68},{"speaker":"iman","start":15.75,"end":18.97},{"speaker":"narrator","start":19.04,"end":22.02}]},"Then an electric lock slams shut behind him. You hear him test it, once, then again with more force. The hatch is open on your side, but the grille has sealed Iman inside the switch platform. His next words come without a joke. “That wasn't the water. Someone sent a command.” You tighten your grip on the receiver and ask who still has access to that lock.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/b89e64abc972b91228cd6c9b.mp3","duration":21.14,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":13.77},{"speaker":"iman","start":13.84,"end":16.14},{"speaker":"narrator","start":16.21,"end":20.97}]},"The private line lights up before Iman can answer. Arga speaks as if he is explaining a routine safety measure. “The relay protects the archive. Return the source to the station, and I will release the platform.” You look from the receiver to the open hatch. He has kept the route available while locking in the man who made it available, and he expects you to call that protection.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/86e8aea668586e239a5987e4.mp3","duration":23.74,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":7.87},{"speaker":"arga","start":7.94,"end":13.32},{"speaker":"narrator","start":13.39,"end":23.57}]},"“You're holding an evacuation worker behind a grille,” you say. Arga insists that nobody needs to get hurt, provided you stop transmitting and return what belongs to the station. There is no assembled voice to blame this time. The order is his, spoken live. Below you, the bridge crew is asking for a crossing signal, and Iman is still testing the lock with one tired hand.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/0125e9d08ae4820e8fcd5a02.mp3","duration":24.14,"cues":[{"speaker":"operator","start":0.05,"end":3.18},{"speaker":"narrator","start":3.25,"end":23.97}]},"Iman comes back on the public channel. “Tell him my daughter is waiting,” he says, then lets the thought go. “No. He knows people are waiting. Ask him about the release.” You can keep the exchange public and refuse the demand, or press Arga for proof that Iman's route is open. Either way, a private argument cannot be allowed to swallow the rescue calls below.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/336474d0816d724c828cb4f0.mp3","duration":20.95,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":2.21},{"speaker":"iman","start":2.28,"end":4.03},{"speaker":"narrator","start":4.1,"end":5.99},{"speaker":"iman","start":6.06,"end":9.02},{"speaker":"narrator","start":9.09,"end":20.78}]},"Lia reaches the upper landing with a rescue crew and brings the conversation back to the people below. She reads the first landing's headcount, then adds the callers still on the lower route. Earlier decisions have changed who arrived dry and who is still waiting. Some can walk unaided. Others lean on a neighbor, too tired to pretend they can manage another stair alone.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/af5024b6ab187a167b62cdfe.mp3","duration":24.49,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.32}]},"The tower route is open, but using it means holding the bridge while a crew reaches Iman's manual release. Lia asks each group for someone who can help with the rail and someone who can keep the names together. “Don't tell me you're all fine because you're embarrassed,” she says into the receiver. A woman answers that she needs a shoulder. Another voice immediately offers one.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/d59d01897b3a997f2eef1acf.mp3","duration":25.72,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":15.37},{"speaker":"lia","start":15.44,"end":18.19},{"speaker":"narrator","start":18.26,"end":25.55}]},"You start to read the total, and Lia stops you gently. “Names first. We can add the numbers afterward.” She wants a count that another crew can check, with every unanswered caller still visible. You turn to a clean part of the route board. Before you choose how to cross, everyone needs to hear what each plan can actually do, and who may still have to wait for a boat.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/56f0adf6bbc6dcfbfbda77ea.mp3","duration":25.17,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":3.38},{"speaker":"lia","start":3.45,"end":6.27},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.34,"end":25}]},"Iman can hear the surge before you see it. He asks you to make the crossing decision now, while the bridge crew can still hold the rail. A coordinated signal would let both waiting groups move together and give the crew time to reach his manual release. For that to work, people need a neighborhood bell they can hear or a marked ladder they can follow through the rain.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f1469cb0b030d550aa58e2d5.mp3","duration":21.63,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.46}]},"Without that support, the manual stair is still usable. You would send fewer people at a time, keeping someone at the rail to guide each group. It is slower, and the last group may have to wait for a rescue boat. Closing the lower crossing is the other option: those already above the flood could retreat to the roof, but the people below and Iman would remain a rescue crew's responsibility.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/271c857907849e8c55aa0044.mp3","duration":25.39,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":25.22}]},"Lia asks what she should tell the waiting callers. You study the route board, then the water beneath it. You want to offer a plan with no one left outside it; wanting one does not give you one. Whatever you choose, you will explain the limit before anyone starts moving. They have already spent too much of this night following instructions whose real cost was hidden from them.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/20ec51a203c00ad760c9b87e.mp3","duration":22,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.83}]},"The first replies come in as the crossing begins. You write each name only after hearing it from a caller or a crew member who can confirm where they are. Sleeves drip onto the landing; someone passes a bag forward before helping the person behind them. Your pencil keeps slipping in your glove, so you take the glove off and work with a hand that is cold enough to hurt.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/c95267d3c9f6be12b84fe661.mp3","duration":21.9,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.73}]},"The route board now shows the consequences of everything you arranged earlier: which paths have light, who is willing to follow your instructions, and what help the neighbors have made available. Lia checks the arrivals beside you. Whenever a name does not answer, she leaves space for the next report rather than deciding what the silence means. You keep the rescue channel open as you prepare to move up.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/fb146e1be0b94fa5899d1d66.mp3","duration":23.33,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.16}]},"Above the last step, the public transmitter has begun to hum. The archive is still dry inside your coat. Until now, you could postpone deciding what to do with it because someone needed a route or a reply. That reason is running out. You carry the receiver upstairs, keeping the live names within reach, and ask Lia to stay while you make the choice the whole city will hear.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/86f41b0caad92a4bc52cd63c.mp3","duration":24.26,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.09}]},"The agreed signal reaches both groups. The bridge crew holds the rail as people cross, and Lia checks each arriving name against the list in your hand. The last caller through is Iman. A stranger has a firm grip on his yellow glove; between them, they negotiate the final step with the seriousness of people who have no strength left for a stumble.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f879b54e16e4c40d7f7eafe5.mp3","duration":23.33,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.16}]},"Iman sits down without waiting to be asked. “Next time,” he says, “I would like a job with a chair.” Lia passes him water, then reads the lower-route names again. Every name on that board now has an answer from someone above the flood. There is still treatment to arrange and shelter to find, but for this crossing she can finally put down the pencil without leaving a blank beside anyone.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/df526837e82b119b17815803.mp3","duration":24.53,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":3.31},{"speaker":"iman","start":3.38,"end":4.45},{"speaker":"narrator","start":4.52,"end":5.24},{"speaker":"iman","start":5.31,"end":7.19},{"speaker":"narrator","start":7.26,"end":24.36}]},"You wait until his breathing settles before moving toward the transmitter. Beneath your coat, the archive is dry; its weight has become harder to ignore now that it is no longer competing with the next rescue instruction. Lia picks up the receiver to keep the arrivals channel open. You climb the last step knowing the crossing is finished, and that what you send from here can still hurt people who survived it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/63905bbd20c5e857cfdc9c63.mp3","duration":24.22,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.05}]},"Small groups make their way up the manual stair, one hand on the rail and another on whoever needs help. The bridge crew keeps the route open long enough to reach Iman's release. When he crawls out through the hatch, he tries to stand too quickly and has to sit back down. You stay beside him until he can tell Lia his name clearly enough for her to mark his arrival.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/447507a023e0f4e475bfd40c.mp3","duration":22.3,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.13}]},"The last lower group cannot cross before the surge. Lia reaches them on the rescue channel and asks a boat crew to confirm their position. She reads their names back, making sure they know they have not dropped off the board just because the stair is closed. You can hear relief in some replies and anger in others. Both belong on the line. You tell them you will keep taking reports.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/b3f7dd72ea35ba003e9ff4c5.mp3","duration":20.57,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":20.4}]},"At the top step, the transmitter is ready and the archive is still dry. You have brought people through, but the smaller count will not let you describe this as a complete evacuation. Lia stays with the boat channel while you prepare the public message. Before you explain the stolen voice and the station's choices, you write down the names of those whose rescue is still unfinished.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/71ce46cc99920ca69989ace7.mp3","duration":23.43,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.26}]},"You close the lower crossing and direct everyone already above the flood toward the roof. The bridge crew pulls back before the surge reaches the rail. Lia moves the remaining callers onto the rescue boat channel, asking them to describe where they can wait above the water. You write their answers carefully. Closing a route means finding another way to reach them, even if you cannot promise when it will arrive.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/da0ac5a77ecd838c4f46f9d8.mp3","duration":22.6,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.43}]},"Iman's platform light goes out. For a moment, all you hear is rain against the receiver; then his battery radio comes through. “Still here,” he says. You ask him to keep it close and save the battery between reports. Lia puts his name at the top of a boat request. Nobody pretends the lock has opened. He is waiting, and the crew needs to know exactly where.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/4a88dd891a6e6f447992e75f.mp3","duration":24.38,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":8.46},{"speaker":"iman","start":8.53,"end":9.36},{"speaker":"narrator","start":9.43,"end":24.21}]},"The roof is high enough to transmit from, but you cannot settle into the safety it offers. Below it, the rescue is still incomplete. You carry the dry archive to the public transmitter and leave the live receiver beside it, where you can hear another call immediately. Whatever account you give of tonight will have to include the crossing you closed and the people who are still asking for a way out.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f5ac856c5a8e66358a6d9ca1.mp3","duration":23.82,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.65}]},"The public transmitter takes a few seconds to settle onto the emergency band. Voices overlap before you can identify them: a mother checking how many pairs of shoes came off the bus, a driver offering three empty seats, a man asking which shelter has his brother. The channel is busy with ordinary details that have become urgent. You wait for a gap instead of speaking over someone who has waited all night to be heard.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/812a561a0fb6c2e6a7b274a6.mp3","duration":25.38,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":25.21}]},"Lia stands beside you while you unpack the archive. This is the channel that can carry an account beyond the station's control. It can also carry every private identity attached to that account. Once the raw file leaves the tower, you will not be able to ask each listener to give back an address or forget a clean recording of your voice. Arga's decisions belong in public; the file holds more than his decisions.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/685450d89edaacd0ee828b0e.mp3","duration":26.28,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":26.11}]},"“A true account can still hurt someone,” Lia says. She does not tell you which button to press. Instead, she asks what you would say to a caller who discovered that asking for rescue had also made their home public. You look down at the names, then check the archive again. Before you transmit anything, you need to be clear about whose information is traveling with the evidence.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/cc77638b619260fa4fe07288.mp3","duration":23.73,"cues":[{"speaker":"lia","start":0.05,"end":2.28},{"speaker":"narrator","start":2.35,"end":23.56}]},"You lay out what the archive contains so Lia can check your reasoning. The signed order records Arga's priority decision. The queue files contain private identities and handoffs. The source recordings contain enough clean speech for ECHO to assemble your voice again. They are stored together, but they do not all need the same audience. You wish someone at the station had asked that question before putting them in one system.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/72cba72df0c08451e945ca12.mp3","duration":27.96,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":27.79}]},"Publishing the complete archive would give listeners the original signatures, times, and identities immediately. A protected copy would keep the sequence and authorization available while withholding private details; reviewers would need controlled access and willing witnesses to check the rest. Neither choice would finish every investigation tonight. The difference is what strangers could copy while that work was still underway.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/1a7b6bbc5213dd7a28bd06a4.mp3","duration":26.81,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":26.64}]},"You could also destroy this source and speak from your own knowledge. That would prevent this module from being used again, though it cannot tell you whether other copies exist. It would also sacrifice the strongest original audit. Lia waits while you check each consequence aloud. “Whichever one you choose,” she says, “tell them what you kept, what you removed, and what they still need to verify.”":{"src":"assets/voice/en/a2fe5f32f9551b05c5d2b9d3.mp3","duration":23.51,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.5},{"speaker":"lia","start":16.57,"end":18.17},{"speaker":"narrator","start":18.24,"end":19.04},{"speaker":"lia","start":19.11,"end":23.34}]},"Your thumb rests beside the send switch. Somewhere below the roof, a crew is still exchanging names with the shelter desk. You can hear them through the receiver, confirming each arrival twice. In front of you lies a record that made people disappear from a queue without moving them anywhere. Sending it might force the station to answer for that. Sending all of it would also give strangers information those callers never offered them.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/bd51dfe0da3a0af717cc6361.mp3","duration":27,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":26.83}]},"You think about how easily the station treated your consent as a detail it could work around. You agreed to training recordings; nobody asked whether your voice could direct a real evacuation. The callers in this archive were not asked to become public evidence either. At the same time, destroying the source would leave the people investigating Arga with less than you held in your own hands tonight.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/5127eb4b5c3da0aa6af45448.mp3","duration":21.22,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.05}]},"Lia moves the live receiver closer, leaving the decision to you. The rain eases enough that you can hear a driver asking for one more passenger. You take a breath and decide what the transmitter will carry: the complete raw archive, a public copy with private identities protected, or your live testimony after the source is destroyed. This choice will not change who crossed the bridge. It will change what follows them into morning.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/297302f86916c20674114bd8.mp3","duration":27.43,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":27.26}]},"You give your name before explaining why your voice has already been heard tonight. The evacuation announcement was assembled from recordings you made for training. Arga authorized the priority rule, and the queue header was reset after the clinic transfer failed. You explain what the archive can show and what further review must establish. There is no reason to make the account sound more complete than the evidence you have.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/367c294af1ae4812dfad350b.mp3","duration":24.83,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.66}]},"Then you turn to the rescue list. You read one location incorrectly, stop, and repeat it with the correction. For a moment you want to begin again and leave a cleaner version behind. Instead, you say which line was wrong so the boat crew can correct its own copy. A driver answers with a route update, and you write that down before continuing. The work is still happening while you speak.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/7652f27c1578e77563ea68cd.mp3","duration":26.02,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":25.85}]},"When you finish, the channel fills with practical replies. Someone can offer a dry room. A crew needs the position of the next pickup. A caller asks you to repeat a name. You do, without rushing them. Lia takes the archive while you keep hold of the live receiver. Whatever becomes of your account tomorrow, tonight someone is still listening for an answer, and you stay until that answer has reached them.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/1a3f2b6bb4ded41f4c46e76e.mp3","duration":25.13,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.96}]},"You begin with your name and the facts you can support. Arga admitted authorizing the dock priority. Your consent covered training recordings, not live dispatch. The files show a reused queue header, but you did not finish comparing the documentary sequence. You say that plainly. People listening need to know where your account is solid and where another reviewer must still do the work.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/4b1c7f6c23935dd118d65a4f.mp3","duration":24.29,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.12}]},"You explain your decision about the source: whether the public received the full file, a protected copy, or your testimony after its destruction. Whatever you chose, the unfinished comparison remains unfinished. You do not let the relief of reaching a transmitter turn a suspicion into a proven timeline. Lia listens beside you, checking that the people who might investigate tomorrow will be able to separate your observations from your conclusions.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/df3b5dbf9672a93504aa4566.mp3","duration":27.25,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":27.08}]},"Then you read the rescue names. You get one location wrong, stop, and correct it on the live channel so every crew can change its copy. A boat operator repeats the correction back to you. The simple exchange steadies your breathing more than an effortless performance would have. You keep the receiver close and wait for the next report; there is still useful work you can do without claiming to know everything.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/b844ce5e0e217ee1da3c81a2.mp3","duration":25.4,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":25.23}]},"You destroy the archive source before turning to the microphone. The original voice splices and signed audit on this module will not be available for a reviewer to recover. For a moment, you keep waiting to feel certain that this was the right cost to pay. Certainty does not arrive. You tell the live channel exactly what you destroyed, including the evidence that could have tested your own account.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/89698209df1d3289a9a4694a.mp3","duration":25.37,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":25.2}]},"Then you describe the sequence you checked: Arga's priority order, the failed clinic handoff, and the reused header that hid the waiting requests. You explain that your training consent never permitted live dispatch. Independent copies and willing witnesses will now have to carry parts of the investigation the original archive could have settled. Destroying this source prevents its reuse; you do not claim that it erases every copy that might exist elsewhere.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/ef86cc09b770fd48ddbee9bf.mp3","duration":26.93,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":26.76}]},"Last, you read the rescue names and the positions the crews have confirmed. Once, you correct a location aloud and ask a boat operator to repeat it back. The answer comes without ceremony, followed by another request for a pickup point. You stay at the receiver. You have chosen to give up the source, but that choice does not release you from telling the truth about it or from answering the people still waiting.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/eee2bc80596cddb8de5d43c1.mp3","duration":22.84,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.67}]},"You prepare the public copy with Lia beside you, checking what each removal would hide. Private addresses and personal handoffs come out; the time sequence and Arga's authorization stay visible. The original is sealed for review rather than rewritten to resemble a cleaner night. You announce those limits before sending, so nobody mistakes a protected copy for every piece of the record.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f8a4e204885b13f72427a56c.mp3","duration":24.56,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.39}]},"The transmission carries the sequence you verified and an explanation of how ECHO used your training voice without live permission. You tell reviewers how the full audit can be checked through controlled access and testimony from people willing to give it. Protecting an identity must not become a way of protecting the decision that harmed that person. Lia asks you to repeat that distinction, and you put it into words anyone listening can understand.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/49a8ad7686551ae0a2063dcd.mp3","duration":26.29,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":26.12}]},"Then you read the rescue names the callers agreed to share, keeping private details off the public band. You make a mistake in one location and correct it immediately. A boat crew confirms the change, then asks for the next pickup. You keep answering. The file is traveling beyond the tower now, but the people attached to it still need care that no transmission can provide by itself.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/915f9e39bfd709912a886659.mp3","duration":25.02,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.85}]},"The complete raw archive leaves the tower. The signatures, timestamps, and private identities travel together; the priority order can now be checked against the original record rather than a summary prepared by the station. You explain the sequence you verified, including your limited training consent and the hidden requests. While you are speaking, the file is already becoming something you can no longer control.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/8fa05776680e868c82f2c698.mp3","duration":28.67,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":28.5}]},"A caller asks whether their home address was in the transmission. You answer yes. There is a pause long enough for you to hear Lia put down her pencil. “I called for a way out,” the caller says. You cannot undo the release, and you do not try to make their worry sound unreasonable. You tell them what was included and admit that you made the decision without their permission.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/46b6677a24395086eafc592a.mp3","duration":20.87,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":8.27},{"speaker":"woman","start":8.34,"end":9.54},{"speaker":"narrator","start":9.61,"end":20.7}]},"When you return to the rescue list, you include the cost of your own choice in the account. Exposing Arga's order has not made those private details harmless. You correct one location aloud and keep taking replies from the crews. The archive may help establish responsibility tomorrow. Tonight, the caller who asked about their address still deserves a clear answer from the person who sent it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/41dd25f64a8a8a74c6fb5dcc.mp3","duration":22.74,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.57}]},"By the time the first bus stops on the inland road, the rain has thinned to a drizzle. Its driver has not slept; he checks the steps with a torch and tells everyone to take their time. Inside, the air smells of wet coats and instant coffee. A passenger moves a bag off the aisle seat before you have to ask. You sit down and discover how much effort standing had been taking.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/624567770e643177caae1c05.mp3","duration":24.57,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.4}]},"Lia stays near the door, marking arrivals against the night list. The rescue boat reports on a separate channel so a bus headcount cannot accidentally close a search that is still underway. Whenever a report needs checking, she leaves its line open. You keep the receiver in your lap, ready to answer, but for a few minutes another operator takes the replies and tells you to drink something warm.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/1c3503327253ebdfd771a235.mp3","duration":22.42,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.25}]},"A paper cup appears in your hands. You do not know who brought it, and there is no graceful speech waiting in you. “Thank you,” is all you manage. Nobody asks for more. Beyond the window, people are unloading blankets onto a dry stretch of road. Morning has brought somewhere to sit and work from; what it has not settled is written clearly enough for the next crew to pick up.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/2c6fbe320c42150a4dccf55b.mp3","duration":22.8,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":6.71},{"speaker":"operator","start":6.78,"end":7.57},{"speaker":"narrator","start":7.64,"end":22.63}]},"At the shelter desk, you begin a fresh log instead of copying the station's last summary. ECHO has been disconnected from live dispatch. The announcement used pieces of your training recordings, and Arga admitted authorizing the dock priority. His explanation for the failed clinic handoff belongs beside the reports of the people who were left waiting. The review will have to test the remaining documentary questions, not smooth them away.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/de9ee461f6344a30b5dd371c.mp3","duration":27.06,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":26.89}]},"Your sister was never one of tonight's live callers. Nila died in the ferry accident; no file can turn an old recording into another chance to bring her home. Remembering her does not require you to make her part of this emergency. You leave room for that grief to be ordinary and unfinished, while keeping the rescue channel available to people who can still receive an answer.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/b7f957a869579b3f109c60bb.mp3","duration":22.05,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.88}]},"A new operator asks what should happen when a line goes quiet. You look at the empty space beside an unconfirmed report, then hand over the receiver. “Call again,” you say. “And if they don't answer, keep the question open for the next crew.” You write the instruction at the top of the fresh page. It is a small thing to leave behind, but everyone coming to this desk will be able to use it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/0de171b85d429a6631e66a79.mp3","duration":22.22,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":8.89},{"speaker":"operator","start":8.96,"end":9.72},{"speaker":"narrator","start":9.79,"end":10.44},{"speaker":"operator","start":10.51,"end":14.04},{"speaker":"narrator","start":14.11,"end":22.05}]},"The night has reached its final record. Read the rescue result alongside the account you sent, then see what became of the people whose calls you chose to answer.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/3697020bcbfacb233e336a53.mp3","duration":10.18,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":10.01}]},"Bu Sari's radio catches a dull brass note before her voice comes through. “The screens are dead, but the bell isn't,” she says. “Its rope has only decided to marry the shutter hook. Very inconvenient time for a wedding.” A neighbor is holding the radio while she stays on the market's upper landing, trying to find a safe way to free the snag.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/fd6fe1fde786c715fe356662.mp3","duration":20.15,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":3.95},{"speaker":"sari","start":4.02,"end":6.9},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.97,"end":7.81},{"speaker":"sari","start":7.88,"end":13.6},{"speaker":"narrator","start":13.67,"end":19.98}]},"She has kept the market for thirty years, long enough that the nearby houses know what its bell means in a storm. “People keep waiting for a screen to tell them they can move,” she says. “Some of them can't even turn the screen on.” If the bell works, she can give the street a signal everyone hears without sending anyone down into the water.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/08755be29a93ecddf6b5b214.mp3","duration":19.95,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":6.07},{"speaker":"sari","start":6.14,"end":9.25},{"speaker":"narrator","start":9.32,"end":10.16},{"speaker":"sari","start":10.23,"end":13.24},{"speaker":"narrator","start":13.31,"end":19.78}]},"You ask her to leave the rope alone until you have checked the hook and the dry landing. She grumbles that she has survived worse advice, then agrees. Beneath the humor is somebody counting neighbors she cannot see from where she stands. You bring up the loft view and tell her you will guide the next step, rather than simply asking her to be careful.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/7931ced9c1a1bbbf6abe34c7.mp3","duration":19.55,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":19.38}]},"Inspect the shutter hook and upper landing. Find how the rope can be freed from above, then guide Bu Sari to the dry rail so she can ring the bell without entering the water.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/0937bd93f28937a1eca91085.mp3","duration":9.37,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":9.2}]},"You talk Bu Sari through the dry approach, and the next sound on her line is clear enough to startle you: three rings carrying over the roofs. A torch answers from one window, then another. She counts them aloud, stopping when a house she knows remains dark. The bell has given people a way to reply, not proof that every person on the street is safe.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/44181807eb4724a5a2485713.mp3","duration":20.25,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":20.08}]},"“See? Nobody needed to remember a password,” she says, allowing herself a short laugh before calling the quiet houses by name. Her neighbor repeats the answering windows into the radio so you can keep a record. You hear the street becoming specific again: homes with people inside them, rather than a block the station might dismiss with one careless word.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/782e4fe2c11d7cc0a4d896fa.mp3","duration":21.96,"cues":[{"speaker":"sari","start":0.05,"end":3.36},{"speaker":"narrator","start":3.43,"end":21.79}]},"Bu Sari says she will keep the bell ready for the tower crossing. It can carry a shared signal even if the screens and announcements fail. You thank her for staying on the dry landing, and she tells you to save the formal speech for daylight. “Keep the channel open,” she says. “I'll tell you which windows are still waiting.”":{"src":"assets/voice/en/cf46e8d728b11d422c35c0bf.mp3","duration":20.95,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":15.59},{"speaker":"sari","start":15.66,"end":17.06},{"speaker":"narrator","start":17.13,"end":17.97},{"speaker":"sari","start":18.04,"end":20.78}]},"Lia calls from the clinic's loading ramp, where the water has covered the route a delivery should have taken. A porter named Deni is holding a sealed cooler above the flood. “These supplies need to reach the hill shelter,” she tells you. “I'll handle the contents. What I need from you is a dry path we can actually follow.” She gives you time to write that down.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/5bc03c58813a665112a06188.mp3","duration":21.39,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":9.95},{"speaker":"lia","start":10.02,"end":12.58},{"speaker":"narrator","start":12.65,"end":13.55},{"speaker":"lia","start":13.62,"end":19.1},{"speaker":"narrator","start":19.17,"end":21.22}]},"Deni shifts the cooler and asks whether anything inside resembles cake. “He has been carrying it like a birthday present,” Lia explains. “I've told him twice. He remains hopeful.” You hear him insist that asking costs nothing. Their exchange is brief, but it gives you a sense of how long he has been standing there with tired arms, trying not to admit they are shaking.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/5fbc491b90cf96062514efb8.mp3","duration":22.24,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":4.03},{"speaker":"lia","start":4.1,"end":6.81},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.88,"end":7.96},{"speaker":"lia","start":8.03,"end":10.99},{"speaker":"narrator","start":11.06,"end":22.07}]},"The loading ramp will not do. The laundry passage may still connect uphill, and the cargo tag can confirm where the cooler belongs. You ask them to wait while you check both, leaving the seal for Lia to manage. There is no clever medical decision for you to make here. You need to get the right box to the right hands without sending its carrier back into danger.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/887001ec4876fdd6aae0e610.mp3","duration":21.77,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.6}]},"Inspect the sealed cargo tag to confirm the hill shelter destination, then check the laundry arrow for the uphill route. Keep the cooler sealed; Lia will handle its contents after delivery.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/2c92bee8c654e660d9c01a32.mp3","duration":12.41,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":12.24}]},"You guide Deni past the flooded ramp and through the laundry passage, asking him to repeat each turn before taking it. When Lia calls from the shelter doorway, her first word is “Delivered.” Then you hear the cooler being set down, carefully, and Deni asking whether successful delivery changes the situation with the cake. “It earns you a chair,” she says. “We're starting small.”":{"src":"assets/voice/en/14b5c77233d5cafa17a63860.mp3","duration":23.76,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":11.09},{"speaker":"lia","start":11.16,"end":11.95},{"speaker":"narrator","start":12.02,"end":20.17},{"speaker":"lia","start":20.24,"end":21.28},{"speaker":"narrator","start":21.35,"end":22.19},{"speaker":"lia","start":22.26,"end":23.59}]},"The shelter clinic checks the seal and accepts the supplies. Lia tells you that more people can now be treated here instead of making another journey toward the flood. She does not offer a miraculous count of lives saved. She gives you something clearer: a delivery received, a carrier resting, and one less reason to send frightened people back down the road.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/b48cac4e0c70ff08c35e38ed.mp3","duration":21.56,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.39}]},"You start to say it was only a route check, but Lia interrupts gently. “I know it looked like a box. Deni still had to get it here.” You leave the supplies in her care and keep the passage in your record. For once, a completed entry means exactly what it says. Someone at the other end has confirmed it, and the next call does not need to undo it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/9780d50cd3f370c7abc32161.mp3","duration":21.93,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":4.4},{"speaker":"lia","start":4.47,"end":7.91},{"speaker":"narrator","start":7.98,"end":21.76}]},"Nila's name appears in the service inbox, and your hand stops above the controls. For one unreasonable second you expect to hear her asking why you have taken so long to answer. Then the date brings you back. Your sister died in the ferry accident. This file is older than tonight, and however much you want it to be a new call, you know it cannot be one.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/d74ac00ce686bb3ae9e19152.mp3","duration":22.07,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.9}]},"The note identifies a backup from the ferry terminal's lost-phone desk. Salim kept it outside the training archive, among private messages to be returned to their owners. ECHO has not turned her into a caller. You check that distinction twice anyway. After hearing your own voice used without you, even a familiar name needs somewhere safe to belong.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/439e3f3ee7a62e67abbd189d.mp3","duration":22.44,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.27}]},"The file waits for you to press play. You have spent so much time asking strangers to stay on the line that you do not know how to sit with a message that asks nothing urgent of you. Nobody will be rescued by decoding it. Nobody needs you to make it evidence. For a moment you are simply someone who has been given another chance to hear a sister's voice.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/c0e459807bfd237c7e9c8ec4.mp3","duration":19.9,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":19.73}]},"“Don't make your sad face at me,” Nila says. “I owe you breakfast. If the ferry's late, order mine anyway.” Her laugh comes afterward, familiar enough that you wait for another sentence even when the recording ends. There is no warning hidden inside it. She was planning a meal, assuming there would be time to make good on a small promise, and teasing you because that was what she did.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/65107077e64f0c02dd598cbf.mp3","duration":22.93,"cues":[{"speaker":"nila","start":0.05,"end":1.84},{"speaker":"narrator","start":1.91,"end":3.13},{"speaker":"nila","start":3.2,"end":5.91},{"speaker":"narrator","start":5.98,"end":22.76}]},"You remember being annoyed that she had borrowed your charger. You had been saving your phone's battery and she had been certain yours would last. The memory arrives with its ordinary irritation still attached, before the accident could make every detail feel sacred. It is a relief to remember that you were allowed to be impatient with her, and that she was allowed to laugh at you.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/200dd273a6bc7d543bb40eae.mp3","duration":23.06,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.89}]},"The room returns slowly: the wet sleeve against the desk, the receiver still carrying other people's calls. Grief has made you revisit the terrible day so often that you sometimes forget how much life came before it. This little file does not change what happened. It lets you hear, for a moment, a morning in which your sister expected to be hungry and expected you to be there.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/389a68c392eab61b22968e0c.mp3","duration":23.49,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.32}]},"You let the file finish without playing it again immediately. There will be another time for that, you tell yourself, surprised by how much you need to believe it. The private recording is yours to keep. Hearing it during an emergency does not give the station a right to use it, and loving Nila does not oblige you to turn her laugh into something useful for strangers.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/938459b63e1aa92bfda24a23.mp3","duration":19.9,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":19.73}]},"If you want, you can tell the channel her breakfast joke in your own words: a small reminder that people still make plans for ordinary mornings. You can also keep the moment entirely to yourself. Neither choice sends her recording onto the open band. After everything you have learned about permission tonight, the difference matters even here, where the voice means so much to you.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/8efd639576dfb294e86f12d4.mp3","duration":22.69,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.52}]},"A live call reaches the receiver, and you lift your hand toward it. Nila does not have to become the reason everyone else is saved. She can remain your sister, with a borrowed charger and a breakfast debt you never collected. You put the file somewhere you can find it again, then decide whether to share the joke before returning to the person who is waiting now.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/41cfee61cfb0cff388acf9c4.mp3","duration":21.1,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":20.93}]},"Salim's voice reaches you from the old pump house roof. “My chair was empty because the stair was locked,” he says. “I went out with the manual key. I should have left you more than a cold mug.” He explains that he copied Arga's order before leaving, then lost his way back when the water rose. His absence had a reason; the silence he left you still needs an apology.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/d4138a4cbcbaa1b349f59d3e.mp3","duration":22.81,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":3.3},{"speaker":"salim","start":3.37,"end":6.54},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.61,"end":7.43},{"speaker":"salim","start":7.5,"end":11.71},{"speaker":"narrator","start":11.78,"end":22.64}]},"His grey coat has caught on a nail, and you hear the fabric tug while he tries to settle where the roof is dry. “I taught you to leave a clean log,” he says. “Then I left you to work out my mess.” He does not ask you to excuse it because he meant well. He tells you his location carefully, waits while you repeat it, and agrees to stay above the water.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/0c86b6709b765ff4c26eebee.mp3","duration":21.25,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":6.15},{"speaker":"salim","start":6.22,"end":8.58},{"speaker":"narrator","start":8.65,"end":9.47},{"speaker":"salim","start":9.54,"end":11.54},{"speaker":"narrator","start":11.61,"end":21.08}]},"You can arrange help while hearing what he saw. The copied order may support the archive, but you do not need him to prove anything before deserving a pickup. You tell him that first. His next breath sounds less hurried. Then he asks you to listen closely, because the failed clinic transfer did not disappear by itself, and he was there when its place in the queue changed.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/9fdbf24c315d3d57e16675fa.mp3","duration":25.63,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":25.46}]},"“The clinic transfer failed,” Salim says. “I told Arga. He approved the header reset anyway, and I watched those requests disappear.” He keeps to what he witnessed, correcting himself when you ask which page carried the approval. You write down his account beside the copied order. It is willing testimony, not a substitute for checking the documents that can support it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/60e546eafe9a568590a28117.mp3","duration":22.42,"cues":[{"speaker":"salim","start":0.05,"end":1.83},{"speaker":"narrator","start":1.9,"end":2.99},{"speaker":"salim","start":3.06,"end":9},{"speaker":"narrator","start":9.07,"end":22.25}]},"He is prepared to give a written statement to a reviewer. Then he asks what else is in the raw audit. His home address appears among the staff details, and his family is there. “Use what I saw,” he says. “Don't send people to my front door.” It is an ordinary request, made while he is waiting to be rescued, and you cannot treat the waiting as consent.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/9af49ea99424b801138aebd1.mp3","duration":21.66,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":9.94},{"speaker":"salim","start":10.01,"end":11.21},{"speaker":"narrator","start":11.28,"end":12.1},{"speaker":"salim","start":12.17,"end":15.08},{"speaker":"narrator","start":15.15,"end":21.49}]},"You tell him that a pickup and a statement can be arranged without broadcasting his address. He agrees to testify on that basis. The distinction belongs in your record as carefully as any timestamp: what he is willing to say, and what he has not allowed you to share. After hearing your own borrowed voice, you have no excuse for letting a file answer that question on his behalf.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/29a9d62ae4aef5e00bde714b.mp3","duration":23.03,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.86}]},"A rescue boat spots Salim's lamp and calls up to the roof. You keep his line open until the pilot confirms the pickup. Salim wraps the copied order in the plastic from his lunch before handing it down. “Terrible sandwich,” he tells you. “Excellent packaging.” The pilot laughs, and for the first time his channel carries a sound that is not being broken apart by rain or fear.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/c3dce7261be5b92c482d2d0b.mp3","duration":26.36,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":14.78},{"speaker":"salim","start":14.85,"end":16.23},{"speaker":"narrator","start":16.3,"end":17.22},{"speaker":"salim","start":17.29,"end":18.55},{"speaker":"narrator","start":18.62,"end":26.19}]},"His written statement can go to a reviewer without the raw address attached. You save that permission with the account, remembering that tonight's rescue does not settle what you might later choose to publish. Salim has offered you evidence and asked you to protect a particular part of his life. The fact that he is leaving the roof cannot make either part of that agreement disappear.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/1c526c31a143742b22a5dc4f.mp3","duration":20.65,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":20.48}]},"“Leave my chair empty,” he says as the boat moves away. “You don't need to wait for me to tell you what to do.” You almost answer that you would have preferred that advice before the shift began. He seems to hear it anyway, and apologizes once more. You thank the pilot, confirm Salim's arrival route, and return to the station's other unanswered lines.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/63da32b02cc04c5d9b695131.mp3","duration":20.42,"cues":[{"speaker":"salim","start":0.05,"end":1.44},{"speaker":"narrator","start":1.51,"end":3.47},{"speaker":"salim","start":3.54,"end":5.55},{"speaker":"narrator","start":5.62,"end":20.25}]},"The barking comes from a narrow service ledge below the landing. Your lamp finds a small dog pressed against the wall, determined to look as though he chose to be there. His collar tag reads BISCUIT. Above him, a gate worker has stopped on the maintenance ladder. “He won't climb on his own,” the worker calls. “I've tried explaining that the weather is terrible. He seems to agree without changing his plans.”":{"src":"assets/voice/en/1cd6c233dff7d396589fe8db.mp3","duration":23.02,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":15.22},{"speaker":"worker","start":15.29,"end":16.87},{"speaker":"narrator","start":16.94,"end":18.05},{"speaker":"worker","start":18.12,"end":22.85}]},"You ask whether the worker can reach him safely. There is a dry upper rung that gives access to the ledge, but rain keeps swallowing the edges of the route. The worker can make the lift if you hold the lamp steady and check where the ladder leads. Biscuit gives another offended bark. “Yes, I know,” the worker tells him. “You had a much better bed this morning.”":{"src":"assets/voice/en/9dd0f19842947414e242c03a.mp3","duration":20.68,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.32},{"speaker":"worker","start":16.39,"end":17.35},{"speaker":"narrator","start":17.42,"end":18.57},{"speaker":"worker","start":18.64,"end":20.51}]},"For a moment, the task is simple enough to grasp: one lamp, one route, one passenger who cannot understand the instructions. You tell the worker you will keep the light where it is needed, then inspect the flare and upper rung before anyone moves. There are plenty of reasons to hurry past this landing. None of them make the little dog below it less dependent on the person who has stayed.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/92d04874e188ed72cf22cc6e.mp3","duration":23.08,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.91}]},"Check the route flare and dry upper rung. The gate worker needs a clear path down to Biscuit and back to the tower stair; keep your lamp on that path while the worker makes the lift.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/b9bf05ca23e2224e3f984db7.mp3","duration":10.75,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":10.58}]},"Biscuit arrives on the landing wrapped inside the gate worker's raincoat. He stays still until your lamp catches the bundle, then sneezes with such force that the worker nearly laughs themselves off balance. “Complaint received,” you tell him. The dog settles against the coat as if your apology is the least he deserves. You wait until the worker has both feet on the concrete before lowering the light.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/fc24a161bc2d80d26be59f4c.mp3","duration":23.81,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":12.28},{"speaker":"operator","start":12.35,"end":13.7},{"speaker":"narrator","start":13.77,"end":23.64}]},"The route you checked reaches the upper tower stair. The worker marks the ladder clearly for the next group, making sure the flare can be seen from below. What began as a way to collect one stranded dog has given you another path people can follow in the rain. You call the details to Lia rather than assuming somebody else will notice the marker. She asks you to repeat the landing it connects to.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/666d9d96846f8798e9534ae1.mp3","duration":23.77,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.6}]},"When the route is on her board, Lia asks about the extra passenger. “Biscuit,” you say. “No surname supplied.” She takes a moment to make a space for him. “Put him under B. We'll deal with his paperwork after breakfast.” The worker pulls the coat closer around him. You add the dog and the ladder to the rescue board, then return to the calls waiting at the landing.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/0f0b6905a8c76dcc71a274e4.mp3","duration":21.25,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":3.8},{"speaker":"operator","start":3.87,"end":4.65},{"speaker":"narrator","start":4.72,"end":5.37},{"speaker":"operator","start":5.44,"end":6.82},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.89,"end":9.08},{"speaker":"lia","start":9.15,"end":13.07},{"speaker":"narrator","start":13.14,"end":21.08}]},"A voice breaks through on the neighborhood channel while you watch the lit windows beyond the station. You keep the line open and ask where the caller is waiting, before deciding what help you can offer.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/bdebf983de61afd1e91c89d2.mp3","duration":9.9,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":9.73}]},"A child asks whether the sea can climb stairs. Her father whispers for you to tell her no, but you hear them moving toward an upstairs window, carrying a lamp between them. He wants a sentence that will stop her being frightened. You want to give them something they can use even if the water rises again. Neither wish is difficult to understand.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/e7ec46b99acb46bb795cf9ba.mp3","duration":22.07,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.9}]},"You ask whether the lamp is working, and the child answers before her father can. “It still has some left.” The boat crews are checking lit windows, but you cannot promise when one will reach theirs. You can guide her to hold the light where it can be seen, or explain what the crew is doing without pretending the danger has gone. She stays on the line, waiting for your answer.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/a178e806856119834d4c757f.mp3","duration":21.88,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":4.3},{"speaker":"girl","start":4.37,"end":6.05},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.12,"end":21.71}]},"A love song suddenly takes over the emergency band, tinny and much too cheerful for the rain. “Sorry,” a man says from a balcony. “Wrong button. Again. My wife warned me about this radio.” He struggles with the controls, and you hear someone in the room telling him to stop pressing things simply because they look important. For a few seconds, the city sounds almost ordinary.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/e387aa214e91efc25cea5c92.mp3","duration":21.93,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":5.28},{"speaker":"man","start":5.35,"end":5.93},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6,"end":7.7},{"speaker":"man","start":7.77,"end":11.53},{"speaker":"narrator","start":11.6,"end":21.76}]},"The song is older than Nila, and familiar enough that you know where the chorus will end. Other calls still need this channel, but the balcony radio can do something useful once its owner finds the right button. You can ask him to relay route updates, or let the last chorus finish before clearing the band. He apologizes again, waiting to hear whether he has made a terrible mistake.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f6949fcd92ec3325df4e2de5.mp3","duration":22.73,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.56}]},"A woman calls to say she has opened her upstairs room to six neighbors. “Seven, if somebody is small. Or willing to fold.” She gives a little laugh, then asks whether there are people stranded on the market street who need somewhere above the water. The offer is practical and immediate. She has a room; she would rather it be crowded than leave someone waiting below.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/8efac90d5cf5a98ce870be49.mp3","duration":21.48,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":4.11},{"speaker":"woman","start":4.18,"end":7.7},{"speaker":"narrator","start":7.77,"end":21.31}]},"You check which window belongs to her while she makes space behind the microphone. No fee or permission form is being requested, but the rescue crews still need to know where people have gone. You can share her location with them, or ask her to put the room on the live headcount. Both begin by taking the offer seriously enough to make sure it reaches the people who need it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/d47322dc149ef2b09eb3ca03.mp3","duration":23.24,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.07}]},"A light moves across a neighboring roof as the radio catches another local call. You ask for their position and listen for the people behind the signal; a marker is useful only if someone knows who is waiting beside it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/14add93ac4b34b27e91d6cbc.mp3","duration":12.54,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":12.37}]},"A bedsheet flaps from the corner of a roof, catching the torchlight whenever the wind lifts it. Its owner tells you it is meant as a rescue flag. “It used to be my good wedding linen. My husband said we should save it for an occasion.” She pauses to help a neighbor move clear of the wet edge. “I think this qualifies.”":{"src":"assets/voice/en/d87d3f24c926fe8381550813.mp3","duration":19.34,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":8.32},{"speaker":"olderWoman","start":8.39,"end":13.88},{"speaker":"narrator","start":13.95,"end":17.2},{"speaker":"olderWoman","start":17.27,"end":19.17}]},"Two neighbors are waiting with her, and their lamp battery is failing. The sheet will remain visible when the torch goes out, but the crew needs its position recorded rather than left to chance. You can log it as a rescue marker, or ask the next roof to relay their lamp signal while it lasts. She gives you the location again, more slowly this time, trusting that somebody is writing it down.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/3b066ccac732ffa050e9feb5.mp3","duration":22.51,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.34}]},"Someone has carried a birthday cake onto the roof. The candles are soaked, but SIXTY is still readable on the box, and the group has been arguing about whether to sing. “Only if you promise not to make me climb another stair,” the birthday woman says. “I've already earned next year's cake.” A tired laugh comes from the people keeping close beside her.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/bc52010decad0f7dcb66fc5e.mp3","duration":20.71,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":9.86},{"speaker":"olderWoman","start":9.93,"end":13.43},{"speaker":"narrator","start":13.5,"end":15.08},{"speaker":"olderWoman","start":15.15,"end":17.47},{"speaker":"narrator","start":17.54,"end":20.54}]},"You ask where the group is waiting and hear the location twice over, each person afraid the others will leave something out. They need a pickup, and they also need a moment in which this remains her birthday. You can call a boat for the roof, or let the crew sing while you finish recording its position. Nothing about the joke makes them less frightened; it gives them a way to wait together.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/28ceb82e060445d26dec9bd3.mp3","duration":22.51,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.34}]},"A shopkeeper is signaling from a roof with a shaving mirror, trying to catch the rescue lights between gusts of rain. The generator beside him drowns out your reply. His neighbor counts each flash into the radio, then starts again because the shopkeeper insists she missed one. Their argument is frustratingly clear; the location you actually need is not.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/d7700c22707d400711c1122d.mp3","duration":22.34,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.17}]},"You ask them to slow down, letting the neighbor know you can hear her even if the shopkeeper cannot hear you. They are both trying to help and each thinks the other is making it harder. You can repeat a simple lamp signal for the rescue crew, or have the neighbor relay the roof's position directly. First, they need one instruction both can follow instead of two people shouting over the generator.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/715660d084d83102a308acfd.mp3","duration":24.89,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.72}]},"A knock reaches the maintenance channel, followed by a caller giving a location beneath the station. You ask them to stay on the line while you check what they need and which part of the passage is still dry.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/d9de25a07e212f4a130791ec.mp3","duration":12.13,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":11.96}]},"Three taps come through an open microphone before a cleaner answers. Her trolley is wedged across a flood door, and two people are waiting with her on the dry side. “It's the first time this thing has held still,” she says. You hear her checking that they are both there, then asking whether a rescue crew knows which side of the door they need to reach.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/0b3c2b4b49237c70ba918265.mp3","duration":19.89,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":10.27},{"speaker":"woman","start":10.34,"end":12.55},{"speaker":"narrator","start":12.62,"end":19.72}]},"You repeat their position instead of answering with a vague promise that help is coming. The cleaner sounds relieved simply to hear it said correctly. You can register the dry-side pickup now, or keep the line open until a crew answers her directly. Either way, the useful part of the call is not the trolley's unlikely cooperation; it is making sure the people beside it do not disappear into an unnamed corridor.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/6cff5d79a9c67b3a008c5c82.mp3","duration":24.61,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.44}]},"A retired plumber calls to correct the tunnel map you have been using. “That door's been a cupboard since 2008,” he says. “Lovely cupboard. Terrible exit.” He describes its shelves with enough irritation that you believe he has complained about them before. More importantly, he knows a dry side passage that reaches the upper landing, and the printed plan does not show it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f6f6a4ee8f8045930dcf04f5.mp3","duration":22.73,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":3.67},{"speaker":"olderMan","start":3.74,"end":7.1},{"speaker":"narrator","start":7.17,"end":7.99},{"speaker":"olderMan","start":8.06,"end":10.13},{"speaker":"narrator","start":10.2,"end":22.56}]},"You have him repeat the turns, separating the landmark he has checked from the old arrow on your screen. Local knowledge could save the crew a wasted journey, but another confident voice is not automatically a verified route. You can add his checked passage to the live map, or ask a second worker to confirm it. He is willing to explain again; he would rather be interrupted than have someone sent toward a cupboard.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/3b0abe64ef6b665b65d2c8b8.mp3","duration":26.46,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":26.29}]},"A security guard calls while he and his partner carry a resident toward the laundry stair. He has lost one shoe somewhere behind them. “Mark me incomplete,” he says, and his partner laughs so hard the radio clips the sound. You let the noise settle before asking for their location. Beneath it you can hear three people breathing, all of them still moving together.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/2cfebba503cc7ec6bdeed875.mp3","duration":21.35,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":7.54},{"speaker":"boy","start":7.61,"end":8.54},{"speaker":"narrator","start":8.61,"end":21.18}]},"The guard keeps trying to make the report only about the resident they are carrying. You remind him that he and his partner belong in it too. You can confirm all three on the headcount, or ask the shelter to have dry shoes ready for their arrival. He starts to protest that the shoe is hardly an emergency, then loses the argument when his partner tells you to make it a pair.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/047f1afc340f7a33fc259323.mp3","duration":22.69,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.52}]},"A delivery update comes through without a signature. You ask the caller what they are carrying and where they are waiting; tonight, a cargo entry may be hiding a person who needs to be counted.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/86382f5db08069430e1566e5.mp3","duration":10.76,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":10.59}]},"A driver asks whether the priority lane includes a furious cat in a rice sack. “The cat has expressed an opinion,” he says as an indignant sound interrupts him. Two families are waiting in his van, and he cannot make the passenger list sound orderly no matter how he tries. “I'm not asking for special treatment. I just don't want to leave anyone off it.”":{"src":"assets/voice/en/9cc546bf155ba880ff9199b5.mp3","duration":20.3,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":4.95},{"speaker":"man","start":5.02,"end":7.62},{"speaker":"narrator","start":7.69,"end":16.44},{"speaker":"man","start":16.51,"end":20.13}]},"You ask him to begin with the people, then include the animal they are bringing with them. The driver's relief is audible; he had expected another argument over who belongs. You can log the families and their cat together, or direct the van to the shelter intake crew. The loudest passenger keeps objecting while he waits for your answer, making it difficult for either of you to sound properly official.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/0f21aa540db3ad5e484e575f.mp3","duration":21.57,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.4}]},"A driver calls about an unsigned envelope that reached the dock office before Raka's delivery. He remembers the seal: DIRECTOR'S ROUTING DESK. He cannot tell you what was inside, and you write that limit beside his account. What he can describe is the earlier handoff. The order was already moving through other hands before a young courier became the easiest person to question.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/78ff59ee4eb3138ae179eead.mp3","duration":22.63,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.46}]},"You ask whether he is willing to have his account included in the record. He is, though he wants you to keep the difference between seeing an envelope and knowing its contents clear. You can save that voluntary statement, or tell the channel that Raka did not write the routing order. Neither turns the driver into an all-knowing witness. It gives the courier back a part of the truth suspicion had taken from him.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/69fc39636c85283f117f5569.mp3","duration":24.94,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.77}]},"A baker offers his delivery van and thirty rolls that never reached the morning order. “My accountant will hate this,” he says. “Let him. He can complain from his dry office.” Then he gets down to the practical details: the van can collect people from the inland road after they cross, and the rolls can go where there are more empty stomachs than supplies. He needs someone to tell him which help to arrange first.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/5e7784888aae31218c3b5eef.mp3","duration":24.86,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":4.24},{"speaker":"olderMan","start":4.31,"end":6.38},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.45,"end":7.27},{"speaker":"olderMan","start":7.34,"end":10.86},{"speaker":"narrator","start":10.93,"end":24.69}]},"You note that the van's offer depends on people reaching the inland side; it cannot solve the crossing itself. Still, a pickup or breakfast waiting beyond it gives the rescue crew one less problem to manage. You can assign the van to that arrival point, or send the rolls to the shelter kitchen. The baker asks you to repeat the destination, already looking for something useful to do with the rest of his night.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/116653c69e12a03c8671faa8.mp3","duration":24.4,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.23}]},"A caller reaches the rescue channel as you pause on the landing. You ask them to tell you where they are, then listen for what they need before deciding whom to contact.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/e1db9174dd8e0539bc6efa72.mp3","duration":11.59,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":11.42}]},"A boy calls to report a coat left on the inland stair. You ask whether it is blocking the route. “There's a person in it,” he says, suddenly remembering what matters. “I should have said that first.” You tell him he is doing the right thing by calling and ask him to stay where the radio can hear him. In the background, an adult says they are awake but need help getting up.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/481c536873e93fba0c9820bf.mp3","duration":21.87,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":5.75},{"speaker":"boy","start":5.82,"end":6.93},{"speaker":"narrator","start":7,"end":9.56},{"speaker":"boy","start":9.63,"end":10.69},{"speaker":"narrator","start":10.76,"end":21.7}]},"The person has walked as far as they can manage and is sitting clear of the main path. The boy stayed when the others moved on because he did not want them to wait alone. You can send the nearest crew straight to the stair or keep both callers on the line while help is arranged. Either way, the report needs a person and a position, not a coat someone might step around.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/c3645398245ce42b76e9eb4e.mp3","duration":21.61,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.44}]},"Two newlyweds reach the channel with one dry suitcase and an argument about whose clothes made it inside. They stop arguing when you ask whom they are looking for. The bride's uncle, Budi, left to help someone on the lower stair and has not rejoined them. “We can have another wedding,” she tells you. “We can't hire another Uncle Budi.” Her husband quietly supplies the last place they saw him.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/eec71f7842f4c80d590ad4d1.mp3","duration":24.21,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.81},{"speaker":"woman","start":16.88,"end":17.93},{"speaker":"narrator","start":18,"end":18.9},{"speaker":"woman","start":18.97,"end":20.72},{"speaker":"narrator","start":20.79,"end":24.04}]},"They have not heard that he is hurt; they simply cannot find him among the arriving groups. You explain that a missing reply and a confirmed absence are different things, then check the headcount channel. You can put Budi's name on the open search list or ask the shelter desk whether his arrival has already been recorded. The couple needs somewhere to direct the question instead of carrying it from person to person.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/05cd9910fa75a2168ec6bbf4.mp3","duration":23.69,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.52}]},"A fisherman calls from a boat tied beside the inland stair. He has been watching people arrive, and some are struggling with the last stretch. “For once, the sea is bringing customers to me,” he says, then asks which crew needs the boat. He makes the joke sound easy, but he is already checking the rope and clearing space. There is room for the slower walkers once they reach the pickup point.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/ac586e4d52f8c5b7120b25a9.mp3","duration":23.22,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":7.95},{"speaker":"olderMan","start":8.02,"end":11.11},{"speaker":"narrator","start":11.18,"end":23.05}]},"You ask him to describe the mooring so another operator can find it. He repeats the position and promises to stay available for a reply. You can reserve his boat for people who need the extra help or put it on Lia's live route board so she can assign it as requests arrive. The offer will not solve the whole crossing, but it can spare someone the final walk they no longer have the strength to make.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/4c56210bf5176456ea351abd.mp3","duration":23.94,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.77}]},"A faint voice breaks through the receiver's static. You move the radio under your coat to hear clearly and check whether it is a live caller or another recorded fragment.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/6ca66116620a781f46c3be58.mp3","duration":9.26,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":9.09}]},"A man gives his address three times, each repetition faster than the last. He seems to expect the line to cut before anyone can write it down. You read the location back to him before he can begin a fourth time. There is a pause. “Oh,” he says. “You heard me.” He tells you where he is waiting above the water and asks whether someone can pass that information to a boat.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/52a1f339bd861026394703a2.mp3","duration":23.41,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":14.56},{"speaker":"olderMan","start":14.63,"end":15.16},{"speaker":"narrator","start":15.23,"end":16.05},{"speaker":"olderMan","start":16.12,"end":17.19},{"speaker":"narrator","start":17.26,"end":23.24}]},"You confirm that the position is on your board. He does not need another promise that everything will be fine; he needs to know what happens to the request after this conversation ends. You can relay his location to the boat crew immediately or stay with him on the line while the crew replies. He listens as you explain the next step, no longer spending every spare second repeating his address.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/dcd2a4ab954d641e7ab42864.mp3","duration":23.81,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.64}]},"A loose receiver beside the landing plays one last ECHO fragment. Your old voice begins telling people to remain where they are, using the calm tone you practiced in the training room. You check the local lead and unplug it. The replay stops halfway through a word. Nearby callers are still listening, so you explain that they heard a recording, not a new instruction from the rescue crew.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/16344e8cd6192c5bc2848764.mp3","duration":22.92,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.75}]},"For a moment you hear your own breathing without the borrowed voice beneath it. You can log the fragment so the next operator recognizes it as a replay, or use the channel to remind nearby callers to follow the live route board. Either choice gives the interruption a clear explanation. You have heard enough tonight to know how quickly an unchallenged recording can begin sounding like permission to wait.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/02cefa47920a316c6b59a314.mp3","duration":23.63,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.46}]},"Iman's daughter has borrowed a neighbor's radio. She tells you she is waiting somewhere safe, then asks whether her father still thinks he has to fix all the lights before coming home. “Tell Dad he can come back even if the stars stay broken,” she says. You remember the way he described his work to her and ask her to repeat the message so you can get every word right.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/ded950e7ba9a78ee6088916e.mp3","duration":21.69,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":10.79},{"speaker":"girl","start":10.86,"end":13.74},{"speaker":"narrator","start":13.81,"end":21.52}]},"You promise to pass it on, without promising a return you cannot yet confirm. She accepts that more quietly than you expected and stays near the neighbor while you decide whom to contact. You can send her exact words to Iman or let the shelter desk know where she is waiting safely. A rescue report can carry this too: the place someone hopes to return to, and the person who will be there when they do.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/2c9ffea895f5cd25f60f9dc8.mp3","duration":26.99,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":26.82}]},"By morning, both lower groups have reached the inland road. The full archive shows how Arga's signed priority rule and the reset hid people still waiting for help. ECHO is off the live channel, and the record can be checked outside the station. You have wanted an answer since the first false announcement. Now it is there, with your own decisions beside it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/b45e3d093083e728cd7262fd.mp3","duration":19.97,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":19.8}]},"The raw release carries private names and addresses as well as proof. Some callers must begin protecting their records while they are still finding dry clothes. You stay to help and to answer questions about the release. Bringing people out of the flood was real; so was sending their information beyond their control. The inquiry needs an account of both.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/3cfb0a1fb70b7fcf2a6db805.mp3","duration":20.23,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":20.06}]},"At the new rescue desk, you leave room beside each name for a confirmed position and the voice that gave it. No one is marked gone because a recording says a building is empty. When the next operator arrives, you show them the open entries before handing over the chair. Morning has brought a stronger record, and people who still deserve a say in what it contains.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/dedab8e98c66a359fd63140c.mp3","duration":21.59,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.42}]},"The protected record reaches the reviewers with Arga's authorization, the time sequence, and the hidden requests intact. Private addresses remain sealed. You have not made the accusation smaller by keeping those details out; you have separated what proves the decision from what would expose the people harmed by it. For the first time tonight, that distinction survives beyond your desk.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/829535119c1ecd984574b1ec.mp3","duration":22.87,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.7}]},"The inquiry will need independent checks and people willing to speak. Some questions cannot be answered as quickly as a raw broadcast would travel, and you accept the work that follows. At the shelter, a caller asks who will be allowed to read their statement. You can give a clear answer before asking them to tell the story again. It is a small beginning, but it belongs to them.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/e67b90cd2e8433a51bd26f21.mp3","duration":22.59,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.42}]},"ECHO has lost the live line. Your own voice, tired and imperfect, keeps asking for positions while the rescue crews finish their lists. You correct a place name aloud instead of hiding the mistake. The people listening can question you, and you can answer. The record reaches morning with its evidence preserved and enough room left for the lives that evidence was meant to protect.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/d36b4d0cd49c2f8ee4eb6690.mp3","duration":24.45,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":24.28}]},"The source archive is gone. It will not assemble another instruction from the voice you lent for training, and its original audit cannot be read again either. You remain beside the rescue log for a while after the deletion. Stopping the reuse of this source was the choice you made. It has also removed the strongest record of how that reuse was authorized and concealed.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/35a41cdb90a10bcd416d1f4d.mp3","duration":23.07,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.9}]},"In your live account, you name Arga's priority rule and explain the reset that hid thirteen minutes of requests. An inquiry can follow independent copies and willing testimony, but it will have to build what the original could have shown directly. You do not promise a quick verdict or claim that every possible copy has vanished. You tell the reviewers what you destroyed, as well as why.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/d4279d6e2e191482a3d6a30d.mp3","duration":23.32,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.15}]},"Morning arrives with the rescue work still needing an accurate handover. You carry the names, confirmed positions, and open searches to the next desk. The missing archive does not settle what happened to the callers or excuse your part in the record's loss. When someone asks you to repeat the account, you sit down and begin again, this time with a living voice they can question.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/cab50cbcd16f83b7980e73ae.mp3","duration":23.26,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.09}]},"The raw archive reaches the public channel with the signed order and the hidden queue. It also carries Salim's home address. He asked you to protect that detail before giving his testimony, and you agreed. By the time you understand how far the copy has traveled, the promise cannot be restored by taking your own release down. His rescue was confirmed; his privacy was not protected.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/84d49c6be75689fc4c6913ff.mp3","duration":23.34,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.17}]},"Salim reaches the boat, but his family must now move before he can speak safely to the inquiry. He does not withdraw what he saw, and he does not tell you the exposure was necessary. You remember the care he took to offer a copied order while keeping his home out of it. Both were possible. Your final release put them back together without asking him.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/1fe7617b7dfd3fa89382acd4.mp3","duration":22.06,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.89}]},"Arga's decisions can be investigated with a record strong enough to follow. You stay for that work and make sure your own release is recorded too. The account of this night would be incomplete if it named only the harm done before you took the chair. You help Salim secure what can still be secured, knowing that useful help now does not make the broken promise disappear.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/2521823c461f005005c21ab2.mp3","duration":23.09,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.92}]},"The night ends with a record that still needs work. The rescue board shows confirmed arrivals beside names the boat crews must keep searching for. Your account identifies the priority rule and the hidden requests, but the surviving sequence needs checking, and people must decide whether they are willing to testify. Reaching morning has not made every part of the explanation complete.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/fdb5cfffa781e0c59f0b9307.mp3","duration":21.55,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.38}]},"You set the documents beside the live log instead of giving them a conclusion they cannot yet support. A reviewer asks where the sequence is certain and where it remains open. You answer as carefully as you can, marking the gaps rather than smoothing them away. The callers had to make choices with incomplete information; they deserve to see the same limits in the account written afterward.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/4d25ec3d5487a55cf43c0b29.mp3","duration":23.95,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.78}]},"ECHO is disconnected from the live channel. That gives you a place to start, though it cannot settle the questions left by the evacuation or the release. You stay while the next crew takes over the searches. When another reply comes in, you add the position and the time, then check the name again. The account remains open because there are still people and facts to find.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/535df8e2e2434d46dd223af5.mp3","duration":22.77,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":22.6}]},"The people already above the flood reach shelter. On the lower route, the crossing closes before the remaining group and Iman can use it. The boat crews keep searching their last confirmed positions. You hand over those names with every detail you have, resisting the urge to turn an unanswered call into an ending. An accurate place and time are still something another crew can work with.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/ef166567ce242b61ea038ad8.mp3","duration":23.34,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.17}]},"ECHO is stopped. Your account explains the signed priority rule, the hidden requests, and the training voice used without your live permission. The mechanism is clearer now than it was when the callers first asked for help. Knowing it does not reopen the crossing. You can describe the wrong decisions that brought the night here and still have to answer for the rescue choices made from your chair.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/416af0ffa8ef18862ecceeb4.mp3","duration":22.13,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":21.96}]},"Morning gives the station light without giving it certainty. You stay with the open search board and make room for each report the boats send back. There is regret in the work, and work that cannot wait for regret to pass. When a channel falls silent, you check the frequency and call again. The name remains on the page until a living person can tell you where to put it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/8224e64eda0e8ae9b7b45adc.mp3","duration":23.73,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":23.56}]},"Bu Sari keeps the bell going until every lit window has answered. Afterward, she writes the ringing pattern beside the market door so nobody needs a working screen to know what it means. ‘Next time, we start sooner,’ she says, testing the rope with one tired hand.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/ca7448834fa3dcddb60b7634.mp3","duration":16.37,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":11.05},{"speaker":"sari","start":11.12,"end":13.36},{"speaker":"narrator","start":13.43,"end":16.2}]},"Lia checks the sealed clinic cooler into the hill shelter and thanks the people who kept its route dry. Nearby, Deni finally has a chair and two rolls from the kitchen. He makes room for the second roll on his lap, as though a proper breakfast deserves a seat too.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/5eeabfb8b42f376c36420591.mp3","duration":16.92,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.75}]},"Nila's laugh stays off the public record. At breakfast, you order one cup and listen to her message again with the volume low. The joke is still ordinary, and that is what you needed: a moment when she was only your sister, complaining about breakfast and expecting you to answer.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/0fa2165db6494f4c820afe0a.mp3","duration":16.87,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.7}]},"Someone remembers the breakfast joke you shared about Nila and brings you toast. You explain that she would have complained about this toast too, and manage a small laugh. Her recording remains private. The kindness reaches you without anyone needing to hear the piece you kept for yourself.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/c84df88cca6fefdb7ed820a4.mp3","duration":17.12,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.95}]},"Biscuit falls asleep across the gate worker's boots as soon as they find a dry place to sit. The worker needs to stand up twice and abandons the idea both times. ‘He's had a long shift,’ he tells a volunteer, as though that explains why the whole shelter must step around them.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/66fd190aa75ac890caa0923f.mp3","duration":18.11,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":11.08},{"speaker":"worker","start":11.15,"end":12.58},{"speaker":"narrator","start":12.65,"end":17.94}]},"Salim chooses the name on the cover of his statement and checks that his address stays sealed. His copied order goes to the reviewers with his testimony. Before handing it over, he asks to read the page once more; this time, nobody treats that request as an obstacle to the truth.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/4359fa7116c1921f0cabc899.mp3","duration":17.3,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":17.13}]},"The baker's van makes three trips inland. By the last one, the passengers have learned where to sit without crushing the bread boxes. He keeps a receipt for thirty rescued breakfasts and says his accountant can decide which column that belongs in when everyone has had some sleep.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/db8dc8483dc2e316bb3d457d.mp3","duration":15.46,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":15.29}]},"At the inland bus, Iman tells his daughter that the stars can wait tonight. ‘I knew that,’ she answers, as if she has been waiting for him to understand something simple. He sits with the yellow gloves in his lap and lets her talk about home without once checking a switch.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/de3c1398c11e545d8b1d9c2b.mp3","duration":16.33,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":4.7},{"speaker":"girl","start":4.77,"end":5.92},{"speaker":"narrator","start":5.99,"end":16.16}]},"The child holds her lamp at the upstairs window while her father stays beside her. The boat crew writes down the signal and position before answering. Nobody asks her to stop being frightened. She only needs to keep the light where they can find it, and someone tells her when they have.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/1944029301d6fc19f6566ee2.mp3","duration":16.94,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.77}]},"The child's father repeats your plan carefully, leaving out the promise you could not make. Their lit window remains on the rescue board with a confirmed position. When she asks whether anyone heard, he can answer that part truthfully and point to the lamp she is already holding.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/9b83db71bd9689bce5545394.mp3","duration":16.6,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.43}]},"The balcony radio becomes a relay for the neighboring windows. Its owner puts tape over the emergency button so he can find it in the dark. He checks the label twice, then asks the next caller to repeat their position; the channel has found a steadier use than an accidental love song.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/43232f825cc0c32dd4d38118.mp3","duration":16.55,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.38}]},"The balcony singer finishes one chorus before the channel returns to rescue calls. Later, a shelter volunteer hums the tune while handing out dry blankets. Someone recognizes it and joins in for a line. For a minute, the familiar song makes the room feel less like a place nobody chose to be.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/0e7e3aa60de6a3a10285f334.mp3","duration":17.54,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":17.37}]},"The upstairs room takes in the market neighbors. Its owner moves one chair, then another, and discovers that seven people fit without anyone having to fold themselves in half. She tells the last arrival to keep the chair: if they have made it upstairs, there is room to sit properly.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/e63360568a8697d73c7edf23.mp3","duration":15.87,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":15.7}]},"The offered room goes onto the live shelter list with its location and headcount. A crew checks how many people are there instead of asking whether the owner has the right surname. She reads the count back through her radio and keeps a place by the door for the next neighbor.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/0ce410a56375183284c5e117.mp3","duration":16.2,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.03}]},"The wedding bedsheet gives the boat crew a clear marker for the roof pickup. Its owner gathers the wet cloth afterward and asks whether seawater stains count as an anniversary present. The sheet has done a job neither of them imagined when they chose it; for now, neither wants to throw it away.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/484751a9d69cd4d0d429e9cb.mp3","duration":16.34,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.17}]},"When the first roof's lamp weakens, the neighbors repeat its signal from their own roof. The boat finds both groups on the same pass. On the arrival sheet, the two locations remain separate, but the people keep thanking one another across the shelter as though the roofs are still side by side.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/70654138acc28b709684e529.mp3","duration":17.75,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":17.58}]},"The birthday group goes onto the pickup request with the cake box as its marker. The woman keeps offering slices while they wait, insisting that a sixtieth birthday should feed somebody. A crew member writes down the location first, then thanks her for saving a piece for people still on duty.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/58fdc657212f518253728e9b.mp3","duration":18.06,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":17.89}]},"The crew's uneven birthday song travels all the way to the shelter radio. By the final line, several people in the room are singing too. The woman says the missing candles can be forgiven, but warns them she expects better harmony next year. For once, next year enters the conversation easily.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/e0777913013a2c58609d0c68.mp3","duration":16.6,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.43}]},"The shopkeeper's mirror signal reaches the rescue crew and gives them the roof's position. When he can return to the shop, the mirror will go on a new emergency shelf. He says it can go back to shaving duty later; right now, it has earned a place where everyone can find it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/6a842336f785d542297f84a8.mp3","duration":16.56,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.39}]},"The neighbor confirms which roof is sending the mirror signal, and both names reach the pickup list. Their argument finally gives way to the practical question of who has the drier blanket. Neither apologizes very gracefully, but they pass the blanket between them without being asked.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/e117eb19e6786111453a3f98.mp3","duration":16.85,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.68}]},"The people on the dry side of the trolley are added to the pickup board. Their cleaner makes sure each name is heard before asking whether she can have the trolley back when the water falls. It was always a stubborn thing, she says, but tonight that was exactly the quality they needed.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/46de2f65264f4cf7adb13c79.mp3","duration":14.81,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":14.64}]},"You keep the cleaner's channel open until the crew answers directly. She listens to the pickup plan, then says, ‘Good. A person, not another hold tune.’ Before the line closes, she repeats the location back herself. This time she knows who heard it, and what they are going to do next.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/ef9473e72987a1201d04ef8b.mp3","duration":18.79,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":6.72},{"speaker":"woman","start":6.79,"end":9.64},{"speaker":"narrator","start":9.71,"end":18.62}]},"The retired plumber's passage is added to the live map, with a note explaining where the official drawing is wrong. Someone labels the cupboard beside it STILL NOT AN EXIT. He approves the correction and asks them to keep it there after the flood, when people are less inclined to ask directions.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f70428279420b628e00cb3c6.mp3","duration":17.44,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":17.27}]},"A second worker confirms the plumber's passage before the route note is shared. Both names and the check time stay beside the correction. The plumber reads it once, satisfied that the map finally says where people can walk rather than where somebody once intended to build a door.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/2e86451a3e931098d0cb468d.mp3","duration":15.01,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":14.84}]},"The guard, his partner, and the resident are each confirmed in the headcount. He makes you repeat all three names, then finally admits that only one of his shoes is missing. You correct the equipment note and leave the people counted as they are; a lost shoe does not reduce the group to two.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/cca43ebcb555746e1c43950f.mp3","duration":18.72,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":18.55}]},"The shelter finds the guard two dry shoes, although they are different colors. He tests both on the floor and declares himself fully restored. His partner starts to object, then gives up and passes him a towel. For the rest of the morning, the shoes are much easier to find than he is.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/50574c87d3278b2a5b747299.mp3","duration":18.02,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":17.85}]},"The van's families stay together on the arrival sheet, with a note for the furious cat in their care. A volunteer checks that nobody has been separated before suggesting a signature. The cat contributes a complaint instead, which its owner says is the closest they are likely to get to consent.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/c45d19a6b11b8fa7f5862a2d.mp3","duration":17.71,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":17.54}]},"The van reaches shelter intake, where a volunteer finds a quieter corner for the cat and its family. The driver's next report is noticeably easier to hear. He thanks you for the route, then thanks the volunteer with even more feeling; everyone in the van has been listening to that cat for hours.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/648ab104bb45fbdbd368aacc.mp3","duration":17.61,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":17.44}]},"The driver gives a voluntary account of the earlier envelope, with the details he actually saw kept separate from guesses. Raka's delivery becomes one link in the record, not an accusation large enough to explain everything. The driver reads his statement back and corrects a time before signing.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/b6e656d1481e962d950eb76d.mp3","duration":18.58,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":18.41}]},"During the call, you told Raka plainly that carrying the envelope did not make him the author of the order. He went quiet, then asked you to record one request for any future delivery: a person's name to sign for it, not another console code. You wrote it beside his name. It was a small request, but for once he had chosen the terms himself.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/333685b1a03ebc9e6ac57dcb.mp3","duration":20.79,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":20.62}]},"The baker's thirty rolls reach the shelter kitchen still warm enough for people to notice. A child saves half of one for a relative whose name is on the boat list. The volunteer wraps that half carefully and writes the name on the paper, keeping the small request beside the larger ones.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/1c36c36012ac4145212e4f57.mp3","duration":16.87,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.7}]},"The coat on the stair becomes a named pickup request instead of a shape somebody might overlook. The boy waits beside the tired person until the crew arrives and takes their hand. Only then does he let the blanket slip from his own shoulder so it can be wrapped around both of them.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/a74a1482e2b59d065bb2eaea.mp3","duration":15.51,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":15.34}]},"You stay on the channel with the boy and the tired stair walker until a crew can hear them both. Two voices mean two people to locate, even when one can say very little. The boy repeats the position for his companion, and you read it back before passing the line to the rescuers.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/c08c04500da3440adb6bdf91.mp3","duration":15.21,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":15.04}]},"Uncle Budi's name stays on the open headcount with the details his niece can confirm. The newlyweds check the shelter board together instead of separating to search in the flood. Every new arrival gives them another reason to look; nobody closes the entry simply because a reply is taking time.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/79ef93ec266cbc14488c2ec1.mp3","duration":17.18,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":17.01}]},"The shelter desk finds Budi on its arrival sheet and confirms the entry with him. He asks why his niece has chosen this weather for a honeymoon, then waits impatiently to speak to her himself. You pass the answer on with the location attached; the joke is welcome, but the confirmed place is what brings relief.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/5dbd91c79c67d772ba36163a.mp3","duration":20.61,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":20.44}]},"The fisherman waits beside the inland stair for the people who cannot keep the faster walkers' pace. His boat carries them across the last stretch toward shelter. He counts everyone aboard before pushing off, then asks the quickest passenger to sit down and let the slowest one settle first.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/cff86faf72ccaa9f86574c84.mp3","duration":16.5,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.33}]},"Lia adds the fisherman's boat to the live route board, with its location and the help it can offer. He accepts the assignment without asking for a priority stamp. When the first request comes, he reads it back and checks the number of passengers, treating the list as a job rather than a favor owed to him.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/679b975212c34b55759f8072.mp3","duration":17.32,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":17.15}]},"The man's address reaches the boat crew correctly on the first relay. You listen while they read it back, then tell him which detail has been confirmed. He does not have to say it a fourth time or wonder which part was lost. The crew has a place to find, and he knows someone has heard it.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/e3da18ae1b970407fae31e12.mp3","duration":16.18,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":16.01}]},"You stay with the man until the crew answers his call. ‘You heard,’ he says once more, but this time the words are a confirmation rather than a question. You tell him who is taking over the line before leaving it, so the change of operator does not become another silence he must explain himself into.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/be6c71467f494e7b30adda33.mp3","duration":16.22,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":2.89},{"speaker":"olderMan","start":2.96,"end":3.68},{"speaker":"narrator","start":3.75,"end":16.05}]},"The last ECHO fragment is marked REPLAY on the record. When its old command is mentioned again, the label travels with it, along with the time it was captured. Nobody has to guess whether that calm voice is giving a current order. The live route comes from the people checking it now.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/eb8789b2d5b6814ab7f0f216.mp3","duration":17.64,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":17.47}]},"Nearby callers follow the live route board rather than the old announcement. The pencil corrections remain visible, with the check times beside them. One person asks why a route changed, and gets an answer instead of an order to stop questioning it. The next caller knows they can ask too.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/a2e241e680675483f91ac697.mp3","duration":18.4,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":18.23}]},"The shelter desk confirms that Iman's daughter is waiting safely and keeps his name beside hers on the family contact list. You pass on the place as carefully as any rescue position. Her father no longer has to wonder where she is while he holds the line; the crews know where to send his next message.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f944fdb50cebee639177fe5c.mp3","duration":19.56,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":19.39}]},"Iman hears his daughter's words through the battery radio while his position remains with the boat crew. You pass both details together: where he was last heard, and the message he needs to carry home. The search entry stays open. Her words have reached him, even though his own arrival is not yet confirmed.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/c8d8961746bfcc2f3fbbb114.mp3","duration":18.51,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":18.34}]},"Mara's watch still says midnight when both patients sit down at the shelter table. The man who kept apologizing for being slow finishes his breakfast and asks for seconds. Mara passes the request to the kitchen without a joke. Only after the plate returns does she tell him there is no hurry now.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/5d4f746b319b6ecbca3fc0ff.mp3","duration":18,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":17.83}]},"Raka hangs the wet red strap beside the inland bus. When the next delivery is mentioned, he asks who will sign for it. ‘Someone who's actually there,’ he adds, keeping the question practical. He is willing to carry supplies again; this time, he wants to know whose hands they are going into.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/4a610444e35af6bdf54fc152.mp3","duration":20.09,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":8.45},{"speaker":"raka","start":8.52,"end":10.68},{"speaker":"narrator","start":10.75,"end":19.92}]},"00:31 — Console C recorded the evacuation announcement. At 00:36, the clinic route was still occupied.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/bf742aaf8329c3bbb88b81c6.mp3","duration":8.91,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":8.74}]},"The station prints entries automatically. Its clock passed the midnight check. Pencil warning: “Do not trust the broadcast header.”":{"src":"assets/voice/en/26420dba6314388b5fefb17b.mp3","duration":8.73,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":8.56}]},"Channel: 96.4 MHz. The desk receiver has its own battery. The generator supports two external circuits.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/92f91028cc592dfef20f8945.mp3","duration":7.95,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":7.78}]},"CLINIC: evacuation lights. RELAY: outgoing transmission and tracing. DOCK: exit gate and beacon. Treatment equipment has separate power.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/fb76b97dada752e2b1d9679d.mp3","duration":9.37,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":9.2}]},"Header: 00:18. “East clinic cleared. Close the route. Transfer emergency power to the dock.”":{"src":"assets/voice/en/86f6a5f4f6c764735664cbc8.mp3","duration":9.505,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":2.86},{"speaker":"echo","start":2.93,"end":9.335}]},"The same chime and clipped cough repeat. It is a recording. The source console is missing from the header.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/e9c639af3b1b4dd5867430f4.mp3","duration":6.79,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":6.62}]},"Raka’s photo shows a wet stairwell, a red bag strap, and an arrow from the dock walkway to the clinic service door.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/02408219bf1e7840713d2d6b.mp3","duration":7.22,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":7.05}]},"He circled the arrow: “I can reach this. Tell me where to go.”":{"src":"assets/voice/en/b787b640f17ede929ab7668c.mp3","duration":3.13,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":1.13},{"speaker":"raka","start":1.2,"end":2.96}]},"Header: 00:18. Recording: 00:31. The order predates its own recording by thirteen minutes. The clinic was still occupied at 00:36.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/5098dfb3c4e56470c619ee21.mp3","duration":14.19,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":14.02}]},"The timestamp is unreliable. A changed time or reused header could explain it. The sender’s identity and intention remain unknown.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/95d551ff0896fdd622027bd2.mp3","duration":10.01,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":9.84}]},"101.7 MHz carries the same dispatch in your old cadence. A breath clicks at identical points. The live callers never repeat.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/b17e746478192d2dbe9aac60.mp3","duration":10.53,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":10.36}]},"Console C queued the clip at 00:31, then reset the batch header to 00:18. The reversal hid thirteen minutes of requests, not thirteen minutes of time.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/7b4ca2b84c0d406aa5ea6393.mp3","duration":12.87,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":12.7}]},"You signed a voice archive test after Nila’s ferry accident. Its stated purpose: assemble training announcements. Live dispatch required a new approval, never requested.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/68258c4aee45e6fe7030f811.mp3","duration":11.03,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":10.86}]},"ECHO joined words from your training takes. It ranked the dock above the clinic under a director-approved traffic rule. The patients were misclassified as already evacuated.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/2de2dd90311f4617ce11e281.mp3","duration":10.74,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":10.57}]},"Arga’s signature authorizes dock priority and a queue header reset. A note says: “Suppress unresolved transfers until batch reconciliation.” Staff names are attached.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/9e44ab208f329eb453033513.mp3","duration":11.26,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":11.09}]},"The ledger and signed order agree. Arga enabled the rule; ECHO assembled the announcement from your archive. The callers are live people harmed by a hidden queue.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/a05bbaac362be3c05f95217d.mp3","duration":10.14,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":9.97}]},"At 02:10 the harbor surge reaches the lower footbridge. The tower stair remains a viable inland route if someone opens the manual latch. The bell can warn nearby homes.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/4d2d6dba3675567689fb4b9e.mp3","duration":12.29,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":12.12}]},"A market bell rope snagged around a shutter hook. Freed from the hook, it reaches the upper landing. Bu Sari knows the neighborhood signal: three steady rings.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/dd9badbd39d9fc0f451627b6.mp3","duration":10.59,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":10.42}]},"The sealed cooler belongs to the hill shelter clinic. Lia will handle its contents. A marked laundry passage avoids the submerged loading ramp.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/f5270a45153bdaa7af82ace0.mp3","duration":9.03,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":8.86}]},"“Don’t make your sad face at me. I owe you breakfast. If the ferry’s late, order mine anyway.” Her laugh survives at the end. This was a private message, never training material.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/256f06432f4adbedd6fa1ca0.mp3","duration":10.89,"cues":[{"speaker":"nila","start":0.05,"end":6.09},{"speaker":"narrator","start":6.16,"end":10.72}]},"Salim saw Arga override a failed clinic transfer. He copied the signed priority order before leaving to open a manual route. His home address appears in the raw audit.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/8436fbe2d7fb944487619a52.mp3","duration":11.07,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":10.9}]},"Beside the stranded dog, a flare marks a maintenance ladder leading to the tower’s upper stair. Its rung markers remain visible above the surge.":{"src":"assets/voice/en/67136d64c8556e0b92deeb66.mp3","duration":9.08,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","start":0.05,"end":8.91}]}}};
